 8\\end{bmatrix}, b = \\begin{bmatrix}45 \\\\ -20 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 19\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533418 17592186045585 false] [17592186120317 1071 "Find $l$, if $5{a}_{1}+l{a}_{2}=b$ and\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}45 \\\\ -20 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 19\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186120317 1074 "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $l$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{a}_{1}+l{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ 5[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+l[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}45 \\\\ -20 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 19\\end{aligned}] \\\\ l[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}45 \\\\ -20 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 19\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\ l[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the final equation above, we can tell that $l=-2.$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186120327 1068 #edb/ref 17592186118071 13194139533418 17592186045585 false] [17592186120327 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316911 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186120327 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186120327 1071 "The set ${a_{1}, a_{2}}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be $a_{2}$ if\n$a_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$?" 13194139533418 17592186045585 false] [17592186120327 1071 "The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186120327 1074 "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -20 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -20 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]=5[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=5{a}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{a}_{1}-5{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ 5{a}_{1}-{a}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 5{a}_{1}+(-1){a}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=5$ and ${x}_{2}=-1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186120333 1068 #edb/ref 17592186118107 13194139533418 17592186045585 false] [17592186120333 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317005 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186120333 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186120333 1071 "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $12V$, a resistance of $40Ω$, a capacitance of $0.005F$, and an inductance of $L = 20H$. Find the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds).\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_{C}$, where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139533418 17592186045585 false] [17592186120333 1071 "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $12\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $40\\,Ω,$ a capacitance of $0.005\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductance of $L=20\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds).\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186120333 1074 "We have the following circuit diagram.\n\n![](images/16/169844e6d45393119ac1579cc89e34850401810bfcc1c7537d5b72851a0b2b02.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=40\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=40I=40\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.005}=200q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=20\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=20\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=20\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=20\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n12=40\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+200q+20\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186120336 1068 #edb/ref 17592186118125 13194139533418 17592186045585 false] [17592186120336 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316680 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186120336 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186120336 1071 "![](images/c8/c8600d487acea6755d032a17a04c1eb584bc1a84888af5ac152001a4a03aa8b1.png)\n\nFind, in coulombs, the amplitude of the steady state charge for the RCL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_{C}$, where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139533418 17592186045585 false] [17592186120336 1071 "![](images/c8/c8600d487acea6755d032a17a04c1eb584bc1a84888af5ac152001a4a03aa8b1.png)\n\nFind, in coulombs, the amplitude of the steady state charge for the RCL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186120336 1074 "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $(39\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-65\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $70\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.01\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $10\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=70\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=70I=70\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.01}=100q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=10\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=10\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=10\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=10\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n39\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-65\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=70\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+100q+10\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+7\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=\\frac{39}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{13}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+7\\cdot \\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+10{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}+B{e}^{-2t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))+7(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\, &  \\\\ +10(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{39}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{13}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-21\\alpha +\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+(\\alpha +21\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =\\frac{39}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{13}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-21\\alpha +\\beta =\\frac{39}{10}\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ \\alpha +21\\beta =-\\frac{13}{2}\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{5},$ $\\beta =-\\frac{3}{10}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}+B{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}+B{e}^{-2t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{3}{10})}^{2}}≈0.36,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186120339 1068 #edb/ref 17592186118133 13194139533418 17592186045585 false] [17592186120339 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316630 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186120339 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186120339 1071 "Consider the matrix $A = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -12 & 4 \\\\ 9 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and the vector $b = \\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}$. Find the value of $k$ such that $b ∈ Null(A)$." 13194139533418 17592186045585 false] [17592186120339 1071 "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -12 & 4 \\\\ 9 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ and the vector $b=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}].$ Find the value of $k$ such that $b\\in \\text{Null}(A).$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186120339 1074 "If $b\\in \\text{Null}(A),$ then we must have $Ab=0.$ Substituting the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ab & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -12 & 4 \\\\ 9 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3k+6 \\\\ -12k-24 \\\\ 9k+18\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3k+6=0 \\\\ -12k-24=0 \\\\ 9k+18=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for each of these equations, we obtain $k=-2.$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186120341 1068 #edb/ref 17592186118145 13194139533418 17592186045585 false] [17592186120341 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316632 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186120341 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186120341 1071 "![](images/00/00602c631bee877e48211dd94a6ea6e1bbb26626f028212c1388ed547f2452f8.png)\n\nFind, in amperes, the amplitude of the steady state current for the RCL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_{C}$, where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139533418 17592186045585 false] [17592186120341 1071 "![](images/00/00602c631bee877e48211dd94a6ea6e1bbb26626f028212c1388ed547f2452f8.png)\n\nFind, in amperes, the amplitude of the steady state current for the RCL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186120341 1074 "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $50\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $105\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $\\frac{1}{180}\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $15\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=105\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=105I=105\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{(1/180)}=180q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=15\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=15\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=15\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=15\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n50\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=105\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+180q+15\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+7\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+12q=\\frac{10}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+7\\cdot \\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+12{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-3t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))+7(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\,\\, &  \\\\ +12(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{10}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-21\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+(3\\alpha +21\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =\\frac{10}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-21\\alpha +3\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ 3\\alpha +21\\beta =\\frac{10}{3}\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{45},$ $\\beta =\\frac{7}{45}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{45}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{7}{45}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{1}{45}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{7}{45}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{1}{45}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{7}{45}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\  & =-4A{e}^{-4t}-3B{e}^{-3t}-\\frac{1}{15}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\frac{7}{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=-4A{e}^{-4t}-3B{e}^{-3t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{1}{15}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\frac{7}{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{1}{15}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\frac{7}{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{15})}^{2}+{(\\frac{7}{15})}^{2}}≈0.47,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186120347 1068 #edb/ref 17592186118176 13194139533418 17592186045585 false] [17592186120347 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317006 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186120347 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186120347 1071 "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $9V$, a resistance of $34Ω$, a capacitance of $\\frac{1}{24}F$, and an inductance of $L = 12H$. Find the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_{C}$, where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}}\ncoulombs." 13194139533418 17592186045585 false] [17592186120347 1071 "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $9\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $34\\,Ω,$ a capacitance of $\\frac{1}{24}\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductance of $L=12\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}}\ncoulombs." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186120347 1074 "The diagram for our circuit is shown below.\n\n![](images/d8/d8303b679d4e00bac0f59f51941a2842473e050033f87f56f099b853431ac5aa.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops. In an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=34\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=34I=34\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{(1/24)}=24q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=12\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=12\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=12\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=12\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n12\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+34\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+24q=9.\n$$\n\nThe *steady-state charge* is the value that the charge $q(t)$ approaches as $t\\to \\infty .$ In a stable RCL circuit with a constant electromotive force, the transient response eventually decays to zero, leaving a constant charge.\n\nWhen the system reaches steady state, the charge $q$ is constant. Consequently, the first and second derivatives of the charge with respect to time are zero:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these steady-state conditions into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n12(0)+34(0)+24q=9,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n24q=9\\,⟹\\,q=\\frac{9}{24}=\\frac{3}{8}.\n$$\n\nPhysically, this represents the state where the capacitor is fully charged and the current has ceased to flow.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $\\frac{3}{8}$ coulombs." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186120348 1068 #edb/ref 17592186118177 13194139533418 17592186045585 false] [17592186120348 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316964 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186120348 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186120348 1071 "![](images/b8/b885a0dc4bb868284ec96bcbfc964c767fc159aa0728044d722f0f6b11cbfb1f.png)\n\nFind the steady-state charge for the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_{C}$, where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}}\ncoulombs." 13194139533418 17592186045585 false] [17592186120348 1071 "![](images/b8/b885a0dc4bb868284ec96bcbfc964c767fc159aa0728044d722f0f6b11cbfb1f.png)\n\nFind the steady-state charge for the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}}\ncoulombs." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186120348 1074 "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $8\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $14\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.1\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $4\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops. In an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=14\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=14I=14\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.1}=10q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+14\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=8.\n$$\n\nThe **steady-state charge** is the value that the charge $q(t)$ approaches as $t\\to \\infty .$ In a stable RCL circuit with a constant electromotive force, the transient response (the part of the solution that depends on the initial conditions) eventually decays to zero, leaving a constant charge.\n\nWhen the system reaches steady state, the charge $q$ is constant. Consequently, the first and second derivatives of the charge with respect to time are zero:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these steady-state conditions into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n4(0)+14(0)+10q=8,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n10q=8\\,⟹\\,q=\\frac{8}{10}=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nPhysically, this represents the state where the capacitor is fully charged, the inductor acts as a short circuit, and the current has ceased to flow.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $\\frac{4}{5}$ coulombs." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186124677 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317473 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186124677 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317476 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186124680 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317478 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186124680 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317479 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186124680 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317480 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186124680 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317483 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186124680 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317484 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186124682 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317235 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186124682 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317236 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186124682 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317237 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186124682 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317238 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186124682 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317239 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186124683 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317240 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186124683 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317241 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186124683 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317242 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186124683 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317243 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186124683 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317244 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186124683 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317252 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186124683 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317253 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186124683 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317276 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186124684 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317264 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186124684 1038 #edb/r