}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{-}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $6$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is continuous at $x=2.$ \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\nf(2)=-{2}^{2}=-4.\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186167300 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164986 13194139533418 17592186045585 false] [17592186167300 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316579 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186167300 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316831 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186167300 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316850 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186167300 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316914 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186167300 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186167300 1071 "$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}0, & x\\in [-\\pi ,0] \\\\ 1, & x\\in (0,\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. The Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{1}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\pi (2n-1)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡((2n-1)x).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.At $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡((2n-1)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-2}}\nat $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1}={{field-4}}.\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186167300 1071 "$F(x) = {0,\\begin{vmatrix}x ∈ [- π, 0] \\\\ 1,\\end{vmatrix}x ∈ (0, π)$\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. The Fourier series of the $2π$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n$f(x)∼\\frac{1}{2} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(2)/(π(2n - 1))\\sin ((2n - 1)x)$.\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.At $x = \\frac{π}{2}$, we have\n$(-1)^{n + 1}$ {{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 false] [17592186167300 1074 "First, we rewrite our Fourier series as\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡((2n-1)x).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡((2n-1)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi )=(-1)^{n+1}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is $\\text{continuous}$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡((2n-1)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot (-1)^{n+1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1} & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186167302 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164996 13194139533418 17592186045585 false] [17592186167302 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316993 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186167302 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186167302 1071 "$$\n{y}^{‴}-5{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+8y=6x{e}^{2x},\\,{{y}_{1}={e}^{-x},\\,{y}_{2}={e}^{2x},\\,{y}_{3}={e}^{4x}}\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation and the set of fundamental solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation above. \nUsing the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){e}^{-x}+{u}_{2}(x){e}^{2x}+{u}_{3}(x){e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the function ${u}_{2}(x)?$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186167302 1071 "$y^{‴} - 5y^{″} + 2y^{′} + 8y = 6xe^{2x}, {y_{1} = e^{-x}, y_{2} = e^{2x}, y_{3} = e^{4x}}$\nConsider the differential equation and the set of fundamental solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation above.\nUsing the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution of the equation can be written in the form\n$y_{p}(x) = u_{1}(x)e^{-x} + u_{2}(x)e^{2x} + u_{3}(x)e^{4x}$.\nWhat is the function $u_{2}(x)$?" 13194139533418 17592186045585 false] [17592186167302 1074 "According to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}+{u}_{3}{y}_{3},\\,{u}_{i}=(-1)^{3-i}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{i}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,i=1,2,3,\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is the forcing term, $W(x)$ is the Wronskian of ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{2}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{3}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the forcing term is $g(x)=6x{e}^{2x}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}={e}^{-x},\\,{y}_{2}={e}^{2x},\\,{y}_{3}={e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(x) & =W({y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x} & {e}^{2x} & {e}^{4x} \\\\ -{e}^{-x} & 2{e}^{2x} & 4{e}^{4x} \\\\ {e}^{-x} & 4{e}^{2x} & 16{e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =({e}^{-x}\\cdot {e}^{2x}\\cdot {e}^{4x})|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 16\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{5x}[1(32-16)-1(-16-4)+1(-4-2)] \\\\  & ={e}^{5x}(16-(-20)+(-6)) \\\\  & =30{e}^{5x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, to find ${u}_{2},$ we compute the determinant\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{2}(x) & ={W}_{2}({y}_{1},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x} & {e}^{4x} \\\\ -{e}^{-x} & 4{e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4{e}^{3x}-(-{e}^{3x}) \\\\  & =5{e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2}(x) & =(-1)^{3-2}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{2}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{(6x{e}^{2x})(5{e}^{3x})}{30{e}^{5x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186167304 1068 #edb/ref 17592186165008 13194139533418 17592186045585 false] [17592186167304 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316932 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186167304 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186167304 1071 "$$\n{y}^{‴}+{y}^{′}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,\\,{{y}_{1}=1,\\,{y}_{2}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\,{y}_{3}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation and the set of fundamental solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation above. \nUsing the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n$$\ny(x)=A+B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+{u}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+{u}_{3}(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nWhat is the function ${u}_{1}(x)?$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186167304 1071 "$y^{‴} + y^{′} = \\sec x\\tan x, {y_{1} = 1, y_{2} = \\cos (x), y_{3} = \\sin (x)}$\nConsider the differential equation and the set of fundamental solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation above.\nUsing the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n$y(x) = A + B\\cos (x) + C\\sin (x) + u_{1}(x) + u_{2}(x)\\cos (x) + u_{3}(x)\\sin (x)$.\nWhat is the function $u_{1}(x)$?" 13194139533418 17592186045585 false] [17592186167304 1074 "According to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}+{u}_{3}{y}_{3},\\,{u}_{i}=(-1)^{3-i}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{i}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,i=1,2,3,\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is the forcing term, $W(x)$ is the Wronskian of ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{2}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{3}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the forcing term is $g(x)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}=1,\\,{y}_{2}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\,{y}_{3}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(x) & =W({y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡(x)+{cos}^{2}⁡(x) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, to find ${u}_{1},$ we compute the determinant ${W}_{1}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡(x)+{sin}^{2}⁡(x) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =(-1)^{3-1}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{1}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\cdot 1}{1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186184156 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317493 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186184156 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317494 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186184156 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317495 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186184156 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317496 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186184156 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317499 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186184158 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317366 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186184158 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317367 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186184158 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317547 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186184158 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317548 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186184158 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317549 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186184158 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317550 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186184158 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317566 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186184197 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317362 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186184245 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317373 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186184245 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317376 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [