{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}}+L{y} & =L{{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot {e}^{t-2}} \\\\ sY(s)+Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s-1} \\\\ (s+1)Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s-1} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{(s-1)(s+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186229745 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228660 13194139533418 17592186045585 false] [17592186229745 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316600 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186229745 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186229745 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+y={u}_{2}(t)\\cdot (t-2)\\,,\\,y(0)=1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{2}(t)=u(t-2)$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L{y(t)}$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{1}{s+1}+\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{{s}^{2}(s+1)}\n$$\n\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t)={e}^{-t}+u(t-2)\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(t).$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186229745 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} + y = u_{2}(t) \\cdot (t - 2), y(0) = 1$,\nwhere $u_{2}(t) = u(t - 2)$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s) = L{y(t)}$ is\n$Y(s) = \\frac{1}{s + 1} + (e^{-2s})/(s^{2}(s + 1))$\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as\n$y(t) = e^{-t} + u(t - 2) \\cdot g(t)$.\nFind the function $g(t)$." 13194139533418 17592186045585 false] [17592186229745 1074 "To find the function $g(t),$ we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the term containing the time delay in $Y(s).$\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential ${e}^{-2s}$ (which corresponds to the step function in the time domain) and identify the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}(s+1)}\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains a linear factor $(s+1)$ and a repeated linear factor ${s}^{2},$ the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}(s+1)}=\\frac{A}{s}+\\frac{B}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{C}{s+1}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n1=As(s+1)+B(s+1)+C{s}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A,B,$ and $C.$\n\n- Setting $s=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(1)+C(0)\\,⟹\\,B=1.\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(0)+C(1)\\,⟹\\,C=1.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n0=A+C\\,⟹\\,A=-C=-1.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=-\\frac{1}{s}+\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{s+1}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}{F(s)}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & ={L}^{-1}{-\\frac{1}{s}+\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{s+1}} \\\\  & =-1+t+{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-2s}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}{{e}^{-as}F(s)}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=2.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-2)f(t-2) & =u(t-2)(-1+(t-2)+{e}^{-(t-2)}) \\\\  & =u(t-2)(t-3+{e}^{-(t-2)})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement $y(t)={e}^{-t}+u(t-2)\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)={e}^{-(t-2)}+t-3.\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186231433 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317424 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186231434 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317425 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186231434 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317426 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186231434 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317428 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186231434 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317429 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186231435 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317433 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186231435 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317435 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186231435 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317436 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186231435 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317437 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186231435 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317438 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186231435 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317439 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186231573 1068 #edb/ref 17592186230290 13194139533418 17592186045585 false] [17592186231573 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316751 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186231573 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186231573 1071 "A particle of mass $0.4\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force $F(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\,\\text{N}$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186231573 1071 "A particle of mass $0.4kg$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force $F(t) = 2\\cos (5t)N$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t > 0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k$?\n\n$k =$\n{{field-1}} $kg/s^{2}$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 false] [17592186231573 1074 "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.4{y}^{″}=-ky+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{5k}{2}y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{5k}{2}\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{2}{5}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t),$ which is $5,$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{2}{5}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {5}^{2}=10\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186233950 1068 #edb/ref 17592186232910 13194139533418 17592186045585 false] [17592186233950 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316957 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186233950 1074 "We know that the surface area of a cube with side length $s$ is given by \n\n$$\nA=A(s)=6{s}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTaking the derivative, the rate of change of $A(s)$ with respect to $s$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{'}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(6{s}^{2}) \\\\  & =6(2s) \\\\  & =12s,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand evaluating at $s=3$ gives\n\n$$\n{A}^{'}(3)=12(3)=36.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate at which the surface area of the cube is changing when its side length is $3\\,\\text{in}$, is $36$ ${\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{in}.$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 false] [17592186233950 1074 "We know that the surface area of a cube with side length $s$ is given by \n\n$$\nA=A(s)=6{s}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTaking the derivative, the rate of change of $A(s)$ with respect to $s$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(6{s}^{2}) \\\\  & =6(2s) \\\\  & =12s,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand evaluating at $s=3$ gives\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(3)=12(3)=36.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate at which the surface area of the cube is changing when its side length is $3\\,\\text{in}$, is $36$ ${\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{in}.$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186233964 1068 #edb/ref 17592186232935 13194139533418 17592186045585 false] [17592186233964 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317067 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186233964 1071 "The area under the curve $y=\\operatorname{sin}(4x+8)$ between $x=-2$ and $x=3$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a left Riemann sum using $n$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the left Riemann sum ${S}_{L}$ in sigma notation?\n\n*Hint: Write down the expression in terms of $j$ and $n.$*\n\n${S}_{L}={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\frac{5}{n}\\,\\operatorname{sin}($\n{{field-1}}$)$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 false] [17592186233964 1071 "The area under the curve $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x+8)$ between $x=-2$ and $x=3$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a left Riemann sum using $n$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the left Riemann sum ${S}_{L}$ in sigma notation?\n\n*Hint: Write down the expression in terms of $j$ and $n.$*\n\n${S}_{L}={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\frac{5}{n}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡($\n\n{{field-1}}$)$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186233964 1074 "The left Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n{S}_{L}={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of subintervals, ${x}_{j}=a+jΔx$, and  $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nThe endpoints indicate that $a=-2$ and $b=3,$ so the step size is\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-(-2)}{n}=\\frac{5}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the left Riemann sum ${S}_{L}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{L}={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})Δx & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f(a+jΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f(-2+j\\cdot \\frac{5}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\,\\frac{5}{n}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}(4(-2+\\frac{5j}{n})+8) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\,\\frac{5}{n}\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{20j}{n}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 false] [17592186233964 1074 "The left Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n{S}_{L}={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of subintervals, ${x}_{j}=a+jΔx$, and  $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nThe endpoints indicate that $a=-2$ and $b=3,$ so the step size is\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-(-2)}{n}=\\frac{5}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the left Riemann sum ${S}_{L}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{L}={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})Δx & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f(a+jΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f(-2+j\\cdot \\frac{5}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\,\\frac{5}{n}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(-2+\\frac{5j}{n})+8) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}\\,\\frac{5}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{20j}{n}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186315117 1081 "II only" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186315117 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186315117 1084 #uuid "000aed7e-35ac-576a-a3d7-c9129bf6c269" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186315118 1081 "0.13" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186315118 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186315118 1084 #uuid "001d17a1-87cd-5834-a656-b3f42b9b78f7" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186315119 1081 "Y(s)=\\frac{{s}^{2}+10s+12}{3s(s+1)(s-2)}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186315119 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186315119 1084 #uuid "002a8adc-95e9-5fd0-9071-7bcfbfd23cbb" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186315120 1081 "1" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186315120 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533418