163 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316892 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317163 1069 "q-341634" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317163 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317163 1071 "If $x=4{y}^{4}-3y,$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at the point $(1,1).$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(1,1)}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317163 1072 #uuid "7b5bef75-1150-511d-bcb8-1729f4fd0d44" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317163 1074 "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(4{y}^{4}-3y) \\\\  & =4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{4})-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y) \\\\  & =4(4{y}^{3})-3 \\\\  & =16{y}^{3}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{16{y}^{3}-3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the point $(1,1),$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(1,1)} & =\\frac{1}{16({1}^{3})-3}=\\frac{1}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317164 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316648 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317164 1069 "q-341635" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317164 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317164 1071 "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [-5,\\infty ).$\n\n$$\nf(x)=⌈x⌉,\\,g(x)=u(x+1)+u(x+4),\\,h(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\n$$\n\nNote that $u(x)$ is the unit step function, and $⌈x⌉$ is the ceiling function." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317164 1072 #uuid "2a15a82b-151b-53d9-80e9-8a1374aa659d" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317164 1074 "Suppose $I⊆R.$ A function $f:I\\to R$ is piecewise continuous on $I$ if it is piecewise continuous on every closed and bounded subinterval $[a,b]⊂I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-5,\\infty ).$ The function has jump discontinuities at $x=…,-2,-1,0,1,2,…,$ and any closed interval $[a,b]⊂[-5,\\infty )$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](images/78/788b349598dcabebf92609183b3c48851d75bb11e22efff881a208e58be510b9.png)\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-5,\\infty )$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [-5,\\infty )$ except at the points $x=-4$ and $x=-1,$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{4}^{-}}g(x)=0,\\,{lim}_{x\\to -{4}^{+}}g(x)=1\n$$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}g(x)=1,\\,{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}g(x)=2\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-5,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,\\pi ].$ Notice that the one-sided limits at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are infinite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\pi /{2}^{-}}h(x)=+\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\pi /{2}^{+}}h(x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f$ and $g$ only.\"" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317165 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316712 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317165 1069 "q-341636" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317165 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317165 1071 "If $x=2{y}^{3}+{y}^{2},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at the point $(20,2).$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(20,2)}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317165 1072 #uuid "138e10f7-a58f-5db5-81ae-1e29e1a9812e" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317165 1074 "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2{y}^{3}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2(3{y}^{2})+2y \\\\  & =6{y}^{2}+2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{6{y}^{2}+2y}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the point $(20,2),$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(20,2)} & =\\frac{1}{6({2}^{2})+2(2)}=\\frac{1}{28}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317166 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316664 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317166 1069 "q-341637" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317166 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317166 1071 "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,\\,g(x)=⌊\\frac{x}{3}⌋,\\,h(x)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & ⌈x⌉\\text{ is even} \\\\ -1,\\, & ⌈x⌉\\text{ is odd}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $u(x)$ is the unit step function, and $⌈x⌉$ is the ceiling function." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317166 1072 #uuid "a18f041d-6273-53e5-b6c8-af0a315b73bf" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317166 1074 "Suppose $I⊆R.$ A function $f:I\\to R$ is piecewise continuous on $I$ if it is piecewise continuous on every closed and bounded subinterval $[a,b]⊂I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ Notice that the one-sided limits at $x=\\pi $ are infinite: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}f(x)=+\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ The function has jump discontinuities at $x=…,-6,-3,0,3,6,…,$ and any closed interval $[a,b]⊂[0,\\infty )$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](images/37/370ce6bc297340f28b1e5bcd4a7e0ca3ce5de6edac660e143ac70ba615184765.png)\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ The function has jump discontinuities at $x=0,1,2,…,$ and any closed interval $[a,b]⊂[0,\\infty )$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](images/9b/9b7758701595db784d2d9aeb59cc823316501748976ccbed41980fa29ad0a892.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$g$ and $h$ only.\"" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317167 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316889 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317167 1069 "q-341641" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317167 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317167 1071 "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [-6,\\infty ).$\n\n$$\nf(x)=u(x-1)+u\\,(x-\\frac{1}{2})+u\\,(x-\\frac{1}{3})+⋯,\\,g(x)=u(x+4)+u(x-6),\\,h(x)={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\, & ⌈x⌉\\text{ is even} \\\\ 1,\\, & ⌈x⌉\\text{ is odd}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $u(x)$ is the unit step function, and $⌈x⌉$ is the ceiling function." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317167 1072 #uuid "4d44a24d-4785-56ce-9a93-d0fdc876c058" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317167 1074 "Suppose $I⊆R.$ A function $f:I\\to R$ is piecewise continuous on $I$ if it is piecewise continuous on every closed and bounded subinterval $[a,b]⊂I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-6,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,1].$ Notice that the function has jump discontinuities at $x=1,\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{3},…,$ so it has infinitely many discontinuities on this closed interval.\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-6,\\infty )$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [-6,\\infty )$ except at the points $x=-4$ and $x=6,$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{4}^{-}}g(x)=0,\\,{lim}_{x\\to -{4}^{+}}g(x)=1\n$$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {6}^{-}}g(x)=1,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {6}^{+}}g(x)=2\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-6,\\infty ).$ The function has jump discontinuities at $x=0,1,2,…,$ and any closed interval $[a,b]⊂[-6,\\infty )$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](images/9e/9e447c0672628ce8b304726f36885f18661bbba790ae34ca806aaae95a465bbb.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$g$ and $h$ only.\"" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317168 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316910 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317168 1069 "q-341645" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317168 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317168 1071 "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & ⌈x⌉\\text{ is even} \\\\ -1,\\, & ⌈x⌉\\text{ is odd}\\end{aligned},\\,g(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,\\,h(x)=u\\,(x-\\frac{1}{2})+u\\,(x-\\frac{1}{4})+u\\,(x-\\frac{1}{8})+⋯\n$$\n\nNote that $u(x)$ is the unit step function, and $⌈x⌉$ is the ceiling function." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317168 1072 #uuid "22b75ee8-7527-5091-aa36-59d8ffc333e4" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317168 1074 "Suppose $I⊆R.$ A function $f:I\\to R$ is piecewise continuous on $I$ if it is piecewise continuous on every closed and bounded subinterval $[a,b]⊂I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ The function has jump discontinuities at $x=0,1,2,…,$ and any closed interval $[a,b]⊂[0,\\infty )$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](images/c9/c93cb4cc987ec951236c40a6e961a9eb9f180a594128d4b871692a140faa31e6.png)\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ Notice that the one-sided limits at $x=\\pi $ are infinite: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}g(x)=+\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}g(x)=-\\infty \n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,\\frac{1}{2}].$ Notice that the function has jump discontinuities at $x=\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{8},…,$ so it has infinitely many discontinuities on this closed interval.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f$ only.\"" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317169 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316951 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317169 1069 "q-341646" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317169 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317169 1071 "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n$$\nf(x)=u\\,(x-\\frac{1}{2})+u\\,(x-\\frac{1}{4})+u\\,(x-\\frac{1}{8})+⋯,\\,g(x)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,\\,h(x)=u(x-2)+u(x-5)\n$$\n\nNote that $u(x)$ is the unit step function." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317169 1072 #uuid "9ee18f19-a1e6-5a78-a0e0-7894f5c741d4" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317169 1074 "Suppose $I⊆R.$ A function $f:I\\to R$ is piecewise continuous on $I$ if it is piecewise continuous on every closed and bounded subinterval $[a,b]⊂I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,\\frac{1}{2}].$ Notice that the function has jump discontinuities at $x=\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{8},\\frac{1}{16},…,$ so it has infinitely many discontinuities on this closed interval.\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,\\pi ].$ Notice that the one-sided limits at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are infinite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\pi /{2}^{-}}g(x)=+\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\pi /{2}^{+}}g(x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ except at the points $x=2$ and $x=5,$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}h(x)=0,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}h(x)=1\n$$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}h(x)=1,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{+}}h(x)=2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$h$ only.\"" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317170 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316985 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317170 1069 "q-341651" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317170 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317170 1071 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(-1,{\\sigma }^{2})$ and $P(X>1)=0.0618,$ use the table to find $\\sigma ,$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.9251\\,$ | $\\,0.9265\\,$ | $\\,0.9279\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.5\\,$ | $\\,0.9382\\,$ | $\\,0.9394\\,$ | $\\,0.9406\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.6\\,$ | $\\,0.9495\\,$ | $\\,0.9505\\,$ | $\\,0.9515\\,$ |\n\n$\\sigma ≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317170 1072 #uuid "f9f19fd2-6f5a-5fbb-8759-a811885f9345" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317170 1074 "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability using the complement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1) & =1-P(X>1) \\\\  & =1-0.0618 \\\\  & =0.9382\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1) & =0.9382 \\\\ P\\,(Z\\le \\frac{1-(-1)}{\\sigma }) & =0.9382 \\\\ Φ\\,(\\frac{2}{\\sigma }) & =0.9382 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.9382)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(1.54)=0.9382\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.9382)=1.54.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sigma } & =1.54 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{2}{1.54} \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈1.30,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317171 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317048 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317171 1069 "q-341655" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317171 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317171 1071 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(3,{\\sigma }^{2})$ and $P(X\\ge 1)=0.8413,$ use the table to find $\\sigma .$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.1587\\,$ | $\\,0.1562\\,$ | $\\,0.1539\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.1841\\,$ | $\\,0.1814\\,$ | $\\,0.1788\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.2119\\,$ | $\\,0.2090\\,$ | $\\,0.2061\\,$ |\n\n$\\sigma =$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317171 1072 #uuid "4a054739-7d5f-59e4-b190-1f466818f897" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317171 1074 "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability using the complement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1) & =P(X<1) \\\\  & =1-P(X\\ge 1) \\\\  & =1-0.8413 \\\\  & =0.1587\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1) & =0.1587 \\\\ P\\,(Z\\le \\frac{1-3}{\\sigma }) & =0.1587 \\\\ Φ\\,(-\\frac{2}{\\sigma }) & =0.1587 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.1587)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(-1.00)=0.1587\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.1587)=-1.00.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sigma } & =-1 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{2}{1} \\\\ \\sigma  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317172 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316721 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317172 1069 "q-34166" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317172 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317172 1071 "If $p=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $q=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$, then $p\\cdot q=$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317172 1072 #uuid "4c73928d-449b-56dc-94fe-ffe1e5cb8e3f" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317172 1074 "To compute the dot product, we add up the products of corresponding components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & ={p}_{1}\\cdot {q}_{1}+{p}_{2}\\cdot {q}_{2}+{p}_{3}\\cdot {q}_{3}+{p}_{4}\\cdot {q}_{4}+{p}_{5}\\cdot {q}_{5} \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot 2+4\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 4+3\\cdot (-3)+(-2)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-10-4-4-9-10 \\\\  & =-37\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317173 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316578 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317173 1069 "q-341671" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317173 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317173 1071 "Given that $f(x)=ln⁡(9x)$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317173 1072 #uuid "8869b9ad-0609-5467-9c5a-358cef5acc13" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317173 1074 "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=ln⁡u,\\,u=9x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=9.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9x}\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{9x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{9}}{\\cancel{9}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317174 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316828 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317174 1069 "q-341679" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317174 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317174 1071 "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $f(x)=ln⁡(7-4x)$ at the point where $x=-1.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317174 1072 #uuid "4e7be6bd-4db9-564a-80e4-a31c43152284" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317174 1074 "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=ln⁡u,\\,u=7-4x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-4.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7-4x}\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{7-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent line at the point where $x=-1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=-1} & ={-\\frac{4}{7-4x}|}_{x=-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{7-4(-1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{11}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317175 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316944 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317175 1069 "q-34169" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317175 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317175 1071 "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{4} \\\\ 4 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{-2} \\\\ -8x \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ If $u\\cdot v=12$ and $x<6$, find the value of $x.$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317175 1072 #uuid "b9fb100e-a0ce-5519-bd8a-5ba4a8438726" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317175 1074 "Starting from the equation $u\\cdot v=12,$ we compute the dot product in terms of $x$ and then solve for $x$ in the resulting equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & =12 \\\\ 2{x}^{4}\\cdot 2{x}^{-2}+4\\cdot (-8x)+12\\cdot 4+6\\cdot 2 & =12 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-32x+48+12 & =12 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-32x+48 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x+12 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x-6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe get two solutions, ${x}_{1}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=6.$ Since we need $x<6$, our final answer is $x=2$ only." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317176 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316652 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317176 1069 "q-341691" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317176 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317176 1071 "Given that $y={log}_{6}⁡(-3{x}^{3}),$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ in terms of $x.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317176 1072 #uuid "168ec5f7-4b56-507b-951f-2b0c361acc27" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317176 1074 "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={log}_{6}⁡(u),\\,u=-3{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{uln⁡(6)},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-9{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{uln⁡(6)}\\cdot (-9{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-9{x}^{2}}{-3{x}^{3}ln⁡(6)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{-3{x}^{2}}\\cdot 3}{\\cancel{-3{x}^{2}}\\cdot xln⁡(6)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{xln⁡(6)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317177 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317080 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317177 1069 "q-341694" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317177 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317177 1071 "Given that $y={log}_{4}⁡(\\sqrt{7x}),$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$  in terms of $x.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317177 1072 #uuid "d47d4d4a-5112-5202-876b-f3e480a31b61" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317177 1074 "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={log}_{4}⁡(u),\\,u=\\sqrt{7x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{uln⁡(4)},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{7x}}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{uln⁡(4)}\\cdot \\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{7x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7x}ln⁡(4)}\\cdot \\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{7x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2\\cdot 7xln⁡(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{7}}{2\\cdot \\cancel{7}xln⁡(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2xln⁡(4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317178 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316643 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317178 1069 "q-34174" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317178 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317178 1071 "![](images/d4/d4bd3b326c89316bc916658421bcd4c65c32a72672af9fbd7aa5397cb53ab374.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317178 1072 #uuid "278579d2-6c97-5a07-b8af-1fbac0d66174" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317178 1074 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The vector $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ lies inside the given subset. But \n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II does not represent a subspace. The vector $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$  lies inside the given subset. But\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram III represents a subspace. It represents the whole plane, which is considered a special subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317179 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317037 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317179 1069 "q-341745" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317179 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317179 1071 "If $x={y}^{4}-\\frac{3}{y},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at the point $(4,-1).$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(4,-1)}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317179 1072 #uuid "0a20dd68-509a-5704-9c10-a08b87646850" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317179 1074 "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{4}-\\frac{3}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{4})-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{-1}) \\\\  & =4{y}^{3}-3(-{y}^{-2}) \\\\  & =4{y}^{3}+3{y}^{-2} \\\\  & =4{y}^{3}+\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{y}^{5}+3}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{-1}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4{y}^{5}+3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the point $(4,-1),$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(4,-1)} & =\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{4(-1)^{5}+3}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317180 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316601 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317180 1069 "q-341751" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317180 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317180 1071 "If $x=-\\frac{16}{\\sqrt[3]{y}}+13,$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at the point $(5,8).$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(5,8)}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317180 1072 #uuid "cedc590a-5ca0-5fc5-bd76-db896782c449" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317180 1074 "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(-\\frac{16}{\\sqrt[3]{y}}+13) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(-16{y}^{-1/3}+13) \\\\  & =-16\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{-1/3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(13) \\\\  & =-16(-\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{-4/3})+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}{y}^{-4/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3{(\\sqrt[3]{y})}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{-1}=\\frac{3{(\\sqrt[3]{y})}^{4}}{16}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the point $(5,8),$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(5,8)} & =\\frac{3{(\\sqrt[3]{8})}^{4}}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(16)}{16} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317181 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317141 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317181 1069 "q-341757" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317181 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317181 1071 "If $x=-\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{y}}-2y\\sqrt{y},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at the point $(-14,1).$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(-14,1)}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317181 1072 #uuid "3fca9bbb-c820-579a-91ba-addcd4437f36" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317181 1074 "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(-\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{y}}-2y\\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(-12{y}^{-1/2}-2y\\cdot {y}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(-12{y}^{-1/2}-2{y}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =-12\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{-1/2})-2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =-12(-\\frac{1}{2}){y}^{-3/2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}{y}^{1/2} \\\\  & =6{y}^{-3/2}-3{y}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{y\\sqrt{y}}-3\\sqrt{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-3{y}^{2}}{y\\sqrt{y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{-1}=\\frac{y\\sqrt{y}}{6-3{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the point $(-14,1),$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(-14,1)} & =\\frac{1\\sqrt{1}}{6-3(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317182 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316676 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317182 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316709 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317182 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316764 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317182 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316862 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317182 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316893 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317182 1069 "q-341776" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317182 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317182 1071 "Let $f(x)$ be the $2L$-periodic extension of a function $F(x)$ from $x\\in [-L,L)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({{field-1}})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({{field-2}}).\n$$\n\nThe $2$nd cosine coefficient of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $F(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)$ and $L=3,$ the $2$nd cosine coefficient becomes\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}={{field-4}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{field-5}}.\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317182 1072 #uuid "db7b5b6b-a8e8-59b9-8c99-30fda8d90168" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317182 1074 "Recall that multiplying the argument of a function by $k\\ne 0$ represents the horizontal stretching of the corresponding graph by a factor of $\\frac{1}{k}.$\n\nNow, let $s=\\frac{\\pi }{L}x.$ Then, since $f(x)$ has a period of $2L,$ the function\n\n$$\ng(s)=f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s)\n$$\n\nmust have a period of $2L\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}=2\\pi .$ \n\nThe Fourier series of $g(s)$ is\n\n$$\ng(s)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(ns)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(ns),\n$$\n\nwhere:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\ {a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(ns)\\,\\text{d}s,\\,n\\ge 1 \\\\ {b}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(ns)\\,\\text{d}s,\\,n\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $s=\\frac{\\pi }{L}x$ and using the fact that $g(s)=f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s),$ we get the Fourier series of $f(x):$\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\n$$\n\nTherefore, $2$nd cosine coefficient of $f(x)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}(\\frac{\\pi }{L}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $L=3$ and $f(x)=F(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317183 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316688 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317183 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316713 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317183 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316821 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317183 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316948 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317183 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317140 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317183 1069 "q-341781" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317183 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317183 1071 "Let $f(x)$ be the $2L$-periodic extension of a function $F(x)$ from $x\\in [-L,L)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({{field-1}})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({{field-2}}).\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd sine coefficient of the series is\n\n$$\n{b}_{3}={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $F(x)={x}^{5}$ and $L=4,$ the $3$rd sine coefficient becomes\n\n$$\n{b}_{3}={{field-4}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{field-5}}.\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317183 1072 #uuid "bc07baec-9d65-5776-a893-8007dc5102e0" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317183 1074 "Recall that multiplying the argument of a function by $k\\ne 0$ represents the horizontal stretching of the corresponding graph by a factor of $\\frac{1}{k}.$\n\nNow, let $s=\\frac{\\pi }{L}x.$ Then, since $f(x)$ has a period of $2L,$ the function\n\n$$\ng(s)=f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s)\n$$\n\nmust have a period of $2L\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}=2\\pi .$ \n\nThe Fourier series of $g(s)$ is\n\n$$\ng(s)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(ns)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(ns),\n$$\n\nwhere:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\ {a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(ns)\\,\\text{d}s,\\,n\\ge 1 \\\\ {b}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(ns)\\,\\text{d}s,\\,n\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $s=\\frac{\\pi }{L}x$ and using the fact that $g(s)=f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s),$ we get the Fourier series of $f(x):$\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\n$$\n\nTherefore, $3$rd sine coefficient of $f(x)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}(\\frac{\\pi }{L}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $L=4$ and $f(x)={x}^{5},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {\\int }_{-4}^{4}{x}^{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317184 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316843 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317184 1069 "q-341789" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317184 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317184 1071 "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=6{x}^{2}+5x+1$ for $x\\in [-1,1).$ The Fourier series of the $2$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317184 1072 #uuid "e12f9894-53cb-5435-b393-ea7e08594626" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317184 1074 "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=1$ and $f(x)=6{x}^{2}+5x+1$ for $x\\in [-1,1).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(6{x}^{2}+5x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[2{x}^{3}+\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}+x]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(2+\\frac{5}{2}+1)-((-2)+\\frac{5}{2}+(-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{2}-(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317185 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317077 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317185 1069 "q-341790" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317185 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317185 1071 "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{4}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{4}x)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=3{x}^{2}-2x$ for $x\\in [-4,4).$ The Fourier series of the $8$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317185 1072 #uuid "6969b41a-9a37-587e-8acc-9fdaedc3d0de" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317185 1074 "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=4$ and $f(x)=3{x}^{2}-2x$ for $x\\in [-4,4).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}(3{x}^{2}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}]}_{-4}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(({4}^{3}-{4}^{2})-((-4)^{3}-(-4)^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(48-(-80)) \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317186 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316726 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317186 1069 "q-341791" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317186 1070 #ed