}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=7$ and $f(x)=3{x}^{2}-1$ for $x\\in [-7,7).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{7}{\\int }_{-7}^{7}(3{x}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}{[{x}^{3}-x]}_{-7}^{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}(({7}^{3}-7)-((-7)^{3}-(-7))) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}(336-(-336)) \\\\  & =96.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317188 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317130 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317188 1069 "q-341805" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317188 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317188 1071 "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{8}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{8}x)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=-x+5$ for $x\\in [-8,8).$ The Fourier series of the $16$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317188 1072 #uuid "f681e236-f02a-536f-b3c2-833df650a871" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317188 1074 "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=8$ and $f(x)=-x+5$ for $x\\in [-8,8).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{-8}^{8}(-x+5)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{[-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+5x]}_{-8}^{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}((-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {8}^{2}+5\\cdot 8)-(-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-8)^{2}+5\\cdot (-8))) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(8-(-72)) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317189 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316556 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317189 1069 "q-341825" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317189 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317189 1071 "Calculate the instantaneous rate of change of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-{x}^{2}$ when $x=0.$\n\n${f}^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317189 1072 #uuid "04d16541-33c4-5168-abf0-25ac42f61efd" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317189 1074 "The instantaneous rate of change when $x=0$ is given by the value of the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ at $x=0.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the derivative, using the fact that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})=2x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡x-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & ={sec}^{2}⁡(0)-2\\cdot 0 \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)})}^{2}-0 \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{1})}^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change is $1.$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317190 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316571 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317190 1069 "q-341833" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317190 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317190 1071 "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{4}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{4}x)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=-5x+2$ for $x\\in [-4,4).$ The Fourier series of the $8$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of ${b}_{3}.$\n\n${b}_{3}=$ {{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317190 1072 #uuid "89318c25-1cdb-5fb3-8e2f-8d5f3a050ff1" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317190 1074 "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the sine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=4$ and $f(x)=-5x+2$ for $x\\in [-4,4).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{b}_{3}=\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}(-5x+2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=-5x+2\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=-5 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=-\\frac{4}{3\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{4}(uv\\,{|}_{-4}^{4}-{\\int }_{-4}^{4}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(-\\frac{4}{3\\pi }{[(-5x+2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}x)]}_{-4}^{4}-{\\int }_{-4}^{4}(-\\frac{4}{3\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}x))(-5)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(-\\frac{4}{3\\pi }{[(-5x+2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}x)]}_{-4}^{4}-\\frac{20}{3\\pi }{\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(-\\frac{4}{3\\pi }((-18)(-1)-(22)(-1))-\\frac{80}{9{\\pi }^{2}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-3\\pi ))) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(-\\frac{4}{3\\pi }\\cdot 40) \\\\  & =-\\frac{40}{3\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317191 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317043 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317191 1069 "q-341858" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317191 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317191 1071 "Find the slope of $y=tan⁡x+2sin⁡x$ at $x=0.$\n\n${y}^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317191 1072 #uuid "194833de-eabd-521e-a061-701c99c91a20" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317191 1074 "We know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(tan⁡x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(sin⁡x)=cos⁡x.$ Applying these formulas, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(tan⁡x+2sin⁡x) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡x+2cos⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$