{3\\pi }-{\\int }_{-3\\pi }^{3\\pi }\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}x)\\cdot (-3)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }(\\frac{3}{4}[(-9\\pi +1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\pi )-(9\\pi +1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-4\\pi )]+\\frac{9}{4}{\\int }_{-3\\pi }^{3\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 0+\\frac{9}{4}\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{4}){[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}x)]}_{-3\\pi }^{3\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }(-\\frac{27}{16}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-4\\pi )]) \\\\  & =-\\frac{27}{16\\pi }(1-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317193 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316746 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317193 1069 "q-341915" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317193 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317193 1071 "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent at the point $(16,\\,4)$ for the curve $\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y}=6.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317193 1072 #uuid "0a97764f-f130-5df7-bef4-c5372f6f59c9" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317193 1074 "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{1/2}+{y}^{1/2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{y}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(16,\\,4)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(16,\\,4)} \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{\\sqrt{y}}{\\sqrt{x}})|}_{(16,\\,4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{4}}{\\sqrt{16}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317194 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316591 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317194 1069 "q-341918" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317194 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317194 1071 "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent at the point $(9,\\,4)$ for the curve $\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}=1.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317194 1072 #uuid "be11712d-8184-5176-b182-3e96ce7e8e3b" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317194 1074 "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{1/2}-{y}^{1/2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{y}}{\\sqrt{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(9,\\,4)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(9,\\,4)} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{\\sqrt{y}}{\\sqrt{x}})|}_{(9,\\,4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{4}}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317195 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316934 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317195 1069 "q-341981" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317195 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317195 1071 "The function $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x-5, & x<2, \\\\ 1, & x\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n${\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317195 1072 #uuid "5e18cf65-d9e0-507c-bafc-1a7de4ad3567" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317195 1074 "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-4}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}x-5)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,{\\int }_{2}^{6}1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}-5x){|}_{-4}^{2}\\,+\\,x{|}_{2}^{6} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{4}(2)^{2}-5(2)\\,-\\,(\\frac{1}{4}(-4)^{2}-5(-4))]\\,+\\,[\\,6-2\\,] \\\\  & =[1-10\\,-\\,(4+20)]+4 \\\\  & =[-9-24]+4 \\\\  & =-33+4 \\\\  & =-29.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317196 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316864 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317196 1069 "q-341991" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317196 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317196 1071 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(0,{\\sigma }^{2})$ is a normal random variable with $P(-2<X<2)=0.6578,$ find ${\\sigma }^{2}$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7995\\,$ | $\\,0.8023\\,$ | $\\,0.8051\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8264\\,$ | $\\,0.8289\\,$ | $\\,0.8315\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8508\\,$ | $\\,0.8531\\,$ | $\\,0.8554\\,$ |\n\n${\\sigma }^{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317196 1072 #uuid "171aa441-28c9-56ed-921a-96a0833a165c" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317196 1074 "First, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-2<X<2) & =0.6578 \\\\ 2P(0<X<2) & =0.6578 \\\\ P(0<X<2) & =0.3289 \\\\ P(X<2)-P(X<0) & =0.3289 \\\\ P(X<2)-0.5 & =0.3289 \\\\ P(X<2) & =0.8289 \\\\ P(X\\le 2) & =0.8289\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =0.8289 \\\\ P\\,(Z\\le \\frac{2-0}{\\sigma }) & =0.8289 \\\\ Φ\\,(\\frac{2}{\\sigma }) & =0.8289 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.8289)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.95)=0.8289\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.8289)=0.95.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sigma } & =0.95 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{2}{0.95} \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈2.105\\,26.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =(2.105\\,26)^{2} \\\\  & ≈4.43\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317197 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316704 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317197 1069 "q-341994" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317197 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317197 1071 "A function $g(x)$ has values according to the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $0.3$ | $0.6$ | $0.9$ | $1.2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g(x)$ | $-2.7$ | $-1.6$ | $-0.4$ | $1.1$ | $3.7$ |\n\nApproximate ${g}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=0.6$ using a forward difference approximation. Do not round the answer.\n\n${g}^{′}(0.6)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317197 1072 #uuid "2ad24a5a-c3d4-5218-8b93-0b9b0b21bc08" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317197 1074 "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{g(x+h)-g(x)}{h}\n$$\n\nat the point $x=0.6.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=0.3.$ This gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(0.6) & ≈\\frac{g(0.6+0.3)-g(0.6)}{0.3} \\\\  & =\\frac{g(0.9)-g(0.6)}{0.3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.1-(-0.4)}{0.3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.5}{0.3} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317198 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316852 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317198 1069 "q-341996" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317198 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317198 1071 "A function $g(x)$ has values according to the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g(x)$ | $20.4$ | $18.6$ | $16.5$ | $14.4$ | $12.3$ | $10.8$ |\n\nApproximate ${g}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=2$ using a forward difference approximation. Do not round the answer.\n\n${g}^{′}(2)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317198 1072 #uuid "430b3d34-e26b-5fad-a8ba-8f574096d22f" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317198 1074 "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{g(x+h)-g(x)}{h}\n$$\n\nat the point $x=2.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=2.$ This gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(2) & ≈\\frac{g(2+2)-g(2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{g(4)-g(2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{14.4-16.5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2.1}{2} \\\\  & =-1.05.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317199 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316585 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317199 1069 "q-342003" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317199 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317199 1071 "A function $d(y)$ has values according to the table below:\n\n| $y$ | $-5$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $d(y)$ | $-8.92$ | $-11.34$ | $-15.23$ | $-17.56$ | $-18.21$ | $-18.50$ |\n\nApproximate ${d}^{′}(y)$ at the point $y=-5$ using a forward difference approximation. Do not round the answer.\n\n${d}^{′}(-5)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317199 1072 #uuid "b2776278-e397-5818-9d00-bb84155b42d8" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317199 1074 "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{d}^{′}(y)≈\\frac{d(y+h)-d(y)}{h}\n$$\n\nat the point $y=-5.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=2.$ This gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}^{′}(-5) & ≈\\frac{d(-5+2)-d(-5)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{d(-3)-d(-5)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-11.34-(-8.92)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2.42}{2} \\\\  & =-1.21.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317200 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316804 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317200 1069 "q-342004" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317200 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317200 1071 "The function $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}-x+4, & x<1, \\\\ 2x-3, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n${\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317200 1072 #uuid "6e084cbe-6414-54d1-af07-4d96798fc98c" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317200 1074 "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1}(-x+4)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,{\\int }_{1}^{4}(2x-3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+4x){|}_{-3}^{1}+({x}^{2}-3x){|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =[\\,-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}+4\\cdot 1-(-\\frac{(-3)^{2}}{2}+4(-3))]+\\,[{4}^{2}-3\\cdot 4-({1}^{2}-3\\cdot 1)] \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{2}+4-(-\\frac{9}{2}-12)]+[16-12-(1-3)] \\\\  & =[\\frac{7}{2}-(-\\frac{33}{2})]+6 \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{2}+6 \\\\  & =20+6 \\\\  & =26.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317201 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317085 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317201 1069 "q-342008" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317201 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317201 1071 "The function $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x+2, & x<0, \\\\ -2x+1, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n${\\int }_{-4}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317201 1072 #uuid "0a5904e5-ff9f-50d9-91cb-c697a66fa301" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317201 1074 "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-4}^{0}(\\frac{1}{2}x+2)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}(-2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}+2x){|}_{-4}^{0}\\,+\\,(-{x}^{2}+x){|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{4}{0}^{2}+2(0)-(\\frac{1}{4}(-4)^{2}+2(-4))]\\,+\\,[-{3}^{2}+3-(-{0}^{2}+0)] \\\\  & =[\\,0-(4-8)]\\,+\\,[\\,-9+3-0] \\\\  & =4-6 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317202 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316996 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317202 1069 "q-342011" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317202 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317202 1071 "The function $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x<1, \\\\ -2x+3, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n${\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317202 1072 #uuid "0c8d2b0d-3143-5cce-8ab8-84982116a634" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317202 1074 "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,{\\int }_{1}^{3}(-2x+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}){|}_{-1}^{1}\\,+\\,(-{x}^{2}+3x){|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}(1)^{3}-(\\frac{1}{3}(-1)^{3})]\\,+\\,[-{3}^{2}+3(3)-(-{1}^{2}+3(1))] \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}-(-\\frac{1}{3})]\\,+\\,[0-2] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}-2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317203 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317082 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317203 1069 "q-342037" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317203 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317203 1071 "![](images/c5/c51e6206f77b63181f6c9f67b116ba5c0024886c061e8c15fe10edab87c87e03.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. \nApproximate ${f}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=3$ using a forward difference approximation with step size $h=2.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${f}^{′}(3)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317203 1072 #uuid "f82fb4ed-bb1b-59cb-b6b8-45248951f1e7" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317203 1074 "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=3.$ Applying the above formula with $x=3$ and $h=2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(3) & ≈\\frac{f(3+2)-f(3)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(5)-f(3)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-5}{2} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317204 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317004 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317204 1069 "q-342038" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317204 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317204 1071 "The volume of water in a tank was recorded over time for $25$ minutes, starting from time $t=0$ minutes. The following table shows the volume $V(t)$, in liters, recorded at time $t.$\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $5$ | $10$ | $15$ | $20$ | $25$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $V(t)$ | $120$ | $150$ | $165$ | $180$ | $215$ | $240$ |\n\nApproximate the rate of change of volume at time $t=5$ using a forward difference approximation. Do not round the answer.\n\nAt $t=5,$ the estimated rate of change is {{field-1}} liters per minute." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317204 1072 #uuid "3153e376-b458-59bd-bfbb-ba1fd9b605ce" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317204 1074 "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{V(t+h)-V(t)}{h}\n$$\n\nat the point $t=5.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=5.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(5) & ≈\\frac{V(5+5)-V(5)}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{V(10)-V(5)}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{165-150}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{5} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, at $t=5$ the rate of change was approximately $3$ liters per minute." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317205 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316840 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317205 1069 "q-342040" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317205 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317205 1071 "A hiker’s elevation was recorded over time for $6$ hours, starting from time $t=0$ hours. The following table shows the elevation $E(t)$, in meters, recorded at time $t.$\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $E(t)$ | $0$ | $200$ | $310$ | $395$ | $450$ | $525$ | $600$ |\n\nApproximate the rate of change of elevation at time $t=1$ using a forward difference approximation. Do not round the answer.\n\nAt $t=1,$ the estimated rate of change is {{field-1}} meters per hour." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317205 1072 #uuid "d324e09c-1fc6-52ea-9221-d54620c871a8" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317205 1074 "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{E}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{E(t+h)-E(t)}{h}\n$$\n\nat the point $t=1.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}^{′}(1) & ≈\\frac{E(1+1)-E(1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{E(2)-E(1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{310-200}{1} \\\\  & =110.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, at $t=1$ the rate of change was approximately $110$ meters per hour." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317206 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316634 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317206 1069 "q-342049" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317206 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317206 1071 "Given a curve $x=2y-\\frac{6}{y},$ what is the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point $(4,3)?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317206 1072 #uuid "36c42cd0-1bd6-5da5-bcdd-62163c6c7278" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317206 1074 "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2y-\\frac{6}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2y-6{y}^{-1}) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y)-6\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{-1}) \\\\  & =2(1)-6(-{y}^{-2}) \\\\  & =2+6{y}^{-2} \\\\  & =2+\\frac{6}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{y}^{2}+6}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{-1}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2{y}^{2}+6}.\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $(4,3)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(4,3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{3}^{2}}{2(3)^{2}+6} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{24} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point-slope formula for a tangent line, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{3}{8}\\cdot (x-4) \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{8}x-\\frac{3}{2}+3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{8}x+\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317207 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316943 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317207 1069 "q-342050" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317207 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317207 1071 "Given a curve $x=-\\frac{5}{y}+{y}^{2},$ what is the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point $(-4,1)?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317207 1072 #uuid "97a8acd9-41d2-5ef2-85c4-307d0b8faeeb" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317207 1074 "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(-\\frac{5}{y}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(-5{y}^{-1}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{-1})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2}) \\\\  & =-5(-{y}^{-2})+2y \\\\  & =2y+5{y}^{-2} \\\\  & =2y+\\frac{5}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{y}^{3}+5}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{-1}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2{y}^{3}+5}.\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $(-4,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(-4,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2(1)^{3}+5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2+5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point-slope formula for a tangent line, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot (x-(-4)) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{7}x+\\frac{4}{7}+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{7}x+\\frac{11}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317208 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316808 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317208 1069 "q-342061" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317208 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317208 1071 "Given a curve $x=6\\sqrt{y}-2{y}^{2},$ what is the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point $(4,1)?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317208 1072 #uuid "0c69ac0b-f1e2-5b4c-9ce4-6a862192ecf5" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317208 1074 "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(6\\sqrt{y}-2{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =6\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\sqrt{y})-2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2}) \\\\  & =6\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{1/2})-2(2y) \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{y}^{-1/2}-4y \\\\  & =3{y}^{-1/2}-4y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{y}}-4y \\\\  & =\\frac{3-4y\\sqrt{y}}{\\sqrt{y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{-1}=\\frac{\\sqrt{y}}{3-4y\\sqrt{y}}.\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $(4,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(4,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1}}{3-4(1)\\sqrt{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3-4} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point-slope formula for a tangent line, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =-(x-4) \\\\ y & =-x+4+1 \\\\ y & =-x+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317209 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316747 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317209 1069 "q-342070" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317209 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317209 1071 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(4,{\\sigma }^{2})$ is a normal random variable with $P(2<X<6)=0.2812,$ find ${\\sigma }^{2},$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6368\\,$ | $\\,0.6406\\,$ | $\\,0.6443\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6736\\,$ | $\\,0.6772\\,$ | $\\,0.6808\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.7088\\,$ | $\\,0.7123\\,$ | $\\,0.7157\\,$ |\n\n*Hint: Note that the interval $2<X<6$ is symmetric about the mean $\\mu =4.$*\n\n${\\sigma }^{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317209 1072 #uuid "797b642c-be56-5ed2-9827-fad42ffdc274" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317209 1074 "First, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<X<6) & =0.2812 \\\\ 2P(4<X<6) & =0.2812 \\\\ P(4<X<6) & =0.1406 \\\\ P(X<6)-P(X<4) & =0.1406 \\\\ P(X<6)-0.5 & =0.1406 \\\\ P(X<6) & =0.6406 \\\\ P(X\\le 6) & =0.6406\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 6) & =0.6406 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{6-4}{\\sigma }) & =0.6406 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{2}{\\sigma }) & =0.6406 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.6406)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.36)=0.6406\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.6406)=0.36.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sigma } & =0.36 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{2}{0.36} \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈5.555\\,56.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2}=(5.555\\,56)^{2}≈30.86\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317210 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316983 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317210 1069 "q-342075" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317210 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317210 1071 "![](images/d8/d84ba0a07821011be4cf5958af44ea73e97493c5a796a0324b068fe2adca8a12.png)\n\nA car is using petrol as it travels. The graph of the amount of petrol $P(d)$, in liters, remaining in the fuel tank after traveling a distance of $d$ miles is shown above. Approximate the rate of change of the amount of petrol at $d=150$ using a forward difference with step size $h=50.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n*Hint*: Assume that the rate of change is negative if the amount of petrol decreases.\n\nAfter traveling $150$ miles, the rate of change of the amount of petrol is {{field-1}} liters per mile." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317210 1072 #uuid "22176dc8-13fa-5e60-9e3c-ce6a132e5f6b" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317210 1074 "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(d)≈\\frac{P(d+h)-P(d)}{h},\n$$\n\nat the distance $d=150$ with step size $h=50.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(150) & ≈\\frac{P(150+50)-P(150)}{50} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(200)-P(150)}{50} \\\\  & =\\frac{10-30}{50} \\\\  & =-0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after traveling $150$ miles, the rate of change of the amount of petrol is $-0.4$ liters per mile." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317211 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317054 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317211 1069 "q-342079" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317211 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317211 1071 "![](images/84/846fc347c71ada4c01a5a85aabd0722203b84edbafcbe462064ae31d1cf2adbb.png)\n\nA drone is descending toward the ground. The graph of its height $H(t)$, in meters, after $t$ minutes is shown above. Approximate the rate of change of the height at $t=9$ using a forward difference with step size $h=2.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n*Hint*: Assume that the rate of change is negative if the height decreases.\n\nAfter $9$ minutes, the rate of change of the height is {{field-1}} meters per minute." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317211 1072 #uuid "fc754b10-9417-5d37-9ac6-29d601a462cd" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317211 1074 "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{H}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{H(t+h)-H(t)}{h},\n$$\n\nat the time $t=9$ with step size $h=2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}^{′}(9) & ≈\\frac{H(9+2)-H(9)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{H(11)-H(9)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-80}{2} \\\\  & =-40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after $9$ minutes, the rate of change of the height is $-40$ meters per minute." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317212 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317071 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317212 1069 "q-342080" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317212 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317212 1071 "![](images/ae/ae57faa04abd053e4a58d751cc084d58e07e84e0520c99a33a122f58dfd8f18c.png)\n\nA slice of jackfruit is placed in an air dryer. The graph of the moisture content $M(t)$, in percent, after $t$ hours is shown above. Approximate the rate of change of the moisture content at $t=3$ using a forward difference with step size $h=2.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n*Hint*: Assume that the rate of change is negative if the content decreases.\n\nAfter $3$ hour, the rate of change of moisture content is {{field-1}} percent per hour." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317212 1072 #uuid "870839f6-6361-5527-8457-2d1a8f03542c" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317212 1074 "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{M(t+h)-M(t)}{h},\n$$\n\nat the time $t=3$ with step size $h=2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}^{′}(3) & ≈\\frac{M(3+2)-M(3)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{M(5)-M(3)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{20-40}{2} \\\\  & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after $3$ hour, the rate of change of moisture content is $-10$ percent per hour." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317213 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316867 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317213 1069 "q-342107" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317213 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317213 1071 "Find the exact value of the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y={x}^{6}-4{x}^{4}+8{x}^{2}-4$ at the point where $x=-1.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317213 1072 #uuid "539fc4f1-fdc4-53aa-aff9-dc358cfa0420" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317213 1074 "Let $f(x)=({x}^{2}-1{)}^{3}+3.$ The slope of the tangent at $x=-1$ is equal to ${f}^{′}(-1).$ Computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,({x}^{6}-4{x}^{4}+8{x}^{2}-4) \\\\  & =6{x}^{5}-4(4{x}^{3})+8(2x)-0 \\\\  & =6{x}^{5}-16{x}^{3}+16x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate at $x=-1$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =6(-1{)}^{5}-16(-1{)}^{3}+16(-1) \\\\  & =-6+16-16 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=-1$ is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(-1)=-6.\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317214 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317115 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317214 1069 "q-34212" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317214 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317214 1071 "Determine if the vector $u$ lies on the plane $Π:r=sb+tc,$ $t,s\\in R$, where  $b=⟨1,7,-3⟩$, $c=⟨-8,10,-4⟩$, and  $u=⟨6,9,-4⟩.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of $s\\cdot t$ from the decomposition $u=sb+tc.$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317214 1072 #uuid "725d2e8b-44d5-5b25-872e-7c29b876b698" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317214 1074 "By definition, $u\\in Π$ if there exist $t$ and $s$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sb+tc & =u \\\\ s[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}s-8t=6 \\\\ 7s+10t=9 \\\\ -3s-4t=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & -8 & 6 \\\\ 7 & 10 & 9 \\\\ -3 & -4 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-7{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -8 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 66 & -33 \\\\ -3 & -4 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -8 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 66 & -33 \\\\ 0 & -28 & 14\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{66}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -8 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & -28 & 14\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+28{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -8 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}s-8t=6 \\\\ t=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nt=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s-8t & =6 \\\\ s-8(-\\frac{1}{2}) & =6 \\\\ s+4 & =6 \\\\ s & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we found a solution $s=2,\\,t=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we conclude that $u$ does indeed lie on the plane $Π.$ In fact, we have\n\n$$\nu=2b-\\frac{1}{2}c.\n$$\n\nFinally, $s\\cdot t=2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})=-1.$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317215 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316775 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317215 1069 "q-342128" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317215 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317215 1071 "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-\\frac{3}{5}$ and ${lim}_{x\\to {(-3/5)}^{-}}f(x)=7,$ then\n\n$f(-\\frac{3}{5})=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317215 1072 #uuid "13ea0f25-f277-59ff-83e7-dfd6094636f5" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317215 1074 "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-\\frac{3}{5}.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to (-3/5)}f(x)$ exists, and\n\n$$\nf(-\\frac{3}{5})={lim}_{x\\to (-3/5)}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {(-3/5)}^{-}}f(x)=7.\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317216 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317160 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317216 1069 "q-342153" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317216 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317216 1071 "Find the slope ${m}^{′}$ of the normal line to the curve $y=4-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}$ at the point $(\\frac{1}{4},2).$\n\n${m}^{′}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317216 1072 #uuid "2cc6d36c-e26a-5fcd-bcb0-d88e0fe39c17" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317216 1074 "Let $f(x)=4-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4-{x}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(\\frac{1}{4},2):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(\\frac{1}{4})=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3}}}=4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope ${m}^{′}$ of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{′} & =-\\frac{1}{m} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317217 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316570 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317217 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316920 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317217 1069 "q-342162" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317217 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317217 1071 "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=6{x}^{2}-7x+4$ at the point $(0,4).$\n\n$x\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\,y\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317217 1072 #uuid "7217f3b7-79ba-5f9c-b811-2d79787fbad8" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317217 1074 "Let $f(x)=6{x}^{2}-7x+4.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{x}^{2}-7x+4)=12x-7\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(0,4):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(0)=12(0)-7=-7\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=\\frac{1}{7}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-4 & =\\frac{1}{7}(x-0) \\\\ 7(y-4) & =x \\\\ 7y-28 & =x \\\\ x-7\\cdot y & =-28.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317218 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316742 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317218 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316938 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317218 1069 "q-342168" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317218 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317218 1071 "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{6}-\\frac{7}{4}{x}^{3}$ at the point $(2,-6).$\n\n$x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\,y\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317218 1072 #uuid "225f0ba1-57bb-5f34-91e4-9ca304d64d6b" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317218 1074 "Let $f(x)=\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{6}-\\frac{7}{4}{x}^{3}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{6}-\\frac{7}{4}{x}^{3})=\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{5}-\\frac{21}{4}{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(2,-6):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(2)=\\frac{3}{4}({2}^{5})-\\frac{21}{4}({2}^{2})=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-6) & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (x-2) \\\\ -3(y+6) & =x-2 \\\\ -3y-18 & =x-2 \\\\ x+3\\cdot y & =-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317219 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316861 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317219 1069 "q-34218" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317219 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317219 1071 "The vectors ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$, ${a}_{3}$, and $b$ are given below. Determine if $b\\in \\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}$ from the decomposition $b={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -14 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317219 1072 #uuid "54dedc31-eea3-5db1-a0e4-025cee199163" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317219 1074 "We know that $b\\in \\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ if there exist ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3} & =b \\\\ {x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -14 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3}=6 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3}=-14 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 6 & -14 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 & -14\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 & -14\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3}=6 \\\\ {x}_{2}=4 \\\\ {x}_{3}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=-2.$ Substituting into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-2(-2)=6\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\nSince the system has a solution, we can conclude that $b\\in \\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}+4{a}_{2}+(-2){a}_{3} & =b \\\\ 2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+4\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+(-2)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -14 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=2\\cdot 4\\cdot (-2)=-16.$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317220 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316853 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317220 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316987 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317220 1069 "q-342190" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317220 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317220 1071 "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt[3]{x}$ at the point $(\\frac{1}{8},\\frac{3}{4}).$\n\n$8x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\,y\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317220 1072 #uuid "773759d8-9f76-5cef-8ffc-9fa90fd16743" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317220 1074 "Let $f(x)=\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt[3]{x}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt[3]{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{1/3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{x}^{-2/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(\\frac{1}{8},\\frac{3}{4}):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(\\frac{1}{8})=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt[3]{{(\\frac{1}{8})}^{2}}}=2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-\\frac{3}{4} & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\ 16(y-\\frac{3}{4}) & =16\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (x-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\ 16y-12 & =-8(x-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\ 16y-12 & =-8x+1 \\\\ 8x+16\\cdot y & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317221 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316933 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317221 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316952 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317221 1069 "q-342200" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317221 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317221 1071 "Find where the normal line to the curve $y=4-{x}^{2}$ at the point $(1,3)$ intersects the $x$-axis.\n\nThe normal line intersects the $x$-axis at\n$($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}} $).$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317221 1072 #uuid "13b0b6f2-9e09-5a5a-a8f6-564d0221d479" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317221 1074 "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4-{x}^{2})=-2x.\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(1)=-2(1)=-2,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(-2)}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{2}+3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $x$-axis by setting $y=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{5}{2} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x & =-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ x & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $x$-axis at $(\\,-5,0\\,).$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317222 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316820 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317222 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317083 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317222 1069 "q-342201" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317222 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317222 1071 "Find where the normal line to the curve $y=3{x}^{2}-6x+5$ at the point $(0,5)$ intersects the $x$-axis.\n\nThe normal line intersects the $x$-axis at\n$($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}} $).$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317222 1072 #uuid "2f7f3a21-7e8d-51e9-8d45-2915b0e3fe06" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317222 1074 "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2}-6x+5)=6x-6.\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(0)=6(0)-6=-6,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(-6)}=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-5 & =\\frac{1}{6}(x-0) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{6}x+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $x$-axis by setting $y=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}x+5 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}x & =-5 \\\\ x & =-30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $x$-axis at $(\\,-30,0\\,).$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317223 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316728 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317223 1069 "q-342215" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317223 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317223 1071 "![](images/c4/c403f11093b76facafbecb72bcc413640ac67fe6ed73d6b64374230f39e29aba.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the left-opening parabola shown above. Express the answer in terms of the variable $y.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317223 1072 #uuid "28f921c8-6fd7-5d79-b2ff-0d1df567197f" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317223 1074 "The diagram shows that the parabola has its vertex at $O,$ and passes through the point $(-3,-1).$\n\nThe general equation of a left- or right-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-3$ and $y=-1$ into the equation above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)^{2} & =4p(-3) \\\\ 1 & =-12p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of our parabola is \n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{3}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{1}{3}x) \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{6y}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317224 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316597 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317224 1069 "q-342218" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317224 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317224 1071 "![](images/73/7367cb4ae1feb4e18b98b712687ceb4a1db67f8c9e4965af3ffd1253a827f346.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the left-opening parabola shown above. Express the answer in terms of the variable $y.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317224 1072 #uuid "a43aa514-b6a3-516e-bf25-cd1e713491f7" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317224 1074 "The diagram shows that the parabola has its vertex at $O,$ and passes through the point $(-6,6).$\n\nThe general equation of a left- or right-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-6$ and $y=6$ into the equation above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{6}^{2} & =4p(-6) \\\\ 36 & =-24p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of our parabola is \n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=-6x.\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-6x) \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-6 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{3}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317225 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317154 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317225 1069 "q-342226" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317225 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317225 1071 "Evaluate $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(-3).$ **Do not round** your answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(-3)=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-3}\\,+$ {{field-1}} $)$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317225 1072 #uuid "ee11962b-06b5-58e5-b379-0ac6c36480e1" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317225 1074 "The definition of $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(-1) & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-3}+{e}^{-(-3)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-3}+{e}^{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317226 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316997 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317226 1069 "q-342227" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317226 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317226 1071 "Evaluate $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}).$ **Do not round** your answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(\\frac{1}{3})=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{1/3}\\,+$ {{field-1}} $)$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317226 1072 #uuid "870bd804-7c6c-5b8b-aa71-4f5256e17cea" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317226 1074 "The definition of $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}) & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{1/3}+{e}^{-1/3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317227 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316990 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317227 1069 "q-342231" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317227 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317227 1071 "Evaluate $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/3)).$ **Do not round** your answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/3))=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317227 1072 #uuid "4233b404-fc80-58bf-a431-9b66af37156a" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317227 1074 "The definition of $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/3)) & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/3)}+{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/3)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/3)}+{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{3}+3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1+9}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317228 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316992 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317228 1069 "q-342240" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317228 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317228 1071 "Given that $z>0,$ simplify $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡{z}^{2})$ using the definition of the hyperbolic cosine.\n\n$\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡{z}^{2})=\\frac{1}{2}({z}^{6}\\,+$ {{field-1}} $)$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317228 1072 #uuid "6b8021d6-72b1-52c8-a3b7-18e10c429aa8" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317228 1074 "The definition of $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡{z}^{2}) & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡{z}^{2}}+{e}^{-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡{z}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{2})^{3}}+{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{2})^{-3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{6})}+{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{-6})}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({z}^{6}+{z}^{-6}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317229 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316903 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317229 1069 "q-342241" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317229 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317229 1071 "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=3$ and $f(3)=-3,$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{x+(f(x))^{3}}{2}=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317229 1072 #uuid "978ad046-894c-5148-bf03-4a350740e436" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317229 1074 "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=3.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=f(3)=-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{x+(f(x))^{3}}{2} & =\\frac{3+(-3)^{3}}{2}=-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317230 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316927 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317230 1069 "q-342242" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317230 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317230 1071 "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=6$ and $f(6)=14,$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {6}^{+}}\\sqrt{f(x)+{x}^{2}-1}=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317230 1072 #uuid "f0e58c97-5919-503f-b4f4-e136c98b02da" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317230 1074 "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=6.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to 6}f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {6}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to 6}f(x)=f(6)=14.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {6}^{+}}\\sqrt{f(x)+{x}^{2}-1} & =\\sqrt{14+{6}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{49} \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317231 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316869 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317231 1069 "q-342244" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317231 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317231 1071 "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-2$ and $f(-2)=2,$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}({x}^{3}+2(f(x))^{2})=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317231 1072 #uuid "d1febd71-2615-5d39-a0d9-4eccd6ea840d" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317231 1074 "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-2.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to -2}f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to -2}f(x)=f(-2)=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}({x}^{3}+2(f(x))^{2}) & =(-2)^{3}+2(-2)^{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317232 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316975 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317232 1069 "q-34226" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317232 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317232 1071 "Let $r=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $s=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}].$ Find $r+s.$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317232 1072 #uuid "f7a239b6-dd51-5d53-9de7-cf5ae156bb2c" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317232 1074 "We add the vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r+s & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4+1 \\\\ 2+5 \\\\ -3+2 \\\\ 1-8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317233 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316904 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317233 1069 "q-342262" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317233 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317233 1071 "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=7$ and $f(7)=5,$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 7}\\frac{(f(x)+1)^{2}}{x-f(x)}=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317233 1072 #uuid "3ba1c56e-5c7c-5788-ab8b-ceb450245e8a" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317233 1074 "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=7.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to 7}f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 7}f(x)=f(7)=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 7}\\frac{(f(x)+1)^{2}}{x-f(x)} & =\\frac{(5+1)^{2}}{7-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{2} \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317234 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316994 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317234 1069 "q-342267" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317234 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317234 1071 "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L{y(t)}$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+y={u}_{\\pi }(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\pi )\\,,\\,y(0)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{\\pi }(t)=u(t-\\pi )$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou may make use of the following result:\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0\n$$\n\n$Y(s)=$\n{{field-1}},$\\,s>0$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317234 1072 #uuid "e5fbd03e-952d-5ca5-b272-4b87d3810500" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317234 1074 "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}+y} & =L{{u}_{\\pi }(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\pi )} \\\\ L{{y}^{′}}+L{y} & =L{{u}_{\\pi }(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\pi )}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L{{y}^{′}},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{′}}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\pi }(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\pi )\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=\\pi $ and $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\pi ).$ This can be interpreted as time-delayed sinusoidal forcing. \n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)}={e}^{-as}\\cdot L{f(t+a)}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-\\pi )\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\pi )} & ={e}^{-\\pi s}\\cdot L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡((t+\\pi )-\\pi )} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi s}\\cdot L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi s}}{{s}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}}+L{y} & =L{{u}_{\\pi }(t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\pi )} \\\\ sY(s)+Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi s}}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ (s+1)Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi s}}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi s}}{({s}^{2}+1)(s+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317235 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317159 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317235 1069 "q-342275" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317235 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317235 1071 "$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{x}{(x+4)}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+4)^{2}}y=0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a **singular** point of the differential equation above?\n\n- $x=-4$\n- $x=-1$\n- $x=2$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317235 1072 #uuid "a8ba2fd2-65b4-550d-8354-517b0631809b" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317235 1074 "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both coefficients $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{x}{x+4}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+4)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check the analyticity of these functions at the given points.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=-4,$ since both denominators at $x=-4$ equal zero. So, $x=-4$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=-1.$ So, $x=-1$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=2.$ So, $x=2$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317236 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316874 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317236 1069 "q-342279" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317236 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317236 1071 "$$\n(x+1){y}^{″}+(x-3){y}^{′}+2xy=0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a **singular** point of the differential equation above?\n\n- $x=-1$\n- $x=0$\n- $x=3$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317236 1072 #uuid "905b994c-71d1-59e7-beab-bb9f96b34458" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317236 1074 "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both coefficients $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x+1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1){y}^{″}+(x-3){y}^{′}+2xy & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{x-3}{x+1}{y}^{′}+\\frac{2x}{x+1}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{x-3}{x+1}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{2x}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check the analyticity of these functions at the given points.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=-1,$ since both denominators at $x=-1$ equal zero. So, $x=-1$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=0.$ So, $x=0$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=3.$ So, $x=3$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317237 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316580 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317237 1069 "q-342290" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317237 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317237 1071 "$$\n(x-2)(x+3){y}^{″}+(x-1){y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is an **ordinary** point of the differential equation above?\n\n- $x=-3$\n- $x=0$\n- $x=2$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317237 1072 #uuid "aae5f1f1-0d61-5bac-bdd4-df8a71221a89" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317237 1074 "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both coefficients $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x-2)(x+3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)(x+3){y}^{″}+(x-1){y}^{′}+y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{x-1}{(x-2)(x+3)}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x-2)(x+3)}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{x-1}{(x-2)(x+3)}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x-2)(x+3)}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check the analyticity of these functions at the given points.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=-3,$ since both denominators at $x=-3$ equal zero. So, $x=-3$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=0.$ So, $x=0$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=2,$ since both denominators at $x=2$ equal zero. So, $x=2$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317238 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317000 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317238 1069 "q-342295" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317238 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317238 1071 "$$\n(x+3)(x-2){y}^{″}+(x-1){y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is an **ordinary** point of the differential equation above?\n\n- $x=-3$\n- $x=0$\n- $x=1$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317238 1072 #uuid "84a30812-4c38-529a-be0d-6e8e7676cbe4" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317238 1074 "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both coefficients $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x+3)(x-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)(x-2){y}^{″}+(x-1){y}^{′}+y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{x-1}{(x+3)(x-2)}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+3)(x-2)}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{x-1}{(x+3)(x-2)}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+3)(x-2)}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check the analyticity of these functions at the given points.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=-3,$ since both denominators at $x=-3$ equal zero. So, $x=-3$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=0.$ So, $x=0$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=1.$ So, $x=1$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317239 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316882 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317239 1069 "q-342296" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317239 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317239 1071 "$$\n(x+2)(x-1){y}^{″}+(x-3){y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is an **ordinary** point of the differential equation above?\n\n- $x=0$\n- $x=-2$\n- $x=1$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317239 1072 #uuid "3c8079ad-949a-5a26-9868-d6f6b2551b3d" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317239 1074 "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both coefficients $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x+2)(x-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)(x-1){y}^{″}+(x-3){y}^{′}+y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{x-3}{(x+2)(x-1)}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)(x-1)}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{x-3}{(x+2)(x-1)}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+2)(x-1)}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check the analyticity of these functions at the given points.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=0.$ So, $x=0$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=-2,$ since both denominators at $x=-2$ equal zero. So, $x=-2$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=1,$ since both denominators at $x=1$ equal zero. So, $x=1$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317240 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316986 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317240 1069 "q-342299" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317240 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317240 1071 "$$\n({x}^{2}-1){y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The functions $P(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-1}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}-1}$ are analytic about $x=0.$\n- The functions $xP(x)$ and ${x}^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=0.$\n- The point $x=0$ is a **regular** singular point of the differential equation." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317240 1072 #uuid "fac58339-a2d8-5129-8e48-737c1bfcbe88" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317240 1074 "A singular point $x=a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $({x}^{2}-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-1){y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-1}{y}^{′}+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}-1}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}-1}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=0.$ So, statement I is true.\n\n- Since $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=0,$ multiplying them by $x$ and ${x}^{2},$ respectively, they remain analytic about $x=0.$ Therefore, statement II is true.\n\n- Since statement I is true, $x=0$ is an ordinary point, not a singular point. Thus, statement III is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317241 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317016 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317241 1069 "q-342300" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317241 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317241 1071 "$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The functions $P(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ are analytic about $x=0.$\n- The functions $xP(x)$ and ${x}^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=0.$\n- The point $x=0$ is an **irregular** singular point of the differential equation." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317241 1072 #uuid "182bf2f2-0927-5526-ba0f-19a051c0f88c" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317241 1074 "A singular point $x=a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by ${x}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Since the denominators of both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ vanish at $x=0,$ they are *not* analytic about $x=0.$ So, statement I is false.\n\n- The function\n\n$$\nxP(x)=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}=1\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=0,$ and the function\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}Q(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=0.$ Therefore, statement II is true.\n\n- Since statement I is false, $x=0$ is a singular point. Also, since statement II is true, it follows that $x=0$ is a **regular** singular point. Thus, statement III is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317242 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316659 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317242 1069 "q-342303" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317242 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317242 1071 "$$\n({x}^{2}+1){y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The functions $P(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}$ are analytic about $x=-1.$\n- The functions $(x+1)P(x)$ and $(x+1)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=-1.$\n- The point $x=-1$ is a **regular** singular point of the differential equation." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317242 1072 #uuid "d598b560-2b94-5f0a-b115-9618b72a3e44" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317242 1074 "A singular point $x=a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $({x}^{2}+1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}+1){y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=-1.$ So, statement I is true.\n\n- Since $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=-1,$ multiplying them by $(x+1)$ and $(x+1)^{2},$ respectively, they remain analytic about $x=-1.$ Therefore, statement II is true.\n\n- Since statement I is true, $x=-1$ is an ordinary point, not a singular point. Thus, statement III is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317243 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316568 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317243 1069 "q-342306" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317243 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317243 1071 "$$\n({x}^{2}-4){y}^{″}+5x{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The functions $P(x)=\\frac{5x}{{x}^{2}-4}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{x}^{2}-4}$ are analytic about $x=2.$\n- The functions $(x-2)P(x)$ and $(x-2)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=2.$\n- The point $x=2$ is an **irregular** singular point of the differential equation." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317243 1072 #uuid "38f95697-88a9-5ddb-b3e7-2448323c712f" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317243 1074 "A singular point $x=a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $({x}^{2}-4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-4){y}^{″}+5x{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{5x}{{x}^{2}-4}{y}^{′}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{x}^{2}-4}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{5x}{{x}^{2}-4}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{x}^{2}-4}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Since the denominators of both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ vanish at $x=2,$ they are *not* analytic about $x=2.$ So, statement I is false.\n\n- The function\n\n$$\n(x-2)P(x)=(x-2)\\cdot \\frac{5x}{{x}^{2}-4}=\\frac{5x}{x+2}\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=2,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}Q(x)=(x-2)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{x}^{2}-4}=\\frac{(x-2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{x+2}\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=2.$ Therefore, statement II is true.\n\n- Since statement I is false, $x=2$ is a singular point. Also, since statement II is true, it follows that $x=2$ is a **regular** singular point. Thus, statement III is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317244 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316635 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317244 1069 "q-342307" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317244 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317244 1071 "$$\n(x+2){y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The functions $P(x)=\\frac{1}{x+2}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{3}}$ are analytic about $x=-2.$\n- The functions $(x+2)P(x)$ and $(x+2)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=-2.$\n- The point $x=-2$ is an **irregular** singular point of the differential equation." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317244 1072 #uuid "8575f381-396c-5d27-97ba-96e6bcb9f145" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317244 1074 "A singular point $a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x+2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2){y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x+2}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{3}}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Since the denominators of both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ vanish at $x=-2,$ they are *not* analytic about $x=-2.$ So, statement I is false.\n\n- The function\n\n$$\n(x+2)P(x)=(x+2)\\frac{1}{x+2}=1\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=-2,$ while the function\n\n$$\n(x+2)^{2}Q(x)=(x+2)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{(x+2)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{x+2}\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=-2$ since its denominator vanishes at $x=-2.$ Therefore, statement II is false.\n\n- Since statement I is false, $x=-2$ is a singular point. Also, since statement II is false, it follows that $x=-2$ is an **irregular** singular point. Thus, statement III is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317245 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316626 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317245 1069 "q-342311" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317245 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317245 1071 "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent at the point $(1,1)$ for the curve $2{y}^{3}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-{x}^{3}=2.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317245 1072 #uuid "45bf2a7e-b636-5c97-90c2-25563ca38da6" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317245 1074 "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the ${x}^{2}{y}^{2}$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{3}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-{x}^{3} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{y}^{3}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-{x}^{3}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{y}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}{y}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2) \\\\ 6{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+[2x{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}]-3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 6{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2x{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (6{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3{x}^{2}-2x{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}-2x{y}^{2}}{6{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(1,1)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,1)} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3{x}^{2}-2x{y}^{2}}{6{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}y})|}_{(1,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1)^{2}-2(1)(1)^{2}}{6(1)^{2}+2(1)^{2}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317246 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316575 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317246 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316888 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317246 1069 "q-342321" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317246 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317246 1071 "The equation of a curve is ${x}^{2}+xy=6{y}^{2}.$ The normal line at the point $(2,1)$ intersects the $y$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$\n\nThe coordinates of $P$ are $($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317246 1072 #uuid "9e44cd61-adda-5320-9484-a3532ad9dce1" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317246 1074 "First of all, we need to find the slope of the normal line at the point $(2,1).$ Remember that the slope of the normal line, ${m}^{′},$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the tangent line:\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $xy$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+xy & =6{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+xy) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{y}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{y}^{2}) \\\\ 2x+(y+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =12y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ 2x+y+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =12y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-12y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x-y \\\\ (x-12y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x-y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{-2x-y}{x-12y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(2,1)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,\\,1)} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{-2x-y}{x-12y})|}_{(2,\\,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2(2)-1}{2-12(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-5)}{(-10)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=-2.\n$$\n\nWe can write the equation of the normal at the point $(2,1)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & ={m}^{′}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =-2(x-2) \\\\ y-1 & =-2x+4 \\\\ y & =-2x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this normal line intersects the $y$-axis. At the point $P$ the $x$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $x=0$ and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2x+5 \\\\ y & =-2(0)+5 \\\\ y & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,\\,5).$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317247 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316750 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317247 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316795 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317247 1069 "q-342325" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317247 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317247 1071 "The equation of a curve is $4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=36.$ The tangent line at the point $(0,2)$ intersects the $y$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$\n\nThe coordinates of $P$ are $($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317247 1072 #uuid "cb9cd239-2bce-5b80-bfae-5a75f2ef2946" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317247 1074 "First of all, we need to find the slope of the tangent line at the point $(0,2).$ \n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(36) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(9{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(36) \\\\ 8x+18y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ 18y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-8x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{8x}{18y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{4x}{9y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(0,2)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,\\,2)} \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{4x}{9y})|}_{(0,\\,2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4\\cdot 0}{9\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write the equation of the tangent line at the point $(0,2)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-2 & =0(x-0) \\\\ y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this tangent line intersects the $y$-axis. At the point $P$ the $x$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $x=0$ and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are\n\n$$\n(0,\\,2).\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317248 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317049 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317248 1069 "q-342350" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317248 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317248 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=2x-{x}^{3}$ at the point  $(1,1).$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317248 1072 #uuid "c92be44a-7e91-581e-ab5b-c070d9da4442" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317248 1074 "Let $f(x)=2x-{x}^{3}.$ Computing the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x-{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}) \\\\  & =2-3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute ${f}^{′}(1)$, which gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(1)=2-3({1}^{2})=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=-1$ and the coordinates of our point $(1,1),$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =(-1)(x-1) \\\\ y & =-x+1+1 \\\\ y & =-x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317249 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317155 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317249 1069 "q-342354" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317249 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317249 1071 "![](images/85/85d75e0f3a9f2a30282d1f1754b0c46da49e8f73ff2a858c856ba5492eb21cca.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the right-opening parabola shown above. Express the answer in terms of the variable $y.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317249 1072 #uuid "3b67c83b-c5b3-5c60-b778-f810479299ef" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317249 1074 "The diagram shows that the parabola has its vertex at $O,$ and passes through the point $(8,-8).$\n\nThe standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=8$ and $y=-8$ into the equation above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-8)^{2} & =4p(8) \\\\ 64 & =32p \\\\ p & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of our parabola is \n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=8x.\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8x) \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =8 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{4}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317250 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316745 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317250 1069 "q-342355" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317250 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317250 1071 "![](images/1a/1aab10c5376ca6ae6c43829bc6f7617fb236b24c883d8ad61c47b83b75511d5a.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the right-opening parabola shown above. Express the answer in terms of the variable $y.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317250 1072 #uuid "c82bb467-368a-5a4c-94f6-b887899fd25e" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317250 1074 "The diagram shows that the parabola has its vertex at $O,$ and passes through the point $(6,-2).$\n\nThe standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=6$ and $y=-2$ into the equation above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2)^{2} & =4p(6) \\\\ 4 & =24p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of our parabola is \n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=\\frac{2}{3}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{3}x) \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{3y}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317251 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316743 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317251 1069 "q-342363" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317251 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317251 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=4\\sqrt[3]{x}$ at the point $(8,8).$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317251 1072 #uuid "b1461926-bf32-5450-8994-1731646edeb9" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317251 1074 "Let $f(x)=4\\sqrt[3]{x}.$ Computing the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\sqrt[3]{x}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt[3]{x}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{1/3}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{x}^{-2/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute ${f}^{′}(8)$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(8) & =\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt[3]{{8}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt[3]{64}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3\\cdot 4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=\\frac{1}{3}$ and the coordinates of our point $(8,8),$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-8 & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-8) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}x-\\frac{8}{3}+8 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{16}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317252 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317026 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317252 1069 "q-342367" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317252 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317252 1071 "$$\n(x+2){y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x+2}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{3}}y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The functions $P(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{4}}$ are analytic about $x=-2.$\n- The functions $(x+2)P(x)$ and $(x+2)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=-2.$\n- The point $x=-2$ is an **irregular** singular point of the differential equation." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317252 1072 #uuid "2a6497aa-26dd-5916-a133-e72ffa5f99b6" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317252 1074 "A singular point $a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x+2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2){y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x+2}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{3}}y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{4}}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{4}}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Since the denominators of both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ vanish at $x=-2,$ they are *not* analytic about $x=-2.$ So, statement I is false.\n\n- The function\n\n$$\n(x+2)P(x)=(x+2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{x+2}\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=-2$ since its denominator vanishes at $x=-2.$ Therefore, statement II is false.\n\n- Since statement I is false, $x=-2$ is a singular point. Also, since statement II is false, it follows that $x=-2$ is an **irregular** singular point. Thus, statement III is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317253 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317074 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317253 1069 "q-342369" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317253 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317253 1071 "$$\n(x-4){y}^{″}+\\frac{2}{x-4}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{2}}y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The functions $P(x)=\\frac{2}{(x-4)^{2}}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{3}}$ are analytic about $x=4.$\n- The functions $(x-4)P(x)$ and $(x-4)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=4.$\n- The point $x=4$ is an **irregular** singular point of the differential equation." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317253 1072 #uuid "6886daeb-f858-5dc5-8034-d2a1fce18371" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317253 1074 "A singular point $a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x-4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4){y}^{″}+\\frac{2}{x-4}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{2}}y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{2}{(x-4)^{2}}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{3}}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{2}{(x-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Since the denominators of both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ vanish at $x=4,$ they are *not* analytic about $x=4.$ So, statement I is false.\n\n- The function\n\n$$\n(x-4)P(x)=(x-4)\\cdot \\frac{2}{(x-4)^{2}}=\\frac{2}{x-4}\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=4,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x-4)^{2}Q(x)=\\frac{1}{x-4}\n$$\n\nis also *not* analytic about $x=4$ since its denominator vanishes at $x=4.$ Therefore, statement II is false.\n\n- Since statement I is false, $x=4$ is a singular point. Also, since statement II is false, it follows that $x=4$ is an **irregular** singular point. Thus, statement III is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317254 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316701 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317254 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317076 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317254 1069 "q-342377" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317254 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317254 1071 "Find where the tangent to the curve $y=\\frac{2}{x}+x$ at the point  $(2,3)$ intersects the $y$-axis.\n\nThe tangent intersects the $y$-axis at\n$($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}} $).$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317254 1072 #uuid "c5c6413c-46e6-5a5d-8df1-b86b59abbe4b" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317254 1074 "Let $f(x)=\\frac{2}{x}+x.$ Let's first calculate the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{x}+x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{-1})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =-2{x}^{-2}+1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at our point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={f}^{′}(2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{2}^{2}}+1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{4}+1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the tangent is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-2) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x-1+3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the tangent line intercepts the $y$-axis, we set $x=0$ and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}(0)+2 \\\\ y & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(0,2).$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317255 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316587 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317255 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316608 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317255 1069 "q-342379" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317255 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317255 1071 "Find where the tangent to the curve $y=-2\\sqrt{x}$ at the point  $(4,-4)$ intersects the $x$-axis.\n\nThe tangent intersects the $x$-axis at\n$($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}} $).$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317255 1072 #uuid "76b250c2-223b-54a5-8096-f983de3f885f" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317255 1074 "Let $f(x)=-2\\sqrt{x}.$ Let's first calculate the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2\\sqrt{x}) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x}) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at our point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={f}^{′}(4) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the tangent is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-4) & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-4) \\\\ y+4 & =-\\frac{1}{2}x+2 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x+2-4 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the tangent line intercepts the $x$-axis, we set $y=0$ and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}x-2 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x & =-2 \\\\ x & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(-4,0).$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317256 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316971 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317256 1069 "q-34238" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317256 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317256 1071 "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s$, where\n\n$$\nr(s)=⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s),\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s),\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)⟩,\\,0\\le s<\\sqrt{2}\\pi .\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317256 1072 #uuid "41d7c399-26f9-5663-8418-176ada36a15d" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317256 1074 "Let $T(s)$ denote the unit tangent to the given curve as a function of the arc length $s.$ Since we are given the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖=‖{r}^{″}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(s)$ and ${r}^{″}(s)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)\\cdot \\sqrt{2},\\,-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s))\\cdot \\sqrt{2},\\,-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s))\\cdot \\sqrt{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s),\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s),\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s))\\cdot \\sqrt{2},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)\\cdot \\sqrt{2},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)\\cdot \\sqrt{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nFinally, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(s) & =‖{r}^{″}(s)‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{2}s)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{2}s)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{2}s)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)+2{cos}^{2}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2({sin}^{2}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)+{cos}^{2}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s))} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317257 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316900 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317257 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317134 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317257 1069 "q-342380" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317257 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317257 1071 "Find where the tangent to the curve $y=-2\\sqrt{x}$ at the point  $(4,-4)$ intersects the $y$-axis.\n\nThe tangent intersects the $y$-axis at\n$($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}} $).$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317257 1072 #uuid "b6131df4-2cc2-54ce-b0ee-1f024fe8b757" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317257 1074 "Let $f(x)=-2\\sqrt{x}.$ Let's first calculate the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2\\sqrt{x}) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x}) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at our point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={f}^{′}(4) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the tangent is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-4) & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-4) \\\\ y+4 & =-\\frac{1}{2}x+2 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x+2-4 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the tangent line intercepts the $y$-axis, we set $x=0$ and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{2}(0)-2 \\\\ y & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(0,-2).$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317258 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316973 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317258 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317119 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317258 1069 "q-342381" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317258 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317258 1071 "Find where the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{x}+\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{x}}$ at the point  $(4,5)$ intersects the $y$-axis.\n\nThe tangent intersects the $y$-axis at\n$($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}} $).$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317258 1072 #uuid "fd3747ed-3ded-5af6-bc1a-40c4627f070b" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317258 1074 "Let $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}+\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{x}}.$ Let's first calculate the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x}+\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{x}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x})+6\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{1/2})+6\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2}+6\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}){x}^{-3/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at our point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={f}^{′}(4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4}}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{4}^{3}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{3}{8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the tangent is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-5 & =-\\frac{1}{8}(x-4) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{8}x+\\frac{1}{2}+5 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{8}x+\\frac{11}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the tangent line intercepts the $y$-axis, we set $x=0$ and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{8}(0)+\\frac{11}{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{11}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(0,\\frac{11}{2}).$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317259 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316766 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317259 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317102 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317259 1069 "q-342387" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317259 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317259 1071 "The equation of a curve is ${x}^{2}+2y=20.$ The tangent line at the point $(2,8)$ intersects the $x$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$\n\nThe coordinates of $P$ are $($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317259 1072 #uuid "69007ed8-dcbc-586e-9203-0e7716f8329c" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317259 1074 "First of all, we need to find the slope of the tangent line at the point $(2,8).$ \n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2y & =20 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+2y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(20) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(20) \\\\ 2x+2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ 2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(2,8)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,\\,8)} \\\\  & ={(-x)|}_{(2,\\,8)} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write the equation of the tangent line at the point $(2,8)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-8 & =-2(x-2) \\\\ y-8 & =-2x+4 \\\\ y & =-2x+12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this tangent line intersects the $x$-axis. At the point $P$ the $y$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $y=0$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =-2x+12 \\\\ 2x & =12 \\\\ x & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(6,\\,0).$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317260 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316593 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317260 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316720 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317260 1069 "q-342401" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317260 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317260 1071 "![](images/0c/0c89b1d28ce7692f54e0fbf32c1d99b091e24729c837883523d6150450decb17.png)\n\nThe equation $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=20x$ represents an ellipse. The tangent line at the point $(4,4)$ on the ellipse intersects the $y$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$\n\nThe coordinates of $P$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317260 1072 #uuid "019eae01-b09f-5893-aaf7-0f3dd1c87dc4" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317260 1074 "First of all, we need to find the slope of the tangent line at the point $(4,4).$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(20x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(20x) \\\\ 8x+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =20 \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =20-8x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{20-8x}{2y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{10-4x}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(4,4)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(4,\\,4)} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{10-4x}{y})|}_{(4,\\,4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{10-4\\cdot 4}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-6)}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write the equation of the tangent at the point $(4,4)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =-\\frac{3}{2}(x-4) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{2}(x-4)+4 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{2}x+10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this tangent line intersects the $y$-axis. At the point $P$ the $x$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $x=0$ and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{3}{2}x+10 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 0+10 \\\\ y & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,\\,10).$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317261 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317081 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317261 1069 "q-342402" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317261 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317261 1071 "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x)-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4},$ we have\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317261 1072 #uuid "dfe90c5f-4f9b-5154-ad49-fe645628cf50" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317261 1074 "Let $g(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x)$ and $h(x)=-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}.$ Then\n\n$$\nf(x)=g(x)+h(x).\n$$\n\nFirst, we differentiate $g(x).$ We express $g(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ng=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡u,\\,u=2x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡u\\operatorname{tan}⁡u\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡u\\operatorname{tan}⁡u\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate $h(x)=-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}.$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x} & =-{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={g}^{′}(x)+{h}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)-{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317262 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316636 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317262 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316838 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317262 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316886 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317262 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317003 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317262 1069 "q-342406" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317262 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317262 1071 "$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}x+1, & x\\in [-2,0) \\\\ -2, & x\\in [0,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-2,2).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-2,2)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | ${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317262 1072 #uuid "a3280171-70dd-50e2-8570-67e489365512" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317262 1074 "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{false}.$ The function $F(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/7c/7cfd6da222fb36d06be1153ac0e398e567f8b9fb8cd1cd9e21f1bb35ed131f8d.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{false}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}(x+1)=-2+1=-1\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}(-2)=-2.\n$$\n\nSo, ${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{false}$ since statements I and III are false.\n\n**Note:** The $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/a5/a59fad69c9790a2b899c21fd730f779c28bc9b70f44a67851ad5ca92bff21951.png)" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317263 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316847 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317263 1069 "q-34241" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317263 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317263 1071 "Find $j$, if ${a}_{1}-j{a}_{2}=b$ and\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317263 1072 #uuid "ce5d7202-47f0-50c1-9bc6-a025b8d27432" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317263 1074 "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $j$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}-j{a}_{2} & =b \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-j[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ j[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ j[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the final equation above, we can tell that $j=2.$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317264 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316558 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317264 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316644 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317264 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317061 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317264 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317110 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317264 1069 "q-342416" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317264 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317264 1071 "$$\nx(x-1)(x+3){y}^{″}+(x+3){y}^{′}+x(x-1)y=0\n$$\n\nClassify the following points for the differential equation given above.$\\,$The point $x=2$ is\n{{field-1}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=-3$ is\n{{field-2}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=0$ is\n{{field-3}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=1$ is\n{{field-4}}\npoint of the differential equation." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317264 1072 #uuid "4abf96b9-217f-538b-9e57-2f255c5fd433" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317264 1074 "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nA singular point $x=a$ is regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $x(x-1)(x+3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(x-1)(x+3){y}^{″}+(x+3){y}^{′}+x(x-1)y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x(x-1)}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{x+3}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{x(x-1)}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{x+3}.\n$$\n\nThe function $P(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $x(x-1)=0,$ which occurs at $x=0$ and $x=1.$\n\nThe function $Q(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $x+3=0,$ which occurs at $x=-3.$\n\nTherefore, $x=2$ is $\\text{an ordinary}$ point of the differential equation, and the singular points are\n\n$$\nx=-3,\\,x=0,\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's classify each singular point.\n\n- For $x=-3,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x+3)P(x)=(x+3)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x-1)},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=-3,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x+3)^{2}Q(x)=(x+3)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+3}=x+3,\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=-3.$ Therefore, $x=-3$ is $\\text{a regular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=0,$ the function\n\n$$\nxP(x)=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x-1)}=\\frac{1}{x-1},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=0,$ and the function\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}Q(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+3},\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=0.$ Therefore, $x=0$ is $\\text{a regular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=1,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x-1)P(x)=(x-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x-1)}=\\frac{1}{x},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=1,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x-1)^{2}Q(x)=(x-1)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+3},\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=1.$ Therefore, $x=1$ is $\\text{a regular singular}$ point of the differential equation." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317265 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317157 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317265 1069 "q-34242" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317265 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317265 1071 "Consider the vectors\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,d=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}=d,$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317265 1072 #uuid "8179ffc2-2381-5ec9-a386-111d6e4cae85" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317265 1074 "If ${x}_{1}{c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}=d,$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0=4 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=5 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first equation is a false statement. Hence,the system has no solution." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317266 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317117 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317266 1069 "q-342420" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317266 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317266 1071 "Given that $f(x)={sin}^{7}⁡(x),$ we have\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317266 1072 #uuid "6e815036-fddb-52a7-beac-06443eed54ea" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317266 1074 "First, we express\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))^{7}.\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={u}^{7},\\,u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=7{u}^{6},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =7{u}^{6}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\  & =7{sin}^{6}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317267 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316625 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317267 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316813 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317267 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317030 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317267 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317044 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317267 1069 "q-342421" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317267 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317267 1071 "$$\n(x-1)(x+2)(x-4){y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{(x-1)(x+2)}{y}^{′}+(x-1)(x-4)y=0\n$$\n\nClassify the following points for the differential equation given above.$\\,$The point $x=-2$ is\n{{field-1}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=0$ is\n{{field-2}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=1$ is\n{{field-3}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=4$ is\n{{field-4}}\npoint of the differential equation." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317267 1072 #uuid "34151355-a1e7-553f-a70a-6a86465ea22c" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317267 1074 "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nA singular point $x=a$ is regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x-1)(x+2)(x-4):$\n\n$$\n\\begi