2186317271 1072 #uuid "9329f849-29f3-5775-a364-358d8e081597" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317271 1074 "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nA singular point $x=a$ is regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by ${x}^{2}(x-7)^{2}(x+1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(x-7)^{2}(x+1){y}^{″}+(x+1){y}^{′}+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x(x-7)}y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}(x-7)^{2}}{y}^{′}+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{3}(x-7)^{3}(x+1)}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}(x-7)^{2}}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{3}(x-7)^{3}(x+1)}.\n$$\n\nThe function $P(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where ${x}^{2}(x-7)^{2}=0,$ which occurs at $x=0$ and $x=7.$\n\nThe function $Q(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where ${x}^{3}(x-7)^{3}(x+1)=0,$ which occurs at $x=-1,$ $x=0,$ and $x=7.$\n\nTherefore, $x=1$ is $\\text{an ordinary}$ point of the differential equation, and the singular points are\n\n$$\nx=-1,\\,x=0,\\,x=7.\n$$\n\nNow, let's classify each singular point.\n\n- For $x=-1,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x+1)P(x)=(x+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}(x-7)^{2}}=\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}(x-7)^{2}},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=-1,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x+1)^{2}Q(x)=(x+1)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{3}(x-7)^{3}(x+1)}=\\frac{(x+1){e}^{x}}{{x}^{3}(x-7)^{3}},\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=-1.$ Thus, $x=-1$ is $\\text{a regular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=0,$ the function\n\n$$\nxP(x)=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}(x-7)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{x(x-7)^{2}},\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=0.$ So, $x=0$ is $\\text{an irregular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=7,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x-7)P(x)=(x-7)\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}(x-7)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}(x-7)},\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=7.$ Therefore, $x=7$ is $\\text{an irregular singular}$ point of the differential equation." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317272 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316908 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317272 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316959 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317272 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317137 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317272 1069 "q-342443" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317272 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317272 1071 "Given that $y=4\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)),$ we have\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}$\\operatorname{sec}⁡($\n{{field-2}} $)\\operatorname{tan}⁡($\n{{field-3}} $).$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317272 1072 #uuid "50d04d8e-3b54-51a1-977a-8035c4a5ed2a" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317272 1074 "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=4\\operatorname{sec}⁡(u),\\,u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=4\\operatorname{sec}⁡(u)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(u),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sec}⁡(u)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(u)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317273 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316646 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317273 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316894 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317273 1069 "q-342445" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317273 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317273 1071 "Given that $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)),$ we have\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x))\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡($\n{{field-2}} $).$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317273 1072 #uuid "36a01249-dd25-5201-9ce2-0ee325c40559" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317273 1074 "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(u),\\,u=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(u)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(u),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(u)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(u)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x))\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317274 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317034 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317274 1069 "q-34245" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317274 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317274 1071 "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(t)$ given that its derivative ${r}^{′}(t)$ and the derivative of the corresponding unit tangent ${T}^{′}(t)$ are as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)=⟨\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,\\,{T}^{′}(t)=⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317274 1072 #uuid "370f1619-74a5-5906-bb7b-088dc720563d" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317274 1074 "Since we are given both the derivative of the curve ${r}^{′}(t)$ and the derivative of the unit tangent ${T}^{′}(t)$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the magnitudes of ${r}^{′}(t)$ and ${T}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}){cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =1 \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{3{sin}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317275 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316559 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317275 1069 "q-342473" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317275 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317275 1071 "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L{y(t)}$ for $s>3$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+y=-8\\operatorname{cosh}⁡3t,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nYou may make use of the following result:\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>|\\omega |.\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317275 1072 #uuid "975ef431-15ea-578a-acac-078a5e05d498" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317275 1074 "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}+y} & =L{-8\\operatorname{cosh}⁡3t} \\\\ L{{y}^{′}}+L{y} & =-8L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L{{y}^{′}},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=1:$\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{′}}=sL{y}-y(0)=sY(s)-1\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have the following result:\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡3t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-9},\\,s>3\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can solve for $Y(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}}+L{y} & =-8L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡3t} \\\\ (sY(s)-1)+Y(s) & =-8(\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-9}) \\\\ sY(s)+Y(s) & =1-\\frac{8s}{{s}^{2}-9} \\\\ (s+1)Y(s) & =\\frac{{s}^{2}-8s-9}{{s}^{2}-9} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{{s}^{2}-8s-9}{({s}^{2}-9)(s+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $s>3,$ we simplify as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y(s) & =\\frac{({s}^{2}-8s-9)}{({s}^{2}-9)(s+1)} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{(s-9)(s+1)}{({s}^{2}-9)(s+1)} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{s-9}{{s}^{2}-9}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317276 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316733 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317276 1069 "q-342479" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317276 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317276 1071 "$$\n({x}^{2}-25){y}^{″}+2x{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The functions $P(x)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}-25}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)}{{x}^{2}-25}$ are analytic about $x=5.$\n- The functions $(x-5)P(x)$ and $(x-5)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=5.$\n- The point $x=5$ is a **regular** singular point of the differential equation." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317276 1072 #uuid "dd3443d8-893d-55ee-8ecb-d75beee76ce3" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317276 1074 "A singular point $x=a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $({x}^{2}-25):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-25){y}^{″}+2x{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}-25}{y}^{′}+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)}{{x}^{2}-25}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}-25}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)}{{x}^{2}-25}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Since the denominators of both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ vanish at $x=5,$ they are *not* analytic about $x=5.$ So, statement I is false.\n\n- The function\n\n$$\n(x-5)P(x)=(x-5)\\cdot \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}-25}=\\frac{2x}{x+5}\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=5,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x-5)^{2}Q(x)=(x-5)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)}{{x}^{2}-25}=\\frac{(x-5)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)}{x+5}\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=5.$ Therefore, statement II is true.\n\n- Since statement I is false, $x=5$ is a singular point. Also, since statement II is true, it follows that $x=5$ is a **regular** singular point. Thus, statement III is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317277 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316583 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317277 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316586 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317277 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316613 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317277 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316765 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317277 1069 "q-342498" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317277 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317277 1071 "$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x+3, & x\\in [-3,0) \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-3, & x\\in [0,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-3,3).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-3,3)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | ${lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317277 1072 #uuid "8d3c4876-7b0c-57e1-8baf-f6c027fdfed6" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317277 1074 "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{false}.$ The function $F(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/38/385029e7709a8d4eda45b101aba6f553c79ae3baef5b97080844e307a0b71cc7.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{true}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}(2x+3)=2(-3)+3=-3\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}((x-3)^{2}-3)=(3-3)^{2}-3=-3.\n$$\n\nSo, ${lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{false}$ since statement I is false.\n\n**Note:** The $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/f4/f4bfe80cbcc0821e93012a859ccc91b3614fa7db2df8e25399fbadc3ef7a3355.png)" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317278 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316588 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317278 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317010 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317278 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317093 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317278 1069 "q-342501" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317278 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317278 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+3y=4{e}^{t},\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below to find the solution using Laplace transforms.\n\nLet $Y(s)=L{y(t)}.$ Taking the Laplace transform of both sides of the equation and solving for $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\nY(s)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nThe partial fraction decomposition of $Y$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)={{field-2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(t)={{field-3}}.\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317278 1072 #uuid "42962f63-2255-5ca0-b6d9-6becd9c2dd54" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317278 1074 "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides of the differential equation. Using the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{′}}+3L{y}=L{4{e}^{t}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the transform of a derivative, $L{{y}^{′}}=sY(s)-y(0),$ and the standard transform \n\n$$\nL{{e}^{at}}=\\frac{1}{s-a},\\,s>a,\n$$\n\n \nwe substitute the known values.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sY(s)-0+3Y(s) & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-1} \\\\ (s+3)Y(s) & =\\frac{4}{s-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $Y(s),$ we have\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{4}{(s-1)(s+3)}.\n$$\n\nNext, we perform a partial fraction decomposition. We write $Y(s)$ in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{(s-1)(s+3)}=\\frac{A}{s-1}+\\frac{B}{s+3}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants $A$ and $B,$ we first clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n4=A(s+3)+B(s-1)\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Setting $s=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n4=A(1+3)+B(0)\\,⟹\\,4=4A\\,⟹\\,A=1.\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n4=A(0)+B(-3-1)\\,⟹\\,4=-4B\\,⟹\\,B=-1.\n$$\n\nThus, the decomposition is\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{1}{s-1}-\\frac{1}{s+3}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-1}} & ={e}^{t} \\\\ {L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+3}} & ={e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the inverse Laplace transform of $Y(s),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-1}}-{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+3}} \\\\ y(t) & ={e}^{t}-{e}^{-3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317279 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316807 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317279 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317127 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317279 1069 "q-342526" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317279 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317279 1071 "![](images/d9/d997ec4e099fc8be5b34a51bdfd0e9511915c348671d9832ed640f0ec4360602.png)\n\nThe equation $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=15y$ represents an ellipse. The normal line at the point $(3,3)$ on the ellipse intersects the $x$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$\n\nThe coordinates of $P$ are $($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317279 1072 #uuid "96578920-5978-5ccd-b815-b15c23dd1fbe" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317279 1074 "First of all, we need to find the slope of the normal line at the point $(3,3).$ Remember that the slope of the normal line, ${m}^{′},$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the tangent line:\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =15y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(15y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(15y) \\\\ 8x+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =15\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-15\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-8x \\\\ (2y-15)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-8x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{8x}{2y-15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(3,3)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(3,\\,3)} \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{8x}{2y-15})|}_{(3,\\,3)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8\\cdot 3}{2\\cdot 3-15} \\\\  & =-\\frac{24}{(-9)} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(\\frac{8}{3})}=-\\frac{3}{8}.\n$$\n\nWe can write the equation of the normal line at the point $(3,3)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & ={m}^{′}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =-\\frac{3}{8}(x-3) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{8}x+\\frac{9}{8}+3 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{8}x+\\frac{33}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this normal line intersects the $x$-axis. At the point $P$ the $y$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $y=0$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{3}{8}x+\\frac{33}{8} \\\\ 0 & =-\\frac{3}{8}x+\\frac{33}{8} \\\\ \\frac{3}{8}x & =\\frac{33}{8} \\\\ x & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(11,\\,0).$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317280 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317038 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317280 1069 "q-342532" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317280 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317280 1071 "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L{y(t)}$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y=2{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot (t-2)^{3}\\,,\\,y(0)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{2}(t)=u(t-2)$ is the unit step function.\n\n$Y(s)=$\n{{field-1}},$\\,s>0$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317280 1072 #uuid "97923b3c-8f90-512a-b7ec-bb8a53b3d22c" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317280 1074 "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}+4y} & =L{2{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot (t-2)^{3}} \\\\ L{{y}^{′}}+4L{y} & =L{2{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot (t-2)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L{{y}^{′}},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{′}}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n2{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot (t-2)^{3}\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=2$ and $f(t)=2(t-2)^{3}.$\n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)}={e}^{-as}\\cdot L{f(t+a)}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{2u(t-2)\\cdot (t-2)^{3}} & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot L{2((t+2)-2)^{3}} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot L{2{t}^{3}} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot 2\\cdot \\frac{3!}{{s}^{3+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12{e}^{-2s}}{{s}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}}+4L{y} & =L{2{u}_{2}(t)(t-2)^{3}} \\\\ sY(s)+4Y(s) & =\\frac{12{e}^{-2s}}{{s}^{4}} \\\\ (s+4)Y(s) & =\\frac{12{e}^{-2s}}{{s}^{4}} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{12{e}^{-2s}}{{s}^{4}(s+4)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317281 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316563 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317281 1069 "q-342540" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317281 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317281 1071 "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L{y(t)}$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=2{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot {e}^{4t-8}\\,,\\,y(0)=-1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{2}(t)=u(t-2)$ is the unit step function.\n\n$Y(s)=-\\frac{1}{s-2}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-1}},$\\,s>4$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317281 1072 #uuid "d8fd84c4-0e86-59b2-88fc-ff50e7b9fc51" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317281 1074 "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}-2y} & =L{2{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot {e}^{4t-8}} \\\\ L{{y}^{′}}-2L{y} & =L{2{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot {e}^{4t-8}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L{{y}^{′}},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=1:$\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{′}}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)-(-1)=sY(s)+1\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n2{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot {e}^{4t-8}\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=2$ and $f(t)=2{e}^{4t-8}.$\n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)}={e}^{-as}\\cdot L{f(t+a)}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{2u(t-2)\\cdot {e}^{4t-8}} & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot L{2{e}^{4(t+2)-8}} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot L{2{e}^{4t}} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot \\frac{2}{s-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{-2s}}{s-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}}-2L{y} & =L{2{u}_{2}(t){e}^{4t-8}} \\\\ (sY(s)+1)-2Y(s) & =\\frac{2{e}^{-2s}}{s-4} \\\\ (s-2)Y(s)+1 & =\\frac{2{e}^{-2s}}{s-4} \\\\ (s-2)Y(s) & =-1+\\frac{2{e}^{-2s}}{s-4} \\\\ Y(s) & =-\\frac{1}{s-2}+\\frac{2{e}^{-2s}}{(s-4)(s-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317282 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316842 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317282 1069 "q-342561" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317282 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317282 1071 "![](images/ad/adcee8bddcbbd96e18d63f85f5b9ebf2edc52897cc1ddcca7e377194462f6bda.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(-1,0)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ The point $B$ has coordinates $(0,-1)$ and lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ If ${f}^{′}(-1)=3,$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317282 1072 #uuid "c15b3ea9-8f3d-5cc5-a69a-f9caee4c79da" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317282 1074 "Since $B\\,(0,-1)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(0)=-1.$\nWe're also told that ${f}^{′}(-1)=3.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317283 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316811 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317283 1069 "q-342562" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317283 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317283 1071 "![](images/08/08ce8625dcad742776846497c4e49b5686a893be85ff1bd5426df8f8b9ce882b.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(1,1)$ and lies on both $y=f(x)$ and $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ If ${f}^{′}(1)=-2,$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317283 1072 #uuid "560351d2-9c68-5425-b187-0b2629e5a704" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317283 1074 "Since $A\\,(1,1)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(1)=1.$\nWe're also told that ${f}^{′}(1)=-2.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317284 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317095 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317284 1069 "q-342570" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317284 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317284 1071 "![](images/fe/feb02396a635c3f416b07383b03269e12fde738da3345aa6d6d9fe5350745746.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(1,0.5)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ The point $B$ has coordinates $(0.5,1)$ and lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ If ${f}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4},$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0.5)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(0.5)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317284 1072 #uuid "4c8c7a88-6e10-5793-8076-5cf568ce9f31" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317284 1074 "Since $B\\,(0.5,1)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(0.5)=1.$\nWe're also told that ${f}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0.5) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0.5))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4})} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡4.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317285 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317053 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317285 1069 "q-342575" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317285 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317285 1071 "![](images/ce/ce948d3eade308a90ffa85a7c7a88a8c403a6620ffda7e6d24444d2abac0770c.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(1,1)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(1,1)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317285 1072 #uuid "f2edf191-9bcb-50c6-bc5b-b3185f8280c9" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317285 1074 "Since $(1,1)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(1)=1.$ \nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [-2,4]$ is $\\frac{2}{3}$, hence, ${f}^{′}(1)=\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is \n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317286 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317120 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317286 1069 "q-342579" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317286 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317286 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}+1}$ and the point $P(1,0)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0).$\n\n${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317286 1072 #uuid "11b19bb2-4705-5a35-a91c-74ec5215102a" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317286 1074 "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(1,0).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{(x-1)^{′}\\cdot ({x}^{2}+1)-(x-1)\\cdot {({x}^{2}+1)}^{′}}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1\\cdot ({x}^{2}+1)-(x-1)\\cdot 2x}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+1-2{x}^{2}+2x}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1+2x-{x}^{2}}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1+2(1)-{1}^{2}}{{({1}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(1,0)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(0,1)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317287 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316960 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317287 1069 "q-342580" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317287 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317287 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=3\\sqrt[3]{x}$ and the point $P(8,6)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(6).$\n\n${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(6)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317287 1072 #uuid "db483005-a4e5-5199-9df5-a86ac78101b5" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317287 1074 "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(8,6).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =3\\sqrt[3]{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{x}^{-2/3} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(8) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{8}^{2}}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(8) & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(8,6)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(6,8)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(6) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(6))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(8)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317288 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316621 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317288 1069 "q-342581" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317288 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317288 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{x}$ and the point $P(4,1)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ the slope of $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ at the point $(1,4)$ is$\\,${{field-1}}." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317288 1072 #uuid "26ec795a-8d02-545c-bd63-a40b17a5e053" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317288 1074 "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(4,1).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{4}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(4,1)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(1,4)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{8})} \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317289 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317078 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317289 1069 "q-342582" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317289 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317289 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)={e}^{2x}$ and the point $P(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,4)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4).$\n\n${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317289 1072 #uuid "01236ef1-a695-5a6b-b74a-3c72e53e2a80" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317289 1074 "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,4).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{2x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =2{e}^{2x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =2{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =2{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =2\\cdot 4 \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =8.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,4)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(4,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317290 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316777 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317290 1069 "q-342583" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317290 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317290 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and the point $P(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1).$\n\n${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317290 1072 #uuid "92ca3690-4c8c-55b7-be9d-9370a5311a98" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317290 1074 "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =2.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317291 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316930 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317291 1069 "q-342584" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317291 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317291 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and the point $P(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ the slope of $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ at the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ is$\\,${{field-1}}." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317291 1072 #uuid "e562f9e3-80f6-5a8e-be6e-6fda00e93b68" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317291 1074 "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317292 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316801 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317292 1069 "q-342585" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317292 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317292 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and the point $P(e,1)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1).$\n\n${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317292 1072 #uuid "eddd138b-b8bc-570d-b13d-ca41c49e9e70" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317292 1074 "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(e,1).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(e) & =\\frac{1}{e}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(e,1)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(1,e)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(e)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{e})} \\\\  & =e.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317293 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317129 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317293 1069 "q-342588" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317293 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317293 1071 "Find $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)$ given the following table of values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $-3$ | $-2$ | $4$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $-7$ |\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317293 1072 #uuid "8517d68d-8110-58f2-a065-5eed954b7e43" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317293 1074 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(1)=2$ and ${f}^{′}(2)=-7.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-7)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317294 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316786 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317294 1069 "q-342589" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317294 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317294 1071 "Find $({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1)$ given the following table of values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $-1$ | $6$ |\n| $4$ | $3$ | $-2$ |\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317294 1072 #uuid "cca80ae0-62ef-5413-ba41-4aa5821f7e48" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317294 1074 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-1))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(-1)=-2$ and ${f}^{′}(-2)=6.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317295 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317029 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317295 1069 "q-342590" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317295 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317295 1071 "Find $({f}^{-1})^{′}(4)$ given the following table of values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-9$ |\n| $-3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(4)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317295 1072 #uuid "918f9438-aa6e-5a76-8250-20275f26c02d" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317295 1074 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(4)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(4)=-3$ and ${f}^{′}(-3)=5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317296 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316694 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317296 1069 "q-342591" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317296 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317296 1071 "Find $({f}^{-1})^{′}(-2)$ given the following table of values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $5$ | $-2$ | $-3$ |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $7$ |\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(-2)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317296 1072 #uuid "89938d94-24bb-5069-96de-7c0c5e9ce7ac" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317296 1074 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(-2)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-2))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(-2)=5$ and ${f}^{′}(5)=-3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(-2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-3)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317297 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317070 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317297 1069 "q-342592" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317297 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317297 1071 "Find $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)$ given the following table of values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| $2$ | $0$ | $-8$ |\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317297 1072 #uuid "2eeba335-8257-5e02-8c8c-83f41ecad374" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317297 1074 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(0)=2$ and ${f}^{′}(2)=-8.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-8)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317298 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317039 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317298 1069 "q-342596" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317298 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317298 1071 "Find ${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)$ of the product of functions, given the following table of values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $0$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $3$ | $-1$ |\n\n${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317298 1072 #uuid "53ed59d8-b22e-538d-9cc1-0f9210effe07" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317298 1074 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $g(x){f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x)+\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(2)={g}^{′}(2){f}^{-1}(2)+\\frac{g(2)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(2)=1,$ $g(2)=-1,$ ${g}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2},$ and ${f}^{′}(1)=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(2) & ={g}^{′}(2){f}^{-1}(2)+\\frac{g(2)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1+\\frac{(-1)}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{(-1)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317299 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316860 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317299 1069 "q-342597" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317299 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317299 1071 "Find ${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(0)$ of the product of functions, given the following table of values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $-3$ | $0$ | $3$ | $-2$ | $4$ |\n\n${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317299 1072 #uuid "7b7f3e30-5890-558d-bc83-f7644d982044" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317299 1074 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $g(x){f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x)+\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(0)={g}^{′}(0){f}^{-1}(0)+\\frac{g(0)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(0)=-3,$ $g(0)=1,$ ${g}^{′}(0)=\\frac{1}{3},$ and ${f}^{′}(-3)=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & ={g}^{′}(0){f}^{-1}(0)+\\frac{g(0)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-3)+\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-3)} \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317300 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316610 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317300 1069 "q-342599" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317300 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317300 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{2}-x.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain $x\\le -1,$ determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2).$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317300 1072 #uuid "9325902e-0c8e-582f-b933-0f5bb27d6720" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317300 1074 "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(2).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(2)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(2) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =2 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x & =2 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x-2 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the domain of $y=f(x)$ is $x\\le -1,$ we have ${f}^{-1}(2)=-1.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(-1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{2}-x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =2x-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =2(-1)-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-3)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317301 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316897 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317301 1069 "q-342604" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317301 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317301 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{x-2}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1).$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317301 1072 #uuid "b7a995a4-9609-5bde-99fc-2c8372501a9a" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317301 1074 "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(-1)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-1))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(-1).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(-1)=x.$ For $x\\ne 2,$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(-1) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{x}{x-2} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{x}{x-2}\\cdot (x-2) & =-1\\cdot (x-2) \\\\ \\frac{x}{\\cancel{(x-2)}}\\cdot \\cancel{(x-2)} & =-x+2 \\\\ x & =-x+2 \\\\ 2x & =2 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(-1)=1.$\n\nNow we calculate ${f}^{′}(1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x}{x-2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{2}{(x-2)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =-\\frac{2}{{(1-2)}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(-1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317302 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316936 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317302 1069 "q-342609" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317302 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317302 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x-2}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain $0\\le x<2,$ determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1).$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317302 1072 #uuid "a202bd7c-eb36-5eca-b211-a45493220604" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317302 1074 "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(-1)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-1))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(-1).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(-1)=x.$ For $x\\ne 2,$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(-1) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{x-2} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{x-2}\\cdot (x-2) & =-1\\cdot (x-2) \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\cancel{(x-2)}}\\cdot \\cancel{(x-2)} & =-x+2 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-x+2 \\\\ {x}^{2}+x-2 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the domain of $y=f(x)$ is $0\\le x<2,$ we have ${f}^{-1}(-1)=1.$\n\nNow we calculate ${f}^{′}(1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x-2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-4x}{(x-2)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{{1}^{2}-4(1)}{(1-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3)}{(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(-1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-3)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317303 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316687 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317303 1069 "q-342621" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317303 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317303 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=x-\\sqrt{\\frac{x+1}{3}}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain $x\\ge 1,$ determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1).$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317303 1072 #uuid "7fcade81-b4f1-5d26-9590-daf109d84f11" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317303 1074 "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(1).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(1)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(1) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =1 \\\\ x-\\sqrt{\\frac{x+1}{3}} & =1 \\\\ \\sqrt{\\frac{x+1}{3}} & =x-1 \\\\ \\frac{x+1}{3} & =(x-1)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{x+1}{3} & ={x}^{2}-2x+1 \\\\ x+1 & =3({x}^{2}-2x+1) \\\\ x+1 & =3{x}^{2}-6x+3 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-7x+2 & =0 \\\\ (3x-1)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3},2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the domain of $y=f(x)$ is $x\\ge 1,$ we have ${f}^{-1}(1)=2.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =x-\\sqrt{\\frac{x+1}{3}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =1-\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{\\frac{x+1}{3}}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =1-\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{\\frac{2+1}{3}}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =1-\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{1}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =1-\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{5}{6})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317304 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316790 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317304 1069 "q-342624" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317304 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317304 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)={5}^{x}+3.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(4).$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(4)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317304 1072 #uuid "358b887e-1fe5-5e35-a258-e961b7a5006e" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317304 1074 "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(4),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(4).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(4)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(4) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =4 \\\\ {5}^{x}+3 & =4 \\\\ {5}^{x} & =1 \\\\ {5}^{x} & ={5}^{0} \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(4)=0.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={5}^{x}+3 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={5}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & ={5}^{0}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317305 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316855 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317305 1069 "q-342625" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317305 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317305 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=(x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1).$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0).$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317305 1072 #uuid "94b649da-ce0f-5be9-8f87-0b0ee7645cbe" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317305 1074 "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(0).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(0)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(0) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =0 \\\\ (x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(0)=2.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1) \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)+\\frac{x+3}{x-1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-1)+\\frac{2+3}{2-1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =0+5 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317306 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317055 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317306 1069 "q-342626" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317306 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317306 1071 "Find $L{u(t-4){e}^{-5(t-4)}},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317306 1072 #uuid "9edef6a3-23e3-5edc-92b1-2a6d0f3a2111" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317306 1074 "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0,$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)}={e}^{-as}F(s),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=L{f(t)}.$\n\nIn our case, we wish to find the transform of\n\n$$\nu(t-4){e}^{-5(t-4)}.\n$$\n\nBy comparing this expression to the form in the theorem, we can identify the following:\n\n- The shift is $a=4.$\n\n- The shifted function is $f(t-4)={e}^{-5(t-4)}.$\n\nFrom the shifted function, we can deduce the original unshifted function $f(t)$ by simply replacing $(t-4)$ with $t:$\n\n$$\nf(t)={e}^{-5t}.\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the Laplace transform $F(s)$ of the unshifted function:\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{{e}^{-5t}}=\\frac{1}{s-(-5)}=\\frac{1}{s+5}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the shifting theorem formula $L{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)}={e}^{-as}F(s)$ by multiplying $F(s)$ by the exponential delay factor ${e}^{-4s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-4){e}^{-5(t-4)}} & ={e}^{-4s}\\cdot F(s) \\\\  & ={e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317307 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317149 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317307 1069 "q-342630" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317307 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317307 1071 "Given that $f(x)={({x}^{2}+2x+1)}^{2},$ the slope at the point $(0,1)$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317307 1072 #uuid "f5911b4d-cb67-58a7-a1eb-0368289d94d7" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317307 1074 "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\nFirst, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf={g}^{2},\\,g={x}^{2}+2x+1\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=2g,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=2x+2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =2g\\cdot (2x+2) \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}+2x+1)(2x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line at the point where $x=0$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & =[2({x}^{2}+2x+1)(2x+2){]}_{x=0} \\\\  & =2((0{)}^{2}+2\\cdot (0)+1)(2\\cdot (0)+2) \\\\  & =2(1)(2) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317308 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316798 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317308 1069 "q-342631" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317308 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317308 1071 "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $L{u(t-5)cos\\,(4(t-5))},$ where $u(t)$ is the unit step function." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317308 1072 #uuid "4aabd133-1479-5897-9bcf-d9c8574ba4ca" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317308 1074 "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0,$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)}={e}^{-as}F(s),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=L{f(t)}.$\n\nIn our case, we wish to find the transform of\n\n$$\nu(t-5)cos\\,(4(t-5)).\n$$\n\nBy comparing this expression to the form in the theorem, we can identify the following:\n\n- The shift is $a=5.$\n\n- The shifted function is $f(t-5)=cos\\,(4(t-5)).$\n\nFrom the shifted function, we can deduce the original unshifted function $f(t)$ by simply replacing $(t-5)$ with $t:$\n\n$$\nf(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the Laplace transform $F(s)$ of the unshifted function:\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the shifting theorem formula $L{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)}={e}^{-as}F(s)$ by multiplying $F(s)$ by the exponential delay factor ${e}^{-5s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-5)cos\\,(4(t-5))} & ={e}^{-5s}\\cdot F(s) \\\\  & ={e}^{-5s}\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317309 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316781 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317309 1069 "q-342637" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317309 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true