unction." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317310 1072 #uuid "1dab4207-8ade-526b-a882-976bc2574a3c" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317310 1074 "The second shifting theorem states that if $a\\ge 0,$ then\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)}={e}^{-as}F(s),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=L{f(t)}.$\n\nIn our case, we wish to find the transform of\n\n$$\nu(t-4)sin\\,(2(t-4)).\n$$\n\nBy comparing this expression to the form in the theorem, we can identify the following:\n\n- The shift is $a=4.$\n\n- The shifted function is $f(t-4)=sin\\,(2(t-4)).$\n\nFrom the shifted function, we can deduce the original unshifted function $f(t)$ by simply replacing $(t-4)$ with $t:$\n\n$$\nf(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the Laplace transform $F(s)$ of the unshifted function:\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}=\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the shifting theorem formula $L{u(t-a)\\,f(t-a)}={e}^{-as}F(s)$ by multiplying $F(s)$ by the exponential delay factor ${e}^{-4s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-4)sin\\,(2(t-4))} & ={e}^{-4s}\\cdot F(s) \\\\  & ={e}^{-4s}\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317311 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316793 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317311 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316913 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317311 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316916 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317311 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317069 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317311 1069 "q-342642" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317311 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317311 1071 "$$\nx(x-2)(x+1){y}^{″}+x(x+1){y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}y=0\n$$\n\nClassify the following points for the differential equation given above.$\\,$The point $x=1$ is\n{{field-1}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=-1$ is\n{{field-2}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=0$ is\n{{field-3}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=2$ is\n{{field-4}}\npoint of the differential equation." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317311 1072 #uuid "e89c8938-34f0-5cef-8c24-9fdf12ba3bd4" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317311 1074 "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nA singular point $x=a$ is regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $x(x-2)(x+1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(x-2)(x+1){y}^{″}+x(x+1){y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x-2}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}(x-2)(x+1)}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}(x-2)(x+1)}.\n$$\n\nThe function $P(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $x-2=0,$ which occurs at $x=2.$\n\nThe function $Q(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where ${x}^{3}(x-2)(x+1)=0,$ which occurs at $x=0,$ $x=2,$ and $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, $x=1$ is $\\text{an ordinary}$ point of the differential equation, and the singular points are\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,x=2,\\,x=-1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's classify each singular point.\n\n- For $x=0,$ the function\n\n$$\nxP(x)=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-2},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=0,$ while the function\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}Q(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}(x-2)(x+1)}=\\frac{1}{x(x-2)(x+1)},\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=0.$ Thus, $x=0$ is $\\text{an irregular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=2,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x-2)P(x)=(x-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-2}=1,\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=2,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}Q(x)=(x-2)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}(x-2)(x+1)}=\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{3}(x+1)},\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=2.$ Therefore, $x=2$ is $\\text{a regular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=-1,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x+1)P(x)=(x+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-2},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=-1,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x+1)^{2}Q(x)=(x+1)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}(x-2)(x+1)}=\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{3}(x-2)},\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=-1.$ Therefore, $x=-1$ is $\\text{a regular singular}$ point of the differential equation." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317312 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316668 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317312 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316966 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317312 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317103 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317312 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317132 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317312 1069 "q-342643" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317312 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317312 1071 "$$\nx(x-6)(x+2){y}^{″}+x(x-6){y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}y=0\n$$\n\nClassify the following points for the differential equation given above.$\\,$The point $x=3$ is\n{{field-1}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=0$ is\n{{field-2}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=-2$ is\n{{field-3}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=6$ is\n{{field-4}}\npoint of the differential equation." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317312 1072 #uuid "8d1c09a1-864e-5e53-b0d6-c9f8752f4484" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317312 1074 "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nA singular point $x=a$ is regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $x(x-6)(x+2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(x-6)(x+2){y}^{″}+x(x-6){y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x+2}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{x(x-6)(x+2)^{3}}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{x(x-6)(x+2)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nThe function $P(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $x+2=0,$ which occurs at $x=-2.$\n\nThe function $Q(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $x(x-6)(x+2)^{3}=0,$ which occurs at $x=0,$ $x=6,$ and $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, $x=3$ is $\\text{an ordinary}$ point of the differential equation, and the singular points are\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,x=-2,\\,x=6.\n$$\n\nNow, let's classify each singular point.\n\n- For $x=0,$ the function\n\n$$\nxP(x)=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+2},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=0,$ and the function\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}Q(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x-6)(x+2)^{3}}=\\frac{x}{(x-6)(x+2)^{3}},\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=0.$ Thus, $x=0$ is $\\text{a regular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=-2,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x+2)P(x)=(x+2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+2}=1,\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=-2,$ but the function\n\n$$\n(x+2)^{2}Q(x)=(x+2)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x-6)(x+2)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{x(x-6)(x+2)},\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=-2.$ Therefore, $x=-2$ is $\\text{an irregular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=6,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x-6)P(x)=(x-6)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+2},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=6,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x-6)^{2}Q(x)=(x-6)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x-6)(x+2)^{3}}=\\frac{x-6}{x(x+2)^{3}},\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=6.$ Therefore, $x=6$ is $\\text{a regular singular}$ point of the differential equation." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317313 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316649 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317313 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316796 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317313 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316891 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317313 1068 #ed