n\nNow, let's classify each singular point.\n\n- For $x=-1,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x+1)P(x)=(x+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x+1)}=\\frac{1}{x},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=-1,$ but the function\n\n$$\n(x+1)^{2}Q(x)=(x+1)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x-9},\n$$\n\nis undefined about $x=-1,$ so is *not* analytic about $x=-1.$ Thus, $x=-1$ is $\\text{an irregular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=0,$ the function\n\n$$\nxP(x)=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x+1)}=\\frac{1}{x+1},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=0,$ but the function\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}Q(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x-9},\n$$\n\nis undefined about $x=0.$ So, $x=0$ is $\\text{an irregular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=9,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x-9)P(x)=(x-9)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x+1)},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=9,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x-9)^{2}Q(x)=(x-9)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x-9}=(x-9)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=9.$ Therefore, $x=9$ is $\\text{a regular singular}$ point of the differential equation." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317314 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316711 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317314 1069 "q-342663" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317314 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317314 1071 "Given that $f(x)={(3x+1)}^{4},$ the slope at the point $(-1,16)$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317314 1072 #uuid "4d9eda9c-9861-5891-97dc-ba4b870f7db4" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317314 1074 "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use chain rule to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\nFirst, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf={g}^{4},\\,g=3x+1\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=4{g}^{3},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=3.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =4{g}^{3}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =12(3x+1{)}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line at the point where $x=-1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-1} & =[12(3x+1{)}^{3}{]}_{x=-1} \\\\  & =12(3\\cdot (-1)+1{)}^{3} \\\\  & =12(-2{)}^{3} \\\\  & =-96.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317315 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316655 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317315 1069 "q-342669" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317315 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317315 1071 "Evaluate $tanh\\,(-\\frac{3}{4}).$ **Do not round** your answer.\n\n$tanh\\,(-\\frac{3}{4})=$\n\n| {{field-1}} |\n| --- |\n| $1+{e}^{3/2}$ |" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317315 1072 #uuid "04f9767b-c101-53fd-a28d-9a00078c88b2" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317315 1074 "The definition of $\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x & =\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}-1}{{e}^{2x}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-2x}}{1+{e}^{-2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the third definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tanh\\,(-\\frac{3}{4}) & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-2(-3/4)}}{1+{e}^{-2(-3/4)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{3/2}}{1+{e}^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317316 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317020 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317316 1069 "q-342671" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317316 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317316 1071 "Evaluate $\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4).$ **Do not round** your answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡