7592186045585 true] [17592186317321 1074 "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={x}^{2}+1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=1,$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2(1) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\cdot 2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317322 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317027 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317322 1069 "q-342707" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317322 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317322 1071 "Given that $r=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta =\\theta (t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{3}$ at the point where $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}$ at that point.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317322 1072 #uuid "f9bc16a4-340b-5d92-b113-ebdd1b46f211" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317322 1074 "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(r) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\,(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot [-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})] \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (-2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317323 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316717 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317323 1069 "q-34271" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317323 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317323 1071 "The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317323 1072 #uuid "62d9b5f5-3d93-555f-bd13-2fa9aa2fe12b" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317323 1074 "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, we notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${x}_{2}=1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317324 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316637 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317324 1069 "q-342736" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317324 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317324 1071 "A particle $P$ is moving along the curve $y=\\sqrt{x+1}$ such that the $x$-coordinate of $P$ is changing at the rate of $-4\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$ Find the rate of change of the $y$-coordinate when $x=3\\,\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nThe $y$-coordinate is changing at the rate of \n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317324 1072 #uuid "2acdbf14-4b63-5d15-b86b-12678fa3bbae" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317324 1074 "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{x+1} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{x+1}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x+1}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-4\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}$ and $x=3$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3+1}} \\\\  & =-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $y$-coordinate is changing at the rate of $-1\\text{ }\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317325 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317023 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317325 1069 "q-342740" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317325 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317325 1071 "A particle $P$ is moving along the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+{x}^{2}$ such that the $x$-coordinate of $P$ is changing at the rate of $-4\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$ Find the rate of change of the $y$-coordinate when $x=3\\,\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nThe $y$-coordinate is changing at the rate of \n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317325 1072 #uuid "94b3a7f9-6cc0-5ccb-aca0-c8164b3cf667" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317325 1074 "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+{x}^{2}] \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+{x}^{2}] \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{x+1}+2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-4\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}$ and $x=3$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-4\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3+1}+2(3)) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}+6) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot \\frac{25}{4} \\\\  & =-25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $y$-coordinate is changing at the rate of $-25\\text{ }\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317326 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316633 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317326 1069 "q-34276" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317326 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317326 1071 "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has only the trivial solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent.\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317326 1072 #uuid "1120ac93-1146-5212-b323-41cae9ac4014" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317326 1074 "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, while ${x}_{3}$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statements I and II are false, while statement III is true." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317327 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317031 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317327 1069 "q-342764" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317327 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317327 1071 "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5x+1}}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317327 1072 #uuid "65a56fb3-c803-55e2-851b-d52f4cc805e8" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317327 1074 "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=5x+1.$ Then $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}={u}^{-1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{(5x+1)^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317328 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317045 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317328 1069 "q-342772" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317328 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317328 1071 "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L{y(t)}$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=5{u}_{\\pi /4}(t)\\cdot cos\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})\\,,\\,y(0)=-3,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{\\pi /4}(t)=u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou may make use of the following result:\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0\n$$\n\n$Y(s)=-\\frac{3}{s+2}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-1}},$\\,s>0$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317328 1072 #uuid "87eb3505-70a1-5321-b696-f100dbab50cb" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317328 1074 "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}+2y} & =L{5{u}_{\\pi /4}(t)\\cdot cos\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})} \\\\ L{{y}^{′}}+2L{y} & =L{5{u}_{\\pi /4}(t)\\cdot cos\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L{{y}^{′}},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=-3:$\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{′}}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)-(-3)=sY(s)+3\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n5{u}_{\\pi /4}(t)\\cdot cos\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $f(t)=5cos\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$ This can be interpreted as time-delayed sinusoidal forcing.\n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)}={e}^{-as}\\cdot L{f(t+a)}.\n$$\n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{5u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot cos\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})} & ={e}^{-(\\pi /4)s}\\cdot L{5cos\\,(3\\,(t+\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi s/4}\\cdot L{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi s/4}\\cdot 5\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\  & =\\frac{5s{e}^{-\\pi s/4}}{{s}^{2}+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}}+2L{y} & =L{5{u}_{\\pi /4}(t)cos\\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})} \\\\ (sY(s)+3)+2Y(s) & =\\frac{5s{e}^{-\\pi s/4}}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\ (s+2)Y(s)+3 & =\\frac{5s{e}^{-\\pi s/4}}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\ (s+2)Y(s) & =-3+\\frac{5s{e}^{-\\pi s/4}}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\ Y(s) & =-\\frac{3}{s+2}+\\frac{5s{e}^{-\\pi s/4}}{({s}^{2}+9)(s+2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317329 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316599 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317329 1069 "q-34278" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317329 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317329 1071 "Consider the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by arc length $s.$ Find the curvature at the point where $s=5\\pi $, if the unit tangent vector of the curve is given by\n\n \n\n$$\nT(s)=\\frac{4}{5}\\,i-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3s}{5})\\,j+\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3s}{5})\\,k.\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317329 1072 #uuid "3a5173f3-affb-5348-89f6-746440382607" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317329 1074 "Since we are given that the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and evaluate it at $s=5\\pi $: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{4}{5})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3s}{5}))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3s}{5}))\\,k \\\\  & =(0)\\,i-\\frac{9}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3s}{5})\\,j-\\frac{9}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3s}{5})\\,k \\\\ {T}^{′}(5\\pi ) & =-\\frac{9}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\cdot (5\\pi )}{5})\\,j-\\frac{9}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\cdot (5\\pi )}{5})\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi )\\,j-\\frac{9}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{25}\\cdot (-1)\\,j-\\frac{9}{25}\\cdot (0)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we obtain the curvature:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(5\\pi ) & =‖{T}^{′}(5\\pi )‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}\\sqrt{{(0)}^{2}+{(1)}^{2}+{(0)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317330 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316887 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317330 1069 "q-342780" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317330 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317330 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+3y={u}_{3}(t)\\cdot (t-3)\\,,\\,y(0)=-2,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{3}(t)=u(t-3)$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L{y(t)}$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)=-\\frac{2}{s+3}+\\frac{{e}^{-3s}}{{s}^{2}(s+3)}\n$$\n\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t)=-2{e}^{-3t}+u(t-3)\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(t).$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317330 1072 #uuid "7d571581-f4a8-5a66-b8eb-3c5c0101beb0" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317330 1074 "To find the function $g(t),$ we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the term containing the time delay in $Y(s).$\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential ${e}^{-3s}$ (which corresponds to the step function in the time domain) and identify the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}(s+3)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains a linear factor $(s+3)$ and a repeated linear factor ${s}^{2},$ the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}(s+3)}=\\frac{A}{s}+\\frac{B}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{C}{s+3}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n1=As(s+3)+B(s+3)+C{s}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A,B,$ and $C.$\n\n- Setting $s=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(3)+C(0)\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(0)+C(9)\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n0=A+C\\,⟹\\,A=-C=-\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=-\\frac{1}{9s}+\\frac{1}{3{s}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{9(s+3)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}{F(s)}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & ={L}^{-1}{-\\frac{1}{9s}+\\frac{1}{3{s}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{9(s+3)}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{t}{3}+\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-3s}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}{{e}^{-as}F(s)}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=3.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-3)f(t-3) & =u(t-3)(-\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{t-3}{3}+\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}) \\\\  & =u(t-3)(\\frac{3t-10}{9}+\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement $y(t)=-2{e}^{-3t}+u(t-3)\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)=\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}+\\frac{3t-10}{9}.\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317331 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316719 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317331 1069 "q-3428" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317331 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317331 1071 "The equation ${a}^{2}+8a=9$ can be written in the form $(a+p{)}^{2}=q.$ Find $\\,p+q,$ and solve the equation." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317331 1072 #uuid "697fde48-49f0-583d-9cb4-d2913bde926c" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317331 1074 "First, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}+8a & =9 \\\\ {a}^{2}+2\\cdot a\\cdot 4 & =9 \\\\ {a}^{2}+2\\cdot a\\cdot 4+{4}^{2}-{4}^{2} & =9 \\\\ ({a}^{2}+2\\cdot a\\cdot 4+{4}^{2})-{4}^{2} & =9 \\\\ ({a}^{2}+2\\cdot a\\cdot 4+{4}^{2})-16 & =9 \\\\ (a+4{)}^{2}-16 & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a+4{)}^{2}-16 & =9 \\\\ (a+4{)}^{2} & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=4,q=25,$ and $p+q=29.$\n\nNow, we solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a+4{)}^{2} & =25 \\\\ \\sqrt{(a+4{)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\ a+4 & =\\pm 5 \\\\ a & =-4\\pm 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =-4+5=1 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =-4-5=-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $a=1$ and $a=-9.$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317332 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316734 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317332 1069 "q-342802" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317332 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317332 1071 "The slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3{x}^{2}+12x}}$ at the point where $x=2$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317332 1072 #uuid "febc6c25-71ff-51d9-b851-6f19e65ebcd1" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317332 1074 "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=3{x}^{2}+12x.$ Then $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}={u}^{-1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6x+12.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\cdot (6x+12) \\\\  & =-\\frac{6x+12}{2\\sqrt{(3{x}^{2}+12x{)}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=2} & ={(-\\frac{6x+12}{2\\sqrt{(3{x}^{2}+12x{)}^{3}}})|}_{x=2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6(2)+12}{2\\sqrt{(3(2{)}^{2}+12(2){)}^{3}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{24}{2\\sqrt{{36}^{3}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317333 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316784 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317333 1069 "q-342804" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317333 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317333 1071 "Given that $f(x)={(4x+3)}^{5},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317333 1072 #uuid "9ebc59fe-5ccc-59b1-9757-9c760459c2ae" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317333 1074 "Let $g(x)=4x+3$ and $f(g(x))=g(x{)}^{5}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{5}{)}^{′}=5{g}^{4}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(4x+3{)}^{′}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =5{g}^{4}\\cdot (4) \\\\  & =20(4x+3{)}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317334 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317073 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317334 1069 "q-342812" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317334 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317334 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y={u}_{2}(t)\\cdot {e}^{-4(t-2)}\\,,\\,y(0)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{2}(t)=u(t-2)$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L{y(t)}$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{(s+3)(s+4)}\n$$\n\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t)=u(t-2)\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(t).$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317334 1072 #uuid "93383a11-c47b-5b60-8ef5-6388678ce390" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317334 1074 "To solve the initial value problem, we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of $Y(s).$\n\nTo find the inverse transform, we first ignore the exponential term ${e}^{-2s}$ (which corresponds to a time shift) and focus on the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{(s+3)(s+4)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains distinct linear factors, the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(s+3)(s+4)}=\\frac{A}{s+3}+\\frac{B}{s+4}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n1=A(s+4)+B(s+3).\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Setting $s=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(1)+B(0)\\,⟹\\,A=1.\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(-1)\\,⟹\\,B=-1.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{s+3}-\\frac{1}{s+4}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}{F(s)}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+3}-\\frac{1}{s+4}} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3t}-{e}^{-4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-2s}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}{{e}^{-as}F(s)}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=2.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-2)f(t-2) & =u(t-2)[{e}^{-3(t-2)}-{e}^{-4(t-2)}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the form given in the problem statement $y(t)=u(t-2)\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)={e}^{-3(t-2)}-{e}^{-4(t-2)}.\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317335 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316692 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317335 1069 "q-342814" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317335 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317335 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-y={u}_{3}(t)\\cdot {e}^{-2(t-3)}\\,,\\,y(0)=2,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{3}(t)=u(t-3)$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L{y(t)}$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{2}{s-1}+\\frac{{e}^{-3s}}{(s-1)(s+2)}\n$$\n\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t)=2{e}^{t}+\\frac{1}{3}u(t-3)\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(t).$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317335 1072 #uuid "350fb974-2dc7-5612-b740-6ce1b236d228" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317335 1074 "To find the function $g(t),$ we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the term containing the time delay in $Y(s).$\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential ${e}^{-3s}$ (which corresponds to the step function in the time domain) and identify the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{(s-1)(s+2)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains distinct linear factors, the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(s-1)(s+2)}=\\frac{A}{s-1}+\\frac{B}{s+2}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n1=A(s+2)+B(s-1).\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Setting $s=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(3)+B(0)\\,⟹\\,A=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(-3)\\,⟹\\,B=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{1}{s-1}-\\frac{1}{s+2}).\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}{F(s)}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\frac{1}{3}({L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-1}}-{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({e}^{t}-{e}^{-2t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-3s}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}{{e}^{-as}F(s)}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=3.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-3)f(t-3) & =\\frac{1}{3}u(t-3)\\cdot [{e}^{(t-3)}-{e}^{-2(t-3)}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement $y(t)=2{e}^{t}+\\frac{1}{3}u(t-3)\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)={e}^{(t-3)}-{e}^{-2(t-3)}.\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317336 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316829 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317336 1069 "q-342815" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317336 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317336 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y={u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\cdot cos\\,(2t-\\pi )\\,,\\,y(0)=-2,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{\\pi /2}(t)=u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L{y(t)}$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)=-\\frac{2}{s+2}+\\frac{s\\,{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{({s}^{2}+4)(s+2)}\n$$\n\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t)=-2{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{1}{4}u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(t).$ \n\nHint: You may make use of the following results:\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0.\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317336 1072 #uuid "01fff398-2b6d-506d-9fb7-5344ae756247" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317336 1074 "To find the function $g(t),$ we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the term containing the time delay in $Y(s).$\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential ${e}^{-\\pi s/2}$ (which corresponds to the step function in the time domain) and identify the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{s}{({s}^{2}+4)(s+2)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains a linear factor $(s+2)$ and an irreducible quadratic factor $({s}^{2}+4),$ the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{s}{({s}^{2}+4)(s+2)}=\\frac{A}{s+2}+\\frac{Bs+C}{{s}^{2}+4}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\ns=A({s}^{2}+4)+(Bs+C)(s+2).\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A,B,$ and $C.$\n\n- Setting $s=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n-2=A((-2)^{2}+4)+0\\,⟹\\,8A=-2\\,⟹\\,A=-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n0=A+B\\,⟹\\,B=-A=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the constant terms, we have\n\n$$\n0=4A+2C\\,⟹\\,0=4\\,(-\\frac{1}{4})+2C\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{4}(-\\frac{1}{s+2}+\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}+\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}).\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}{F(s)}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\frac{1}{4}(-{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+2}}+{L}^{-1}{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}}+{L}^{-1}{\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(-{e}^{-2t}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-\\pi s/2}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}{{e}^{-as}F(s)}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})f\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}[-{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /2)}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the trigonometric identities $cos\\,(2\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$ and $sin\\,(2\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),$ we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}(-{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /2)}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\,u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})({e}^{-2(t-\\pi /2)}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement $y(t)=-2{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{1}{4}u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)={e}^{-2(t-\\pi /2)}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317337 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316979 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317337 1069 "q-342851" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317337 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317337 1071 "Find ${g}^{″}(1)$ if $g(x)=-{x}^{3}+4x\\sqrt{x}.$\n\n${g}^{″}(1)=$ \n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317337 1072 #uuid "6ad0715e-8443-526b-9290-e5e4f91ac239" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317337 1074 "Computing the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & ={(-{x}^{3}+4x\\sqrt{x})}^{′} \\\\  & ={(-{x}^{3}+4{x}^{3/2})}^{′} \\\\  & =3(-{x}^{2})+\\frac{3}{2}(4{x}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =-3{x}^{2}+6{x}^{1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(x) & ={(-3{x}^{2}+6{x}^{1/2})}^{′} \\\\  & =2(-3x)+\\frac{1}{2}(6{x}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =-6x+3{x}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =-6x+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the second derivative at $x=1$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(1) & =-6(1)+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{1}} \\\\  & =-6+3 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317338 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316654 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317338 1069 "q-342871" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317338 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [