,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below to find the solution using Laplace transforms.\n\nLet $Y(s)=L{y(t)}.$ Taking the Laplace transform of both sides of the equation and solving for $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\nY(s)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nThe partial fraction decomposition of $Y$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)={{field-2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(t)={{field-3}}.\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317344 1072 #uuid "4bb7e88b-ffaf-5c1c-ba09-8748331a63e6" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317344 1074 "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides of the differential equation. Using the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{′}}+2L{y}=L{5{e}^{-3t}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the transform of a derivative, $L{{y}^{′}}=sY(s)-y(0),$ and the standard transform \n\n$$\nL{{e}^{at}}=\\frac{1}{s-a},\\,s>a,\n$$\n\nwe substitute the known values. Note that $y(0)=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(sY(s)-1)+2Y(s) & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+3} \\\\ (s+2)Y(s)-1 & =\\frac{5}{s+3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe rearrange to solve for $Y(s).$ First, add $1$ to the right-hand side and find a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(s+2)Y(s) & =1+\\frac{5}{s+3} \\\\ (s+2)Y(s) & =\\frac{s+3}{s+3}+\\frac{5}{s+3} \\\\ (s+2)Y(s) & =\\frac{s+8}{s+3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing by $(s+2),$ we obtain\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{s+8}{(s+2)(s+3)}.\n$$\n\nNext, we perform a partial fraction decomposition. We write $Y(s)$ in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{s+8}{(s+2)(s+3)}=\\frac{A}{s+2}+\\frac{B}{s+3}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants $A$ and $B,$ we first clear the denominators:\n\n$$\ns+8=A(s+3)+B(s+2)\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Setting $s=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n-2+8=A(-2+3)+B(0)\\,⟹\\,6=1A\\,⟹\\,A=6.\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n-3+8=A(0)+B(-3+2)\\,⟹\\,5=-1B\\,⟹\\,B=-5.\n$$\n\nThus, the decomposition is\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{6}{s+2}-\\frac{5}{s+3}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+2}} & ={e}^{-2t} \\\\ {L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+3}} & ={e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the inverse Laplace transform of $Y(s),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =6{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+2}}-5{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+3}} \\\\ y(t) & =6{e}^{-2t}-5{e}^{-3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317345 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316827 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317345 1069 "q-342927" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317345 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317345 1071 "Find ${y}^{′}$ when $y=\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-1)}.$\n\n| ${y}^{′}$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-1)}$ |" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317345 1072 #uuid "2c163105-12ab-5d59-bb79-bbc5b4799b09" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317345 1074 "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{u},\\,u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,v=2x-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡v)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-1)}}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-1))\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-1)}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-1)}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-1)}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317346 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316566 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317346 1068 #edb/