 correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-3,3)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | ${lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317349 1072 #uuid "33a3c9fc-2528-5adc-9b2e-4d84493c2477" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317349 1074 "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{false}.$ The function $F(x)$ is not continuous on $x\\in (-3,3)$ because $\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to {0}^{+}.$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/77/7736417b068541a1609efa46e9824cd3f65cd34856c1d2f5d0ec7258ec883b5c.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{false}.$ The function $F(x)$ is not piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3)$ because ${F}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{false}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}(2x+1)=2(-3)+1=-5\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{9}.\n$$\n\nSo, ${lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{false}$ since statements I and II are false.\n\n**Note:** The $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/ac/ac4d89fcccca99bebaeab4b44baac9c5c03651c38cef7e4d14cfe57978d066b7.png)" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317350 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316642 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317350 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316832 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317350 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317114 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317350 1069 "q-342961" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317350 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317350 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+2y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below to find the solution using Laplace transforms. You may make use of the following results:\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0.\n$$\n\nLet $Y(s)=L{y(t)}.$ Taking the Laplace transform of both sides of the equation and solving for $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\nY(s)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nThe partial fraction decomposition of $Y$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)={{field-2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(t)={{field-3}}.\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317350 1072 #uuid "7c31d5d2-d84b-5f0f-a8dc-d915106eb0d2" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317350 1074 "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides of the differential equation. Using the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{′}}+2L{y}=L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}+L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the transform of a derivative $L{{y}^{′}}=sY(s)-y(0)$ and the standard trigonometric transforms\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}=\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1},\n$$\n\nwe substitute the known values. Note that $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(sY(s)-0)+2Y(s) & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}+\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ (s+2)Y(s) & =\\frac{s+1}{{s}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe rearrange to solve for $Y(s).$ Dividing by $(s+2),$ we obtain\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{s+1}{(s+2)({s}^{2}+1)}.\n$$\n\nNext, we perform a partial fraction decomposition. Since the denominator has a linear factor $(s+2)$ and an irreducible quadratic factor $({s}^{2}+1),$ we write\n\n$$\n\\frac{s+1}{(s+2)({s}^{2}+1)}=\\frac{A}{s+2}+\\frac{Bs+C}{{s}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\ns+1=A({s}^{2}+1)+(Bs+C)(s+2)\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-2,$ we have\n \n\n$$\n-2+1=A((-2)^{2}+1)+0\\,⟹\\,-1=5A\\,⟹\\,A=-\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n  \n\n$$\n0=A+B\\,⟹\\,0=-\\frac{1}{5}+B\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the constant terms, we have\n \n\n$$\n1=A+2C\\,⟹\\,1=-\\frac{1}{5}+2C\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nThus, the decomposition is\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{5})}{s+2}+\\frac{(\\frac{1}{5}s+\\frac{3}{5})}{{s}^{2}+1}=-\\frac{1}{5(s+2)}+\\frac{s+3}{5({s}^{2}+1)}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+2}} & ={e}^{-2t} \\\\ {L}^{-1}{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1}} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ {L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the inverse Laplace transform of $Y(s),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =-\\frac{1}{5}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+2}}+\\frac{1}{5}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1}}+\\frac{3}{5}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}} \\\\ y(t) & =-\\frac{1}{5}{e}^{-2t}+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317351 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316700 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317351 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316759 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317351 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317148 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317351 1069 "q-342962" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317351 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317351 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+6y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below to find the solution using Laplace transforms. You may make use of the following results:\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0.\n$$\n\nLet $Y(s)=L{y(t)}.$