}+\\frac{s+9}{15({s}^{2}+9)}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+6}} & ={e}^{-6t} \\\\ {L}^{-1}{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9}} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t \\\\ {L}^{-1}{\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+9}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the inverse Laplace transform of $Y(s),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =-\\frac{1}{15}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+6}}+\\frac{1}{15}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9}}+\\frac{1}{5}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+9}} \\\\ y(t) & =-\\frac{1}{15}{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{1}{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317352 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316604 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317352 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316661 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317352 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316682 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317352 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316708 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317352 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316780 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317352 1069 "q-342963" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317352 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317352 1071 "Let $f(x)$ be the $6$-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=-3{x}^{2}+1$ defined on $[-3,3).$  Select the correct options in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic extension of $G(x)=-6x$ from the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$The Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n-3{x}^{2}+1∼-8+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{108(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is \n{{field-2}}\nand\n{{field-3}}\non $R,$ we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n\n$$\n-6x∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{{field-4}}\\cdot {{field-5}}.\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317352 1072 #uuid "7127629f-cc39-5e9d-b607-fa5f8e083997" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317352 1074 "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=3$ and we are already given the coefficients ${a}_{0}$ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-8+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{108(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that \n\n- the function $F$ is continuously differentiable on $(-3,3),$\n\n- continuous on $[-3,3),$ and\n\n- satisfies $F(-3)=F(3),$ if we extend the value at $x=3$ using the formula $F(x)=-3{x}^{2}+1.$\n\nTherefore, the $6$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{continuous}$ and $\\text{piecewise smooth}$ on $R,$ so we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-3{x}^{2}+1) & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-8)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{108(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\ -6x & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{108(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\ -6x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{-36(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\ -6x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{36(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317353 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316735 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317353 1069 "q-342966" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317353 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317353 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ for $y=6\\sqrt{x}+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n$\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=$ \n{{field-1}}" 1319413953