d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{x}^{4}+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2{x}^{-3}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 5{x}^{3}+3(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+(-3)\\cdot 2{x}^{-4} \\\\  & =20{x}^{3}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317355 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317150 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317355 1069 "q-34300" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317355 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317355 1071 "![](images/cc/ccb3b1dc1f175f6f018cb10e47db43a2ac5b4377c565dec4ee389b2a80455203.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}$ or ${R}^{3}?$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317355 1072 #uuid "113a385d-0512-508f-a50c-e45bc26c3c1f" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317355 1074 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram I is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram II is a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ lies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317356 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316878 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317356 1069 "q-343050" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317356 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317356 1071 "Calculate ${f}^{′}(x)$ for $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\,$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317356 1072 #uuid "63f7bfdc-41d8-518f-af80-7426cf484c38" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317356 1074 "The formula for the derivative of arccosine is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorn