me{cos}⁡2t}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the transform of a derivative $L{{y}^{′}}=sY(s)-y(0)$ and the standard trigonometric transforms\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}=\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4},\n$$\n\nwe substitute the known values. Note that $y(0)=-2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(sY(s)-(-2))+3Y(s) & =2(\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4})-3(\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}) \\\\ (s+3)Y(s)+2 & =\\frac{4-3s}{{s}^{2}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe rearrange to solve for $Y(s).$ First, subtract $2$ from the right-hand side and find a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(s+3)Y(s) & =\\frac{4-3s}{{s}^{2}+4}-2 \\\\ (s+3)Y(s) & =\\frac{4-3s}{{s}^{2}+4}-\\frac{2({s}^{2}+4)}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\ (s+3)Y(s) & =\\frac{-2{s}^{2}-3s-4}{{s}^{2}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing by $(s+3),$ we obtain\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{-2{s}^{2}-3s-4}{(s+3)({s}^{2}+4)}.\n$$\n\nNext, we perform a partial fraction decomposition. Since the denominator has a linear factor $(s+3)$ and an irreducible quadratic factor $({s}^{2}+4),$ we write\n\n$$\n\\frac{-2{s}^{2}-3s-4}{(s+3)({s}^{2}+4)}=\\frac{A}{s+3}+\\frac{Bs+C}{{s}^{2}+4}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n-2{s}^{2}-3s-4=A({s}^{2}+4)+(Bs+C)(s+3)\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-3,$ we have\n \n\n$$\n-2(-3)^{2}-3(-3)-4=A((-3)^{2}+4)+0\\,⟹\\,-13=13A\\,⟹\\,A=-1.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n  \n\n$$\n-2=A+B\\,⟹\\,-2=-1+B\\,⟹\\,B=-1.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the constant terms, we have\n \n\n$$\n-4=4A+3C\\,⟹\\,-4=4(-1)+3C\\,⟹\\,C=0.\n$$\n\nThus, the decomposition is\n\n$$\nY(s)=-\\frac{1}{s+3}+\\frac{-s}{{s}^{2}+4}=-\\frac{1}{s+3}-\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+3}} & ={e}^{-3t} \\\\ {L}^{-1}{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the inverse Laplace transform of $Y(s),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =-{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+3}}-{L}^{-1}{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}} \\\\ y(t) & =-{e}^{-3t}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317358 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317108 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317358 1069 "q-343075" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317358 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317358 1071 "Given $g(x)=2{x}^{5}-3{x}^{3},$ we have\n\n${g}^{‴}(x)=$ \n{{field-1}}." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317358 1072 #uuid "f082c550-bc0d-5284-a89a-ca202b931026" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317358 1074 "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{5}-3{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =10{x}^{4}-9{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(10{x}^{4}-9{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =40{x}^{3}-18x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, computing the third derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(40{x}^{3}-18x) \\\\  & =120{x}^{2}-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317359 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316981 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317359 1069 "q-343079" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317359 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317359 1071 "Find the exact value of the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y=5\\sqrt{x}-10{x}^{3/4}$ at the point $(16,-60).$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317359 1072 #uuid "588f16ad-5b4e-55ce-b21e-9b01f70507db" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317359 1074 "This time, we'll use $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ notation. First, we compute the derivative, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5\\sqrt{x}-10{x}^{3/4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{x}^{1/2}-10{x}^{3/4}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2}-10\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}{x}^{-1/4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{-1/2}-\\frac{15}{2}{x}^{-1/4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=16$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=16} & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{15}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{25}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point $(16,-60)$ is\n\n$$\nm={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=16}=-\\frac{25}{8}.\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317360 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317105 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317360 1069 "q-343086" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317360 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317360 1071 "Given $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{5}-{x}^{2}+x}{4{x}^{2}},$ we have\n\n${f}^{‴}(x)=$ \n{{field-1}}." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317360 1072 #uuid "6406b0de-85c2-57d8-ad6d-cd0fcf72814c" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317360 1074 "First, notice that we can rewrite the function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{5}-{x}^{2}+x}{4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{4x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3x}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, computing the third derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3x}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{3}{2{x}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317361 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316755 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317361 1069 "q-343089" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317361 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317361 1071 "Given $f(x)=3{e}^{x}-\\frac{1}{3{x}^{2}},$ we have\n\n${f}^{‴}(x)=$ \n{{field-1}}." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317361 1072 #uuid "340cc804-6eed-5bf6-9664-2ced31609b52" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317361 1074 "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{e}^{x}-\\frac{1}{3{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{e}^{x}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{-2}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{x}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{e}^{x}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{-3}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{x}-2{x}^{-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, computing the third derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{e}^{x}-2{x}^{-4}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{x}+8{x}^{-5} \\\\  & =3{e}^{x}+\\frac{8}{{x}^{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317362 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316802 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317362 1069 "q-3431" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317362 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317362 1071 "The equation $2{z}^{2}+4z=10$  can be written in the form $(z+p)^{2}+q=0.$ Find $p,$ and solve the equation." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317362 1072 #uuid "de3b2a31-7290-5ee6-bd88-a474c5c4972e" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317362 1074 "First, we divide out by the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{z}^{2}+4z & =10 \\\\ 2({z}^{2}+2z) & =10 \\\\ {z}^{2}+2z & =5 \\\\ {z}^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot z & =5 \\\\ {z}^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot z+{1}^{2}-{1}^{2} & =5 \\\\ {(z+1)}^{2}-{1}^{2} & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(z+1)}^{2}-{1}^{2} & =5 \\\\ {(z+1)}^{2}-1 & =5 \\\\ {(z+1)}^{2}-6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, $p=1$ and $q=-6.$ \n\nNow, let's solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(z+1)^{2}-6 & =0 \\\\ (z+1)^{2} & =6 \\\\ \\sqrt{(z+1)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{6} \\\\ |z+1| & =\\sqrt{6} \\\\ z+1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{6} \\\\ z & =-1\\pm \\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317363 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316702 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317363 1069 "q-343110" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317363 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317363 1071 "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L{y(t)}$ for $s>2$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=5-2{e}^{-t},\\,y(0)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317363 1072 #uuid "7597fdf3-9405-5418-b0fa-60d2dd89b4dd" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317363 1074 "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}-2y} & =L{5-2{e}^{-t}} \\\\ L{{y}^{′}}-2L{y} & =5L{1}-2L{{e}^{-t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L{{y}^{′}},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=\\frac{1}{3}:$\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{′}}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)-\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}},\\,s>0,\\,n\\in {N}_{0} \\\\ L{{e}^{at}} & =\\frac{1}{s-a},\\,s>a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{1} & =\\frac{1}{s} \\\\ L{{e}^{-t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-(-1)}=\\frac{1}{s+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}}-2L{y} & =5L{1}-2L{{e}^{-t}} \\\\ (sY(s)-\\frac{1}{3})-2Y(s) & =\\frac{5}{s}-\\frac{2}{s+1} \\\\ (s-2)Y(s) & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{5}{s}-\\frac{2}{s+1} \\\\ (s-2)Y(s) & =\\frac{s(s+1)+15(s+1)-6s}{3s(s+1)} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{{s}^{2}+10s+15}{3s(s+1)(s-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317364 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316865 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317364 1069 "q-343114" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317364 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317364 1071 "Given $f(x)=2ln⁡x+{x}^{4},$ we have\n\n${f}^{‴}(x)=$ \n{{field-1}}." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317364 1072 #uuid "d9d7023a-3aa5-56f6-a910-25e31e021f84" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317364 1074 "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2ln⁡x+{x}^{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x}+4{x}^{3} \\\\  & =2{x}^{-1}+4{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{-1}+4{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =-2{x}^{-2}+12{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, computing the third derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2{x}^{-2}+12{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =4{x}^{-3}+24x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}+24x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317365 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316816 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317365 1069 "q-343120" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317365 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317365 1071 "Find ${g}^{(4)}(x)$ for $g(x)=sin⁡x-{e}^{x}.$\n\n${g}^{(4)}(x)=$ \n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317365 1072 #uuid "9f16160f-2d16-509d-a78c-972837e58147" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317365 1074 "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(sin⁡x-{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =cos⁡x-{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(cos⁡x-{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =-sin⁡x-{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the third derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-sin⁡x-{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =-cos⁡x-{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, computing the fourth derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{(4)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-cos⁡x-{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =sin⁡x-{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317366 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316877 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317366 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317101 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317366 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317153 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317366 1069 "q-343133" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317366 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317366 1071 "![](images/d8/d8fcc410e057fcfee15df2e182cdc1cf593c1b1fcf09029366e920fe48f17409.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is defined on $[-6,6].$\nThe graph of the second derivative $y={g}^{″}(x)$ of the function $g(x)$ intersects the $x$-axis at the points $x=-4,$ $x=1,$ and $x=5,$ as shown above. The intervals on which the function $g(x)$ is concave down are\n\n$(-6,$\n{{field-1}}$)\\,$\nand \n$\\,(${{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$).$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317366 1072 #uuid "bc8c80c0-3604-5258-a9b8-ea7e2b9df2be" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317366 1074 "A function $g(x)$ is concave down over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${g}^{″}(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y={g}^{″}(x),$ we can see that ${g}^{″}(x)<0$ on the intervals $(-6,-4)$ and $(1,5).$ \n\nTherefore, the function $g(x)$ is concave down on the intervals \n\n$$\n(-6,-4\\,)\\,\\text{and}\\,(\\,1,5\\,).\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317367 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317007 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317367 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317018 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317367 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317100 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317367 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317151 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317367 1069 "q-343146" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317367 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317367 1071 "![](images/19/19d9904c5236534ee6b4f176d760268df397f644d4ea7016d1d35c4b5db6e413.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is defined on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\nThe graph of the second derivative $y={g}^{″}(x)$ of the function $g(x)$ intersects the $x$-axis at the points $x=-4,$ $x=-2,$ $x=0,$ $x=2,$ and $x=4,$ as shown above. The intervals on which the function $g(x)$ is concave up are\n\n$(-\\infty ,$\n{{field-1}}$),\\,(${{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$),\\,(${{field-4}}$,4).$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317367 1072 #uuid "ced620c8-0174-5392-a65c-f8e34db07ced" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317367 1074 "A function $g(x)$ is concave up over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${g}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y={g}^{″}(x)$, we can see that ${g}^{″}(x)>0$ on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-4),$ $(-2,0),$ and $(2,4).$ \n\nTherefore, the function $g(x)$ is concave up on the intervals \n\n$$\n(\\,-\\infty ,-4\\,),\\,(\\,-2,0\\,),\\,(\\,2,4\\,).\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317368 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317012 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317368 1069 "q-343160" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317368 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317368 1071 "The number of views $V,$ in millions, of a viral video after $t$ days is modeled by $V(t)=\\frac{{10}^{t}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡10}$ for $0\\le t\\le 6.$ What is the instantaneous rate of change of the number of views as a function of $t?$\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change is\n{{field-1}} views per day." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317368 1072 #uuid "224166c5-b66f-5ed1-bc58-63e5ea30c6cd" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317368 1074 "The instantaneous rate of change of the number of views is equal to ${V}^{′}(t).$ \n\nFirst, we take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{10}^{t}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡10}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{10}^{t}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡10}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡10} \\\\  & ={10}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change of the number of views is ${10}^{t}.$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317369 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316969 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317369 1069 "q-343161" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317369 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317369 1071 "The function $C(t)=6{e}^{-2t}$ gives the concentration, in milligrams per liter, of a medication in the bloodstream $t$ hours after administration. Find the instantaneous rate of change of the concentration as a function of $t.$\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change of the concentration is\n{{field-1}}\nmilligrams per liter per hour." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317369 1072 #uuid "e15c65e8-81d8-5da3-b2ac-19e9e08d6d33" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317369 1074 "The instantaneous rate of change of the concentration is equal to ${C}^{′}(t).$\n\nNow, the rate of change of $C(t)$ with respect to $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{e}^{-2t}) \\\\  & =6(-2){e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =-12{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change of the concentration is $-12{e}^{-2t}$ milligrams per liter per hour." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317370 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316794 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317370 1069 "q-343166" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317370 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317370 1071 "The funding $F,$ in millions of dollars, allocated to a research project $t$ years after it begins is modeled by\n\n$$\nF(t)=5+(t+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1),\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nWhat is the instantaneous rate of change of the funding as a function of $t?$\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change of the funding is\n{{field-1}} million dollars per year." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317370 1072 #uuid "95b94717-7611-52a1-8e4e-d899c366fca7" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317370 1074 "The instantaneous rate of change of the funding is ${F}^{′}(t).$\n\nWe take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}[5+(t+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)] \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)+(t+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{t+1} \\\\  & =1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change of the funding is $1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)$ million dollars per year." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317371 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316770 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317371 1069 "q-343167" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317371 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317371 1071 "The inventory level $I,$ in hundreds of units, of a medical supply $t$ weeks after ordering begins, is modeled by\n\n$$\nI(t)={t}^{2}{e}^{-t}+12,\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nWhat is the instantaneous rate of change of the inventory level as a function of $t?$ \n\nThe instantaneous rate of change of the inventory level is\n{{field-1}} hundreds of units per week." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317371 1072 #uuid "e072cabd-89cb-5d04-9199-6008aaa8be64" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317371 1074 "The instantaneous rate of change of the inventory level is ${I}^{′}(t).$\n\nWe take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}[{t}^{2}{e}^{-t}+12] \\\\  & =2t{e}^{-t}+{t}^{2}\\cdot (-{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =2t{e}^{-t}-{t}^{2}{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =t{e}^{-t}(2-t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change of the inventory level is $t{e}^{-t}(2-t)$ hundreds of units per week." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317372 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317128 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317372 1069 "q-343169" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317372 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317372 1071 "Air is pumped into and released from a pressurized chamber. After $t$ minutes, the volume of air (in cubic meters) is modeled by\n\n$$\nA(t)=-6{t}^{2}+90t+400,\\,0\\le t\\le 15.\n$$\n\nFind the exact value of the instantaneous rate of change of the air volume with respect to time when $t=7$ minutes.\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change when $t=7$ minutes is\n{{field-1}} cubic meters per minute." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317372 1072 #uuid "443ebc4e-23e9-56de-b259-2cc9ef5670ff" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317372 1074 "The instantaneous rate of change of the air volume at $t=7$ minutes is ${A}^{′}(7).$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}\\,(-6{t}^{2}+90t+400) \\\\  & =-12t+90.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=7,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(7) & =-12(7)+90 \\\\  & =-84+90 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change of the air volume at $t=7$ minutes is $6$ cubic meters per minute." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317373 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317152 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317373 1069 "q-343173" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317373 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317373 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\frac{7}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6-x))=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317373 1072 #uuid "d1c4a7f6-dfa8-527f-a427-71639bc74bac" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317373 1074 "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6-x):$ \n\n![](images/04/04dd8257ef32291b930aa29f5bed4de2c41761692fd48d08ed2479f6cbd78659.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6-x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6-x)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\frac{7}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6-x))=\\frac{7}{\\pi }\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317374 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316881 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317374 1069 "q-343215" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317374 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317374 1071 "Find the exact value of the slope $m$ of the tangent line to the curve $y=2\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(1+x)$ at $x=-1.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317374 1072 #uuid "fff73ba7-51c3-55f6-9993-4fc2cbedd36a" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317374 1074 "The formula for the derivative of arccosine is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule and the formula above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[2\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(1+x)] \\\\  & =2(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(1+x)^{2}}})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1+x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{1-(1+2x+{x}^{2})}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{-2x-{x}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent line to the curve at $x=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{-2(-1)-(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317375 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316800 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317375 1069 "q-343223" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317375 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317375 1071 "Find ${y}^{′}$ for $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5{x}^{3}).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317375 1072 #uuid "d592f5e3-092e-5a54-a675-93bc9d6bbd25" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317375 1074 "The formula for the derivative of arctangent is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule and the formula above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5{x}^{3})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{(5{x}^{3})}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+25{x}^{6}}\\cdot 15{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{15{x}^{2}}{1+25{x}^{6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317376 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316772 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317376 1069 "q-343225" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317376 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317376 1071 "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }(10\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+3))=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317376 1072 #uuid "b99f3255-7a91-5700-9ad5-6321b83c8397" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317376 1074 "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+3):$ \n\n![](images/bb/bb49c052cabe12e8869270ab61b1de9e7f45b6acd6eaa9bc0d4c6892446a7cb6.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+3)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+3)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }(10\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+3))=(10\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})=5\\pi .\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317377 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316982 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317377 1069 "q-343232" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317377 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317377 1071 "![](images/6f/6f98744ddd2406853334d5025c5346f64f82499520def67e4efac20e5ae356e3.png)\n\nConsider the graph of a function $y=f(x),$ shown above. It consists of two line segments. Find the value of\n${\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n${\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317377 1072 #uuid "0a4bff44-0a51-5c27-922f-522b06b7d52b" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317377 1074 "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-3,0]$ and the line segment for $x\\in [0,3].$ Therefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,0].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+3)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,3].$ The corresponding region is also a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3+1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =6+6 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317378 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316744 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317378 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316917 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317378 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317015 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317378 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317017 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317378 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317046 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317378 1069 "q-343245" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317378 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317378 1071 "Let $f(x)$ be the $6$-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=-7{x}^{2}+4$ defined on $[-3,3).$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic extension of $G(x)=-14x$ from the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$The Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n-7{x}^{2}+4∼-17+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{252(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is \n{{field-2}}\nand\n{{field-3}}\non $R,$ we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n\n$$\n-14x∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{{field-4}}\\cdot {{field-5}}.\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317378 1072 #uuid "ce2d6842-c273-5f28-8b8b-7bdf00107d27" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317378 1074 "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=3$ and we are already given the coefficients ${a}_{0}$ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-17+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{252(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that \n\n- the function $F$ is continuously differentiable on $(-3,3),$\n\n- continuous on $[-3,3),$ and\n\n- satisfies $F(-3)=F(3),$ if we extend the value at $x=3$ using the formula $F(x)=-7{x}^{2}+4.$\n\nTherefore, the $6$-periodic extension $f$ 