72 #uuid "fe7adb62-e3df-5206-9031-d8f651922dd6" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317404 1074 "The instantaneous rate of change of the root depth when $t=6$ months is equal to ${D}^{′}(6).$ \n\nFirst, we take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{2t+13}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (2t+13)^{(1/2)-1}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(2t+13)^{-1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=6,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{′}(6) & =\\frac{1}{4}(2(6)+13)^{-1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{25}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change of the root depth when $t=6$ months is $\\frac{1}{20}$ meters per month." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317405 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316773 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317405 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316785 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317405 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316998 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317405 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317013 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317405 1069 "q-343421" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317405 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317405 1071 "Consider the function $F(x)=|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})|$ defined on $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ The Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{2}{\\pi }-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{\\pi (4{n}^{2}-1)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.At $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\cdot 0)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-2}}\nat $x=0,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(0)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4{n}^{2}-1}={{field-4}}.\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317405 1072 #uuid "e47b2683-e120-52c6-81fc-c52d398d6446" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317405 1074 "First, we rewrite our Fourier series as\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{2}{\\pi }-\\frac{4}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4{n}^{2}-1}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\cdot 0)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is $\\text{continuous}$ at $x=0,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(0)=|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{0}{2})|=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }-\\frac{4}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4{n}^{2}-1}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\cdot 0) \\\\ 0 & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }-\\frac{4}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4{n}^{2}-1}\\cdot 1 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\pi } & =-\\frac{4}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4{n}^{2}-1} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4{n}^{2}-1} & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317406 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316872 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317406 1069 "q-343424" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317406 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317406 1071 "The concentration $C$ (in millimoles per liter) of a chemical in a reactor $t$ minutes after the reaction begins is modeled by the function $C(t)={t}^{3}+25$ for $0\\le t\\le 10.$ Find the instantaneous rate at which the concentration is changing with respect to time at the moment when the concentration is $150$ millimoles per liter.\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change when the concentration is $150$ millimoles per liter is\n{{field-1}} millimoles per liter per minute." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317406 1072 #uuid "20befefa-749f-5edd-aa1b-af398dc92f4d" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317406 1074 "Since we are given that the chemical's concentration is $150$ millimoles per liter, the time can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(t) & =150 \\\\ {t}^{3}+25 & =150 \\\\ {t}^{3} & =125 \\\\ t & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe instantaneous rate at which the chemical's concentration is changing when $t=5$ minutes is equal to ${C}^{′}(5).$\n\nTaking the derivative, the rate of change of $C(t)$ with respect to $t$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}+25) \\\\  & =3{t}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand evaluating at $t=5$ gives\n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(5)=3(5)^{2}=75.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change of the chemical's concentration when its concentration is $150$ millimoles per liter is  $75$ millimoles per liter per minute." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317407 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316830 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317407 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316863 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317407 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317008 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317407 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317042 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317407 1069 "q-343429" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317407 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317407 1071 "$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}-1, & x\\in [-2,0] \\\\ 2, & x\\in (0,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. The Fourier series of the $4$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{1}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{\\pi (2n-1)}sin\\,(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.At $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\nsin\\,(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi }{2})={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-2}}\nat $x=1,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(1)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1}={{field-4}}.\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317407 1072 #uuid "0d801752-c780-5add-95cf-b3a0d0a59bd1" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317407 1074 "First, we rewrite our Fourier series as\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{6}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot sin\\,(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that at $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\nsin\\,(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi }{2})=sin\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi )=(-1)^{n+1}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is $\\text{continuous}$ at $x=1,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(1)=2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{6}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot sin\\,(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi \\cdot 1}{2}) \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{6}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot (-1)^{n+1} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & =\\frac{6}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1} & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317408 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316616 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317408 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316639 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317408 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316669 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317408 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316737 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317408 1069 "q-343443" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317408 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317408 1071 "$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}3, & x\\in [-\\pi ,0] \\\\ -5, & x\\in (0,\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. The Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-1-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{16}{\\pi (2n-1)}sin\\,((2n-1)x).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.At $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nsin\\,((2n-1)\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-2}}\nat $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1}={{field-4}}.\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317408 1072 #uuid "0b3adfef-3238-5b0a-8f95-7aed0a3d3235" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317408 1074 "First, we rewrite our Fourier series as\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-1-\\frac{16}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot sin\\,((2n-1)x).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that at $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nsin\\,((2n-1)\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))=sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-n\\pi )=(-1)^{n}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is $\\text{continuous}$ at $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=3.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-1-\\frac{16}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot sin\\,((2n-1)\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\ 3 & =-1-\\frac{16}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot (-1)^{n} \\\\ 4 & =-\\frac{16}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{2n-1} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{2n-1} & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1} & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317409 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316631 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317409 1069 "q-34346" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317409 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317409 1071 "The matrix $D$ and the vector $b$ are given below.\nDetermine if $b\\in \\text{Col}(D).$ \nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{4}$, where ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$, ${x}_{3}$, and ${x}_{4}$ are real numbers such that $b={x}_{1}{d}_{1}+{x}_{2}{d}_{2}+{x}_{3}{d}_{3}+{x}_{4}{d}_{4},$ and ${d}_{1}$, ${d}_{2}$, ${d}_{3}$, and ${d}_{4}$ are respective columns of $D.$\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317409 1072 #uuid "d1232415-f101-5f93-96ed-3610c46ba358" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317409 1074 "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(D),$ we need to check if the equation $Dx=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[D\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[D\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 4 & 7 & 11 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 6 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 & -1 & 7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 4 & 7 & 11 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 & -1 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}+7{x}_{4}=11 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{3}-{x}_{4}=-2 \\\\ 0=9 \\\\ {x}_{1}+3{x}_{3}-{x}_{4}=7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get a false statement $0=9.$ Therefore, the system is inconsistent, and we conclude that $b\\notin \\text{Col}(D).$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317410 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316778 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317410 1069 "q-343468" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317410 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317410 1071 "![](images/48/48fc0e80cf6e2ff5a83a659a53afc05ce8b7343edd4377b8cb935d1abb480514.png)\n\nConsider the graph of the function $f(x),$ shown above. It consists of three line segments. Find the value of\n${\\int }_{1}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317410 1072 #uuid "a02c6800-14f8-5f18-99b0-c98ce90bf7ce" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317410 1074 "The graph consists of $3$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [1,3],$ the line segment for $x\\in [3,5],$ and the line segment for $x\\in [5,6].$ Therefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{5}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThe $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,3].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3+1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [3,5].$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =lw \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [5,6].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+4)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =4+2+\\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317411 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316605 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317411 1069 "q-343474" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317411 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317411 1071 "![](images/29/29c0f5b8a290434e8c20f652beb73b245192c6e122f16a28f45b2029bde53a6d.png)\n\nConsider the graph of the function $y=f(x),$ shown above. It consists of five line segments. Find the value of\n${\\int }_{-5}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n${\\int }_{-5}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317411 1072 #uuid "5b11317d-29d7-577a-9a37-d2301d03ad08" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317411 1074 "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the three line segments for $x\\in [-5,0]$ and the two line segments for $x\\in [0,5].$ Therefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-5}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-5,0].$\nThe corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2+5)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,5].$\nThe corresponding region is also a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2+5)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2} \\\\  & =7-7 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317412 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317057 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317412 1069 "q-343488" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317412 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317412 1071 "The bases of a trapezoid are increasing and the larger base is always three times the smaller base. The height of the trapezoid is $3\\,cm.$ Find the rate of change of the area of the trapezoid with respect to its smaller base $b.$\n\nThe rate of change of the area of the trapezoid with respect to its smaller base is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317412 1072 #uuid "8639ec62-62bc-5949-a15f-43d061aee46a" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317412 1074 "The area of a trapezoid is given by \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{(B+b)h}{2},\n$$\n\n where $b$ is the smaller base, $B$ is the larger base and $h$ is the height of the trapezoid.\n\nWe are given that $B=3b$ and $h=3,$ so we can express the area $A$ as a function of the base $b$ by substituting $B=3b$ and $h=3$ in the formula above. We get\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{(b+3b)(3)}{2}=6b.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $A(b)$ with respect to $b$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(b) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}b}(6b) \\\\  & =6\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}b}(b) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317413 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316705 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317413 1069 "q-343491" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317413 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317413 1071 "![](images/c6/c63dfe563c07b143bdc7cc28ab4d1142bea8c455be31ce924fa310c5e4903305.png)\n\nThe area $A$ of the circular disk above is changing as the circle expands. The circumference and radius of the circle are $C$ and $r,$ respectively. \nFind the rate of change of the area of the circle with respect to its circumference.\n\nThe rate of change of the area of the circle with respect to its circumference $C$ is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317413 1072 #uuid "1381662a-cc6b-586a-8c7d-1d07a1ad3310" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317413 1074 "The area of a circle is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the circle.\n\nWe know that $C=2\\pi r,$ so we can express the area $A$ as a function of the circumference $C$ by substituting $r=\\frac{C}{2\\pi }$ into the formula above. We get\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {(\\frac{C}{2\\pi })}^{2}=\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $A(C)$ with respect to $C$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(C) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}({C}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\pi }(2C) \\\\  & =\\frac{C}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317414 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316598 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317414 1069 "q-343496" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317414 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317414 1071 "The lateral surface area $A$ of a cone is increasing. The radius of the base of the cone is $r,$ and the slant height of the cone is always twice its radius. Find the rate of change of the lateral surface area of the cone with respect to its radius.\n\nThe rate of change of the lateral surface area of the cone with respect to its radius $r$ is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317414 1072 #uuid "b0d9abc0-a1df-5565-bb5f-50d57c4f0d8e" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317414 1074 "The area of the lateral surface area of the cone is given by\n\n$$\nA=A(r)=\\pi rℓ,\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the base of the cone and $ℓ$ is its slant height.\n\nWe are given that $ℓ=2r,$ so we can express the area $A$ as a function of the radius $r$ by substituting $ℓ=2r$ into the formula above. We get\n\n$$\nA=\\pi r(2r)=2\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $A(r)$ with respect to its radius $r$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(2\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\pi (2r) \\\\  & =4\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317415 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316925 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317415 1069 "q-343498" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317415 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317415 1071 "The surface area $A$ of a sphere is increasing. Find the instantaneous rate at which the surface area of the sphere is changing with respect to its radius at the moment when the radius of the sphere is equal to $5\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change at the moment when its radius is $5$ inches is equal to\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{in}.$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317415 1072 #uuid "03945ef5-4c82-5edb-b5e2-dcda91f332d4" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317415 1074 "We know that the surface area of a sphere with radius $r$ is given by \n\n$$\nA=A(r)=4\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTaking the derivative, the rate of change of $A(r)$ with respect to $r$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(4\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\  & =4\\pi (2r) \\\\  & =8\\pi r,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand evaluating at $r=5$ gives\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(5)=8\\pi (5)=40\\pi .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate at which the surface area of the sphere is changing when its radius is $5\\,\\text{in}$, is $40\\pi $ ${\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{in}.$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317416 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316880 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317416 1069 "q-343502" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317416 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317416 1071 "![](images/5f/5fc1aff711355a147abac33a513434c55575c552ab58f0164ebc87222530f371.png)\n\nThe volume of a cube-shaped container is increasing. Find the instantaneous rate at which the volume of the container is changing with respect to the side length at the moment when the face diagonal of the cube is equal to $10\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change at the moment when the face diagonal is $10\\sqrt{2}$ centimeters is equal to\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{cm}.$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317416 1072 #uuid "1113df94-3130-5c2b-b9d2-2a93c2813bb6" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317416 1074 "Since we are given that the face diagonal of the cube is $10\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{cm}$, its side length $s$ is $10\\,\\text{cm}.$ Now, the volume of a cube with side length $s$ is given by \n\n$$\nV=V(s)={s}^{3}.\n$$\n\nTaking the derivative, the rate of change of $V(s)$ with respect to $s$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}({s}^{3}) \\\\  & =3{s}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand evaluating at $s=10$ gives\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(10)=3(10)^{2}=300.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate at which the volume of the container is increasing when the face diagonal is $10\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{cm}$ is $300\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{cm}.$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317417 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316885 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317417 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317146 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317417 1069 "q-343536" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317417 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317417 1071 "Given that $g(x)=3{x}^{4}-8{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}+1$ is defined for $x\\in (-8,8),$ find the values of $x$ for which the function is strictly decreasing.\n\n$x\\in (${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317417 1072 #uuid "6d68da28-980c-5baf-9e24-524d3a71b6c8" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317417 1074 "The function is strictly decreasing where ${g}^{′}(x)<0.$ Differentiating $g(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =12{x}^{3}-24{x}^{2}+12x \\\\  & =12x({x}^{2}-2x+1) \\\\  & =12x{(x-1)}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n12x{(x-1)}^{2}<0.\n$$\n\nSince $(x-1)^{2}\\ge 0$ for all real $x$, the sign of $12x{(x-1)}^{2}$ is determined by the sign of $12x$, so $12x{(x-1)}^{2}<0$ exactly when $12x<0.$ This yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x & <0 \\\\ x & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is restricted to $x\\in (-8,8),$ we conclude that $g(x)$ is strictly decreasing on the interval $x\\in (-8,0).$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317418 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316710 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317418 1069 "q-343546" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317418 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317418 1071 "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent line to the curve $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}{x}$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317418 1072 #uuid "f1cf5087-7d07-5a4d-8585-5e5e761a770e" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317418 1074 "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ and the denominator $v(x)=x.$\n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\,{v}^{′}(x)=1.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))(x)-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))(1)}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={f}^{′}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-3(\\frac{\\pi }{2})sin\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-cos\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})}{{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3(\\frac{\\pi }{2})(-1)-0}{\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317419 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317065 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317419 1069 "q-343557" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317419 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317419 1071 "Consider the parabola ${y}^{2}-20x=0.$ Given that $x=x(t),$ $y=y(t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{3}{10}$ on the curve at the point $(5,10),$ find the value of  $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317419 1072 #uuid "517deff4-9d3f-5d53-8827-44c3d53aae87" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317419 1074 "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-20x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({y}^{2}-20x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({y}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(20x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(20x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2y)-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 20 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{3}{10},$ $x=5,$ and $y=10,$ and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2y)-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 20 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2\\cdot 10)-(\\frac{3}{10})\\cdot 20 & =0 \\\\ 20\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-6 & =0 \\\\ 20\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317420 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316844 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317420 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316918 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317420 1069 "q-343574" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317420 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317420 1071 "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{2}{5}\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{4}{3}\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}+2$ is defined for $x\\in (0,\\infty ),$ find the values of $x$ for which the function is strictly decreasing.\n\n$x\\in (${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317420 1072 #uuid "758e4090-789f-5002-ba1a-7f6701bdb413" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317420 1074 "We first write $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2}{5}{x}^{5/2}-\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{3/2}+2.\n$$\n\nThe function is strictly decreasing where ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$ Differentiating $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}{x}^{3/2}-\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{1/2} \\\\  & ={x}^{3/2}-2{x}^{1/2} \\\\  & ={x}^{1/2}(x-2) \\\\  & =(x-2)\\sqrt{x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n(x-2)\\sqrt{x}<0.\n$$\n\nThe expression $\\sqrt{x}$ is always positive. So, this reduces to\n\n$$\nx-2<0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & <0 \\\\ x & <2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is restricted to $x\\in (0,\\infty ),$ we conclude that $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing on the interval $x\\in (0,2).$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317421 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316924 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317421 1069 "q-343576" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317421 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317421 1071 "If $y=\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{x+2},$ find ${y}^{′}(1).$\n\n${y}^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317421 1072 #uuid "b2ff9150-55a2-5862-9d62-f9ca9a7361d7" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317421 1074 "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)={e}^{3x}$ and the denominator $v(x)=x+2.$\n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=3{e}^{3x},\\,{v}^{′}(x)=1.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3{e}^{3x})(x+2)-{e}^{3x}(1)}{(x+2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3x+6-1)\\,{e}^{3x}}{(x+2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3x+5)\\,{e}^{3x}}{(x+2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of ${y}^{′}(x)$ at $x=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{(3(1)+5)({e}^{3(1)})}{((1)+2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8{e}^{3}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317422 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316703 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317422 1069 "q-34364" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317422 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317422 1071 "The matrix $E$ and the vector $b$ are given below.\nDetermine if $b\\in \\text{Col}(E).$ \nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}\\cdot {x}_{4}$, where ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$, ${x}_{3}$, and ${x}_{4}$ are real numbers such that $b={x}_{1}{e}_{1}+{x}_{2}{e}_{2}+{x}_{3}{e}_{3}+{x}_{4}{e}_{4},$ and ${e}_{1}$, ${e}_{2}$, ${e}_{3}$, and ${e}_{4}$ are respective columns of $E.$\n\n$$\nE=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -2 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 16 & -4 & 10 & 2 \\\\ 11 & -3 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317422 1072 #uuid "d2325a17-fb51-5bea-98a4-12b407e10918" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317422 1074 "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(E),$ we need to check if the equation $Ex=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[E\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[E\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -2 & 5 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 16 & -4 & 10 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 11 & -3 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}8 & -2 & 5 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -9 \\\\ 11 & -3 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}8{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}+{x}_{4}=4 \\\\ 0=-9 \\\\ 11{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}-{x}_{4}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get a false statement $0=-9.$ Therefore, the system is inconsistent, and we conclude that $b\\notin \\text{Col}(E).$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317423 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316937 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317423 1069 "q-34366" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317423 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317423 1071 "The matrix $F$ and the vector $b$ are given below. Determine if $b\\in \\text{Col}(F).$ In the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are real numbers such that $b={x}_{1}{f}_{1}+{x}_{2}{f}_{2},$ and ${f}_{1},$  ${f}_{2}$ are the respective columns of $F.$\n\n$$\nF=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317423 1072 #uuid "0d0936ed-b5bf-5b20-a019-8454c54094c7" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317423 1074 "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(F),$ we need to check if the equation $Fx=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[F\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[F\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -14\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=5 \\\\ 7{x}_{2}=-14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation we get ${x}_{2}=-2.$ Substituting into the first equation gives  ${x}_{1}=1.$ \n\nSince we have a solution, the equation $Fx=b$ is consistent, which implies that $b\\in \\text{Col}(F).$\n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${f}_{1}$ and ${f}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}=1\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{1}}+(-2)\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{2}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=1\\cdot (-2)=-2.$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317424 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316817 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317424 1069 "q-343662" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317424 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317424 1071 "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+y=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Which value of the parameter $p$ causes the oscillator to resonate?" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317424 1072 #uuid "bc1701ab-6384-5d15-af29-a24b10d78b11" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317424 1074 "The equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=1\\,⟹\\,\\omega =1.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency $p$ of the external periodic force $f(t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡pt$ coincides with the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\omega =1.$\n\nTherefore, the oscillator experiences resonance when $p=1.$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317425 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317050 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317425 1069 "q-343667" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317425 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317425 1071 "A particle of mass $0.6\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force $F(t)=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\,\\text{N}$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317425 1072 #uuid "b200d20b-8850-5ea9-88a8-3f31357f34c9" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317425 1074 "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.6{y}^{″}=-ky-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{5k}{3}y=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{5k}{3}\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{3}{5}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t),$ which is $5,$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{3}{5}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot {5}^{2}=15\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317426 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316857 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317426 1069 "q-343679" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317426 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317426 1071 "A particle of mass $0.8\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force $F(t)=2cos\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t)\\,\\text{N}$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317426 1072 #uuid "9c255544-1d5d-5734-8a75-e3f42b3984e9" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317426 1074 "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.8{y}^{″}=-ky+2cos\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{5k}{4}y=\\frac{5}{2}cos\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{5k}{4}\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{4}{5}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=\\frac{5}{2}cos\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t),$ which is $\\frac{5}{2},$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{4}{5}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot {(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}=5\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317427 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317091 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317427 1069 "q-343687" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317427 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317427 1071 "Find ${y}^{′}$ for $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2{x}^{3}+x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317427 1072 #uuid "1bd08fc3-0dde-53c8-b3d9-2eee4eb29d2d" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317427 1074 "The formula for the derivative of arctangent is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule and the formula above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2{x}^{3}+x)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{(2{x}^{3}+x)}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}+x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+(2{x}^{3}+x)^{2}}\\cdot (6{x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{6{x}^{2}+1}{1+(2{x}^{3}+x)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317428 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316822 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317428 1069 "q-343691" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317428 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317428 1071 "A particle of mass $0.4\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force \n\n$$\nF(t)=(4{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t)-2)\\,\\text{N}\n$$\n\nis applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317428 1072 #uuid "7756f2f0-f8ff-5a38-a573-589b8cc562e9" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317428 1074 "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.4{y}^{″}=-ky+(4{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t)-2).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{5k}{2}y=5(2{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t)-1).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{5k}{2}\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{2}{5}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=5(2{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t)-1)$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$ Since\n\n$$\nf(t)=5(2{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t)-1)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $3.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{2}{5}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {3}^{2}=\\frac{18}{5}\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317429 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316870 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317429 1069 "q-343699" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317429 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317429 1071 "A particle of mass $0.5\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force \n\n$$\nF(t)=2{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{N}\n$$\n\nis applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317429 1072 #uuid "c9d9c170-a8c4-5910-b604-3da53c4eb5a6" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317429 1074 "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.5{y}^{″}=-ky+2{cos}^{2}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2ky=4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=2k\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$ Since\n\n$$\nf(t)=4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)=2(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)),\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $4.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {4}^{2}=8\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317430 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316876 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317430 1069 "q-343735" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317430 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317430 1071 "Find the exact value of the $x$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y=2{x}^{2}+4x-3$ whose tangent has a slope of $1.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317430 1072 #uuid "ba8bd60a-e86a-5439-823a-fbaac32bf4b1" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317430 1074 "Let $f(x)=2{x}^{2}+4x-3.$ The slope of the tangent at the point $x$ is equal to ${f}^{′}(x).$ Computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2}+4x-3) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =2(2x)+4(1) \\\\  & =4x+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set ${f}^{′}(x)=1$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+4 & =1 \\\\ 4x & =-3 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent is equal to $1$ at the point where $x=-\\frac{3}{4}.$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317431 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316612 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317431 1069 "q-343737" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317431 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317431 1071 "Find the **positive** $x$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+14x-6$ whose tangent has a slope of $22.$\n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317431 1072 #uuid "da3c67ed-99d8-57ba-b0f5-8fc958679a71" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317431 1074 "Let $f(x)={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+14x-6.$ The slope of the tangent at the point $x$ is equal to ${f}^{′}(x).$ Computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+14x-6) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+2x+14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set ${f}^{′}(x)=22$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+2x+14 & =22 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+2x-8 & =0 \\\\ (3x-4)(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,\\,\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the positive $x$-coordinate of the points whose tangent has a slope of $6,$ is\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317432 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316623 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317432 1069 "q-343738" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317432 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317432 1071 "$$\n\nConsider the curve $x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=2.$ Given that $x=x(t),\\text{ }y=y(t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=6$ on the curve at the point $(4,\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point. Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317432 1072 #uuid "5563b807-3ce9-515c-a365-9e78582b198e" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317432 1074 "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡y-x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=6,$ $x=4,$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡y-x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0 \\\\ 6\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-4\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0 \\\\ 6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0 \\\\ 3-2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317433 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317135 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317433 1069 "q-343739" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317433 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317433 1071 "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317433 1072 #uuid "1c611f45-045c-5502-9311-b6daf9203ddc" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317433 1074 "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $2.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =2.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =2$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[4(D-Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4(C+Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)]+4t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 4D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=-1$ and $D=\\frac{3}{2}.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n\nOnly solutions I and II match the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317434 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316699 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317434 1069 "q-343745" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317434 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317434 1071 "$$\n\nConsider the curve $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+y=0.$ Given that $x=x(t),\\text{ }y=y(t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=5$ on the curve at the point $(1,0),$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317434 1072 #uuid "13251a0d-5cd2-5f7b-8d19-43b3f2b8afe0" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317434 1074 "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=5,$ $x=1,$ and $y=0,$ and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0 \\\\ 5\\cdot \\frac{1}{1}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0 \\\\ 5+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592