070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317440 1071 "Find the exact value of the slope $m$ of the tangent line to the curve $f(x)=3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({x}^{2}-2x)$ at $x=2.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317440 1072 #uuid "4ef6945e-be3b-5304-a3a9-6ef3ce7dfc07" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317440 1074 "The formula for the derivative of arctangent is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule and the formula above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({x}^{2}-2x)] \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{1+({x}^{2}-2x)^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-2x) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{1+{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}}\\cdot (2x-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{6x-6}{{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent line to the curve $y=f(x)$ at $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={f}^{′}(2) \\\\  & =\\frac{6(2)-6}{(2)^{4}-4(2)^{3}+4(2)^{2}+1} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317441 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317036 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317441 1069 "q-343779" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317441 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317441 1071 "A function $f(x)$ has values according to the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $0.7$ | $0.8$ | $0.9$ | $1.0$ | $1.1$ | $1.2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $13$ | $15$ | $18$ | $20$ | $22$ | $25$ |\n\nApproximate ${f}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=1.2$ using a backward difference approximation. Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\,\\,{f}^{′}(1.2)≈$ {{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317441 1072 #uuid "c2223f19-900a-59af-8219-3437cd18e818" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317441 1074 "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x)-f(x-h)}{h}\n$$\n\n \n\nat the point $x=1.2.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case, $h=0.1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1.2) & ≈\\frac{f(1.2)-f(1.2-0.1)}{0.1} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(1.2)-f(1.1)}{0.1} \\\\  & =\\frac{25-22}{0.1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{0.1} \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317442 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316567 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317442 1069 "q-343782" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317442 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317442 1071 "A function $f(x)$ has values according to the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $-0.5$ | $-0.25$ | $0$ | $0.25$ | $0.5$ | $0.75$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $10$ | $9$ | $8$ | $6$ | $5$ | $3$ |\n\nApproximate ${f}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=0.75$ using a backward difference approximation. Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\,\\,{f}^{′}(0.75)≈$ {{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317442 1072 #uuid "d9c527c9-c026-5af2-8078-3d374b145b43" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317442 1074 "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x)-f(x-h)}{h}\n$$\n\n \n\nat the point $x=0.75.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case, $h=0.25.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0.75) & ≈\\frac{f(0.75)-f(0.75-0.25)}{0.25} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(0.75)-f(0.5)}{0.25} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-5}{0.25} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{0.25} \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317443 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316972 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317443 1069 "q-343788" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317443 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317443 1071 "A distance function $s(t)$ has values according to the table below:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $0.02$ | $0.04$ | $0.06$ | $0.08$ | $0.10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $s(t)$ | $0$ | $3$ | $5$ | $9$ | $13$ | $18$ |\n\nApproximate the velocity ${s}^{′}(t)$ at the point $t=0.06$ using a backward difference approximation. Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\,\\,{s}^{′}(0.06)≈$ {{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317443 1072 #uuid "c44d4d0b-eb44-5f39-a106-5b66e352fb91" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317443 1074 "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{s}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{s(t)-s(t-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the point $t=0.06.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case, $h=0.02.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}^{′}(0.06) & ≈\\frac{s(0.06)-s(0.06-0.02)}{0.02} \\\\  & =\\frac{s(0.06)-s(0.04)}{0.02} \\\\  & =\\frac{9-5}{0.02} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{0.02} \\\\  & =200.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317444 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316741 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317444 1069 "q-34381" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317444 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317444 1071 "What is the curvature of the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2},{t}^{3}⟩$, for $t>0?$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317444 1072 #uuid "a8320061-9385-5e97-8a78-62a775ef29e2" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317444 1074 "The curvature $κ$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})⟩=⟨2t,3{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+(3{t}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+9{t}^{4}} \\\\  & =t\\sqrt{4+9{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $t>0$, we have $|t|=t.$\n\nNow, we find the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})⟩=⟨2,6t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(t)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨2t,\\,3{t}^{2},\\,0⟩\\times ⟨2,\\,6t,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2t & 3{t}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6t & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}2t & 3{t}^{2} \\\\ 2 & 6t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnitude of the cross product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖=6{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{t}^{2}}{(t\\sqrt{4+9{t}^{2}})^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{t}^{2}}{{t}^{3}(4+9{t}^{2})^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{t(4+9{t}^{2})^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317445 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316617 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317445 1069 "q-343816" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317445 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317445 1071 "![](images/2c/2c633342d5dc1ddb1244d492d81b6b9c00ffe33858398a72acb2a73467d93bb0.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $g(x)$ is shown above. \nFind the approximate ${g}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=4$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=2.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n*Hint: Assume that the graph passes through the points with integer $y$-coordinates when $x=2$ and $x=4.$*\n\n${g}^{′}(4)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317445 1072 #uuid "46b6e919-5039-5c49-a1c9-e4b9c3ec336c" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317445 1074 "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{g(x)-g(x-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=4.$ Applying the above formula with $x=4$ and $h=2$ gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(4) & ≈\\frac{g(4)-g(4-2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{g(4)-g(2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-1}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317446 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316846 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317446 1069 "q-343833" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317446 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317446 1071 "The number of deer in a wildlife reserve, measured in hundreds, is given by the function $D(t)$, where $t$ is the time, in years, since the study began. The values of $D(t)$ for several values of $t$ are shown in the table below:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $D(t)$ | $5$ | $7$ | $10$ | $14$ | $19$ |\n\nApproximate the rate of change of the deer population when $t=4$ using a backward difference approximation. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe estimated rate of change when $t=4$ is\n{{field-1}} hundreds per year." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317446 1072 #uuid "18c42922-ce39-5e6f-9e60-1d0180103d45" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317446 1074 "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{D}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{D(t)-D(t-h)}{h},\n$$\n\nat the time $t=4.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{′}(4) & ≈\\frac{D(4)-D(4-1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{D(4)-D(3)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{19-14}{1} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the estimated rate of change when $t=4$ is $5$ hundreds per year." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317447 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317158 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317447 1069 "q-34384" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317447 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317447 1071 "The matrix $A$ and vectors ${b}_{1}$, ${b}_{2}$, ${b}_{3}$ are given below. Which of the vectors lie in $\\text{Null}(A)?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 6 & -10 \\\\ 9 & 15\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317447 1072 #uuid "0d4cb2fe-d794-5b24-afd6-08f39ce18357" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317447 1074 "A vector $b$ lies in $\\text{Null}(A)$ if $Ab=0.$ So, we need to compute the product of $A$ and each of the given vectors and check whether it is equal to $0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{b}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 6 & -10 \\\\ 9 & 15\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 90\\end{aligned}]\\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ A{b}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 6 & -10 \\\\ 9 & 15\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-59 \\\\ 118 \\\\ -33\\end{aligned}]\\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ A{b}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 6 & -10 \\\\ 9 & 15\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 180\\end{aligned}]\\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, none of the vector lies in $\\text{Null}(A).$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317448 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316627 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317448 1069 "q-343841" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317448 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317448 1071 "The battery charge of a delivery drone, measured in percent, is given by the function $B(t)$, where $t$ is the time, in hours, since the drone was fully charged. The values of $B(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $B(t)$ | $80$ | $72$ | $55$ | $40$ | $35$ |\n\nApproximate the rate of change of the battery charge when $t=6$ using a backward difference approximation. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe estimated rate of change when $t=6$ is\n{{field-1}} percent per hour." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317448 1072 #uuid "8f5d6352-2ca2-5433-901d-91ec01309941" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317448 1074 "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{B}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{B(t)-B(t-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the time $t=6.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{′}(6) & ≈\\frac{B(6)-B(6-2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{B(6)-B(4)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{40-55}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-15}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the estimated rate of change when $t=6$ is $-\\frac{15}{2}$ percent per hour." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317449 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316606 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317449 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316756 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317449 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317019 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317449 1069 "q-343854" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317449 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317449 1071 "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{′}-2y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}∼{{field-1}} & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317449 1072 #uuid "ae77a2f6-2db6-5433-96eb-14517b11c7c7" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317449 1074 "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))+{b}_{n}\\cdot \\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,-2(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,\\,=-2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -2\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{a}_{n})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{b}_{n})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,=-2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and the coefficients next to sines and cosines, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=-2 \\\\ \\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{a}_{n}=\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}\\pi } \\\\ -\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{b}_{n}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, ${a}_{0}=2,$ which means the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNext, from the third equation, ${b}_{n}=-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{6}.$ Substituting this into the third equation, gives\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{6})\\frac{n\\pi }{3}-2{a}_{n}=\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\,⇒\\,{a}_{n}=-\\frac{72}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${a}_{n}$ with $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n\\pi }{6}\\cdot (-\\frac{72}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}∼1 & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,-\\frac{72}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{12}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317450 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316614 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317450 1069 "q-343858" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317450 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317450 1071 "![](images/3a/3a4526a6979a86aa924bddca54b05778e91bb67a1dcb6159c5436cc975b22668.png)\n\nA cyclist records the total distance traveled after $x$ minutes. The total distance traveled after $x$ minutes, in miles, is given by the function $D(x).$ The graph of $D(x)$ is shown above.  \n\nApproximate the rate of change when $x=45$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=15.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n*Hint: Assume that the graph passes through the points with integer $y$-coordinates when $x=30$ and $x=45.$*\n\nThe estimated rate of change when $x=45$ is\n{{field-1}} miles per minute." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317450 1072 #uuid "17f9807b-5199-5b54-950d-4180d3ccbfc2" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317450 1074 "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{D}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{D(x)-D(x-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=45.$  Applying the above formula with $x=45$ and $h=15$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{′}(45) & ≈\\frac{D(45)-D(45-15)}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{D(45)-D(30)}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{11-6}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the estimated rate of change when $x=45$ is $\\frac{1}{3}$ miles per minute." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317451 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316774 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317451 1069 "q-343862" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317451 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317451 1071 "![](images/9a/9a5fa5cdd4734813d18af5b99e3869051f15e137f72e98ad5529c751e6f85d87.png)\n\nA model rocket’s height is recorded during a launch. The height after $x$ seconds, in meters, is given by the function $H(x).$ The graph of $H(x)$ is shown above.  \n\nApproximate the rate of change when $x=16$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=12.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n*Hint: Assume that the graph passes through the points with integer $y$-coordinates when $x=4$ and $x=16.$*\n\nThe estimated rate of change when $x=16$ is\n{{field-1}} meters per second." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317451 1072 #uuid "c788ba8d-c7ad-5608-b592-f77cc5ec1d7b" 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317451 1074 "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{H}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{H(x)-H(x-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=16.$  Applying the above formula with $x=16$ and $h=12$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}^{′}(16) & ≈\\frac{H(16)-H(16-12)}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{H(16)-H(4)}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{7-5}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the estimated rate of change when $x=16$ is $\\frac{1}{6}$ meters per second." 13194139533418 17592186045585 true] [17592186317452 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317